var e=JSON.parse(`[[1401,"Most often the sum of an array's elements is obtained by means of the \`reduce()\` method. It handles the elements in sequence and accumulates the result. You can also use a loop or \`forEach()\`, but \`reduce()\` is considered the standard solution. The method is convenient and compact.","For the computation of a sum the \`reduce()\` method is used, which is intended for the accumulation of a result.\\nThe definition:\\n\`reduce()\` is an array method that successively applies a function to the elements of an array, passing the intermediate result on.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- It takes the initial value of the accumulator.\\n\\n- It successively adds the elements.\\n\\n- It returns the final value.\\n\\nThe alternative variant\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- The counting of totals.\\n\\n- The aggregation of data.\\n\\n- The forming of objects and dictionaries.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\`reduce()\` is the standard and most universal way of aggregating data in an array.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\n\\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, value) => {\\n  return acc + value;\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(sum); // 10\\n\\nlet sum = 0;\\nnumbers.forEach(n => sum += n);"],[1402,"For the sorting the \`sort()\` method with a comparison function is used. In the function you have to return the difference of the objects' numeric fields. If the result is negative, the element stays earlier; if it is positive, they change places. This way is used most often.","The \`sort()\` method sorts an array in place and takes a comparison function.\\nThe definition:\\nA compare function is a function that determines the order of the elements in the sorting.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- Two elements of the array are taken.\\n\\n- The difference of the values is computed.\\n\\n- Depending on the result the order is determined.\\n\\nSorting in descending order\\n\\nAn example in React\\nThe sorting is often carried out before the rendering of a list.\\n\\nWhy a copy of the array is used\\n\\n- \`sort()\` changes the original array.\\n\\n- In React it matters to preserve the immutability of the data.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo sort by a numeric field \`sort()\` and a comparison function are used, usually with the preliminary copying of the array.","const users = [\\n  { name: \\"Ivan\\", age: 30 },\\n  { name: \\"Anna\\", age: 20 },\\n  { name: \\"Petr\\", age: 25 }\\n];\\n\\nusers.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);\\n\\nusers.sort((a, b) => b.age - a.age);\\n\\nconst sortedUsers = [...users].sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);\\n\\n// then the render of the list"],[1403,"For the sorting of strings the \`sort()\` method and the \`localeCompare()\` function are usually used. The latter correctly compares strings with the case and the language taken into account. Such a way is considered more reliable than a simple comparison with the \`>\` operator. It is used in the sorting of lists of names, titles and other textual data.","In the sorting of strings it matters to take the particulars of the comparison of textual values into account.\\nThe definition:\\n\`localeCompare()\` is a method of strings that compares two strings with the locale and the rules of the language taken into account.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- Two elements of the array are taken.\\n\\n- \`localeCompare()\` is called.\\n\\n- A number determining the order is returned.\\n\\nWhen it matters to use localeCompare\\n\\n- In work with Cyrillic.\\n\\n- In the sorting of users' names.\\n\\n- In the display of lists in a UI.\\n\\nSorting in descending order\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor string fields it is preferable to use \`localeCompare()\`, since it takes the rules of the language into account and gives the correct order.","const users = [\\n  { name: \\"Ivan\\" },\\n  { name: \\"Anna\\" },\\n  { name: \\"Petr\\" }\\n];\\n\\nusers.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));\\n\\nusers.sort((a, b) => b.name.localeCompare(a.name));"],[1404,"To replace every space, the \`replace()\` method with a regular expression and the \`g\` flag is used. The flag means a global replacement. For example, you can replace the spaces with hyphens. This is often used in the forming of a URL or a slug.","The \`replace()\` method replaces parts of a string, and regular expressions let you replace every match.\\nThe definition:\\nA regular expression is a pattern for the search for and the replacement of text.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- The pattern \`/ /\` searches for a space.\\n\\n- The \`g\` flag means replace every match.\\n\\n- A new string is returned.\\n\\nA more universal variant\\n\\nThis variant replaces any whitespace characters.\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The generation of a URL.\\n\\n- The forming of a slug.\\n\\n- The normalisation of strings.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo replace every space, \`replace()\` and a regular expression with the \`g\` flag are used.","const text = \\"hello world example\\";\\n\\nconst result = text.replace(/ /g, \\"-\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(result); // hello-world-example\\n\\ntext.replace(/\\\\s/g, \\"-\\");"],[1405,"Axios is a library for carrying out HTTP requests from a browser or from Node.js. It lets you send GET, POST and other requests and automatically handles JSON. The requests return a Promise, so \`async/await\` or \`.then()\` is used. Axios is often applied for work with APIs.","Axios simplifies the interaction of a client with a server.\\nThe definition:\\nAxios is an HTTP client that carries out requests and returns the result in the form of a Promise.\\nAn example of a GET request\\n\\nAn example of a POST request\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- The automatic conversion of JSON.\\n\\n- The convenient handling of errors.\\n\\n- Support for interceptors.\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- The retrieval of data from an API.\\n\\n- The sending of forms.\\n\\n- Authorisation.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAxios is a convenient HTTP client, which simplifies the carrying out of requests and the handling of responses.","import axios from \\"axios\\";\\n\\nconst response = await axios.get(\\"/api/users\\");\\nconsole.log(response.data);\\n\\nawait axios.post(\\"/api/login\\", {\\n  username: \\"test\\",\\n  password: \\"123\\"\\n});"],[1406,"An Axios instance is a separate instance of Axios with settings given in advance. In it you can specify the base URL, the headers and the timeouts. This lets you avoid repeating the settings in every request. It is usually used in large projects.","In real projects many requests have identical parameters.\\nThe definition:\\nAn Axios instance is a client object created through \`axios.create()\` with a configuration of its own.\\nAn example of the creation\\n\\nThe use\\n\\nWhy this is convenient\\n\\n- The shared settings are given once.\\n\\n- The maintenance of the code is simplified.\\n\\n- You can add interceptors.\\n\\nAn example of an interceptor\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn Axios instance lets you manage the settings of HTTP requests centrally and reduces the duplication of code.","import axios from \\"axios\\";\\n\\nconst api = axios.create({\\n  baseURL: \\"/api\\",\\n  timeout: 5000\\n});\\n\\nconst response = await api.get(\\"/users\\");\\n\\napi.interceptors.request.use(config => {\\n  // the addition of the token\\n  return config;\\n});"],[1407,"An authorisation token is usually passed in the \`Authorization\` header. In Axios it can be specified in the request's parameters or set globally in the client's instance. The \`Bearer token\` scheme is most often used. Such an approach is applied in practically every REST API.","On a request to a protected API the server expects a token in the request's headers.\\nThe definition:\\nThe Authorization header is an HTTP header that is used for the passing of credentials or of an access token.\\nAn example of the addition of a token to one request\\n\\nThe addition of a token to an Axios instance\\n\\nThe addition through an interceptor\\nThis way is most often used in real projects.\\n\\nWhy an interceptor is used\\n\\n- The token is automatically added to every request.\\n\\n- There is no need to repeat the code.\\n\\n- It is convenient to refresh the token.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe optimal way of passing a token is through the \`Authorization\` header, most often with the use of an interceptor or an Axios instance.","axios.get(\\"/api/profile\\", {\\n  headers: {\\n    Authorization: \\"Bearer TOKEN_VALUE\\"\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nconst api = axios.create({\\n  baseURL: \\"/api\\"\\n});\\n\\napi.defaults.headers.common[\\"Authorization\\"] = \\"Bearer TOKEN_VALUE\\";\\n\\napi.interceptors.request.use(config => {\\n  const token = localStorage.getItem(\\"token\\");\\n  if (token) {\\n    config.headers.Authorization = \`Bearer \${token}\`;\\n  }\\n  return config;\\n});"],[1408,"Custom validation is usually implemented through functions that check the values of the fields and return the errors. The state of the values and of the errors is held in a \`useState\` or in a reducer. The checks are started on a change of a field, the loss of focus or the sending of the form. The errors are displayed next to the corresponding fields. Such an approach gives complete control, but it requires more code and discipline.","Custom validation is useful when the requirements are simple or non-standard and the attachment of a library is excessive.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: the custom validation of a form is the manual checking of the user's data against given rules before the form is sent or handled.\\nThe basic architecture\\nBefore the implementation it matters to separate the form's data and the logic of the checking.\\n\\n- The state of the values\\n\\n- It holds the current data of the fields\\n\\n- The state of the errors\\n\\n- It holds the messages of the errors for every field\\n\\n- The validator functions\\n\\n- They check one field or the whole form\\n\\nAn example of simple validation\\n\\nThe use in a component\\n\\nWhen to start the validation\\n\\n- On \`onBlur\` — a soft UX\\n\\n- On \`onChange\` — instantaneous feedback\\n\\n- On \`onSubmit\` — the final check\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Validation in the render without memoization\\n\\n- The mixing of the UI and the logic of the checks\\n\\n- The absence of a single format of errors\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustom validation is justified for simple forms or non-standard rules, but for complex scenarios it is better to use specialised libraries.","const validateEmail = (value: string) => {\\n  if (!value) return \\"The email is obligatory\\";\\n  if (!value.includes(\\"@\\")) return \\"An incorrect email\\";\\n  return null;\\n};\\n\\nconst error = validateEmail(email);\\n// if error !== null we show the message"],[1409,"A middleware chain is built on the sequential call of functions, in which every one receives control and can pass it on. The \`next\` pattern is usually used, which explicitly calls the following middleware. Such an approach lets you add logic before and after the main handling. Middleware are often applied in routing, logging and authentication. The mechanism is founded on closures and the composition of functions.","Middleware chains let you extend a system's behaviour flexibly without a rigid coupling of the components.\\nThe definition\\nThe definition: middleware is an intermediary function that handles a request and decides whether to pass control to the next handler.\\nThe basic principle of the working\\nBefore looking at the implementation it matters to understand the flow of the execution.\\n\\n- The first middleware receives the request\\n\\n- It carries out its logic\\n\\n- It calls \`next()\`\\n\\n- Control passes on down the chain\\n\\nA simplified example\\n\\nWhat this works by means of\\n\\n- Higher-order functions\\n\\n- Closures\\n\\n- The explicit passing of control through \`next\`\\n\\nWhere it is met with\\n\\n- Server-side frameworks\\n\\n- Client-side routers\\n\\n- Next.js middleware\\n\\n- Redux middleware\\n\\nConclusion\\nMiddleware chains are implemented through the composition of functions and the explicit passing of control, which lets you embed additional logic flexibly.","const mw1 = (ctx, next) => {\\n  console.log(\\"before\\");\\n  next();\\n  console.log(\\"after\\");\\n};"],[1410,"The functions setTimeout and setInterval are not part of the standard of the JavaScript language (ECMAScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web API or other APIs of the environment. In the ECMAScript specification there is no description of these functions. They are used for the postponed or periodic execution of code, but their implementation and their availability depend on the specific platform.","The belonging to standards\\nThe functions \`setTimeout\` and \`setInterval\` are not in the ECMAScript specification (the main standard of JavaScript). They are defined in the APIs of the environment:\\n\\n- In browsers: Part of the Web API, standardised by the WHATWG and the W3C (in the HTML Living Standard specification, for example).\\n- In Node.js: Part of the global object provided by the runtime environment.\\nWhy this matters\\nSince these functions are not part of the language's core, their behaviour and their availability may differ in different environments. For example, the minimal delay, the precision of the timer or additional parameters may depend on the implementation.\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nConclusion\\n\`setTimeout\` and \`setInterval\` are widely distributed functions, but they are not standardised in ECMAScript. They belong to the APIs of the environment, which means that they depend on the platform (the browser, the server and so on). Developers should take that into account when writing code for different runtime environments.","// An example in a browser or in Node.js\\nconsole.log('Start');\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('Executed after 1 second');\\n}, 1000);\\n\\nlet counter = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  counter++;\\n  console.log(\`Interval tick \${counter}\`);\\n  if (counter >= 3) {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    console.log('Interval stopped');\\n  }\\n}, 500);"],[1411,"setTimeout and setInterval are not part of the standard of the JavaScript language (ECMAScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web API. In browsers they are defined in the WHATWG HTML Living Standard specification, and in Node.js in the timers module. This means that their behaviour may differ a little in different environments, although the main functionality is identical.","setTimeout and setInterval are functions for work with timers, which are often associated with JavaScript, but technically they are not in the ECMAScript specification, which is the standard of the language. Instead they are part of the API provided by the runtime environment.\\n\\nIn browsers these functions are defined in the WHATWG HTML Living Standard specification as part of the Web API. They are available in the global scope (usually on the window object), but they are not methods of the language itself. In Node.js they are implemented in the \`timers\` module and are also available globally, for compatibility.\\n\\nThe practical application of these functions includes the postponed execution of code (setTimeout) and the periodic execution (setInterval). For the creation of a simple timer or the postponed loading of data, for example.\\n\\nAn example of the use of setTimeout:\\n\\n// The postponed printing of a message after 2 seconds\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\nconsole.log('A message after the delay');\\n}, 2000);\\n\\n// You can pass arguments into the function\\nfunction greet(name) {\\nconsole.log(\`Hello, \${name}!\`);\\n}\\nsetTimeout(greet, 1000, 'Anna');\\n\\nAn example of the use of setInterval:\\n\\n// The creation of a simple stopwatch\\nlet seconds = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\nseconds++;\\nconsole.log(\`\${seconds} seconds have elapsed\`);\\nif (seconds >= 5) {\\nclearInterval(intervalId); // The stopping of the interval\\nconsole.log('The stopwatch has been stopped');\\n}\\n}, 1000);\\n\\nIt matters to remember that timers in JavaScript do not guarantee a precise time of execution. They add the callback to the queue of events after the delay specified, but the actual execution may be postponed if the main thread is busy with other tasks.\\n\\nSummary: setTimeout and setInterval are important tools for the management of the time of the execution of code, but they are part of the environment's API rather than of the ECMAScript standard, which has to be taken into account when moving code between different runtime environments.",null],[1412,"setTimeout and setInterval are not part of the standard of the JavaScript language (ECMAScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web APIs. In browsers they are defined in the HTML Living Standard specification, and in Node.js in the timers module. These functions let you postpone the execution of code, using the mechanism of the event loop.","setTimeout and setInterval are functions that let you carry code out asynchronously after particular spans of time. They are not in the ECMAScript specification, which defines the core of the JavaScript language, but are provided by the environment in which the code is carried out.\\nThe source and the standardisation\\nIn browsers these functions are part of the Web APIs, defined in the HTML Living Standard specification from the WHATWG. In Node.js they are implemented in the timers module, which imitates the browser's behaviour. This means that their behaviour may differ a little in different environments, but the main logic is identical.\\nHow this works\\nOn the call of setTimeout or setInterval, the timer is registered in the environment, and the callback is placed in the queue of events (the event loop) after the delay specified. This lets you not block the main thread of execution.\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nIn this example setTimeout runs the function once, and setInterval repeatedly, until clearInterval is called.\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- Animations and UI: For the updating of the interface with delays.\\n- The polling of a server: Regular requests for data with the use of setInterval.\\n- Postponed actions: The hiding of a notification after a setTimeout, for example.\\nIt matters to remember that the precision of timers is not guaranteed, especially under a high load on the main thread.\\nConclusion: Use setTimeout and setInterval for the management of intervals of time in client-side and server-side applications in JavaScript, but take into account that they depend on the environment and may have limitations of precision.","// An example of setTimeout: carry the code out once after 2 seconds\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log('Carried out after 2 seconds');\\n}, 2000);\\n\\n// An example of setInterval: carry the code out every second\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log('It repeats every second');\\n}, 1000);\\n\\n// Stop the interval after 5 seconds\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    console.log('The interval has been stopped');\\n}, 5000);"],[1413,"setTimeout and setInterval are not part of the ECMAScript standard (the core of JavaScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web API and the Timer API respectively. In browsers they are defined in the HTML Living Standard specification, and in Node.js in the timers module. These functions let you postpone the execution of code, using the mechanism of the event loop. An understanding of this difference helps you avoid errors when working in different environments.","JavaScript as a language is defined by the ECMAScript standard, which includes the syntax, the built-in objects (Array and Date, for example) and the main functions. Functions such as setTimeout and setInterval, however, are not in that specification — they are provided by the runtime environment in which JavaScript works.\\nWhere are setTimeout and setInterval defined?\\nIn browsers these functions are part of the Web API, described in the HTML Living Standard specification. They let you schedule the execution of code after a delay or at regular intervals, using the event loop for the asynchronous handling. In Node.js analogous functions are provided through the built-in timers module, which also uses the event loop but is implemented at the level of libuv.\\nA practical example of the use\\nHere is a simple example demonstrating the working of setTimeout and setInterval in a browser:\\n\\nThis code prints a message after 2 seconds and then increments a counter every second, stopping after 5 iterations.\\nThe importance of understanding the source of the API\\nA knowledge that setTimeout and setInterval are not a standard of JavaScript helps developers:\\n\\n- Avoid assumptions about the availability of these functions in every environment (in pure JavaScript engines without a Web API, for example).\\n- Choose the alternatives correctly, such as requestAnimationFrame for animations in browsers or worker threads for heavy tasks in Node.js.\\n- Understand JavaScript's asynchronous model of working through the event loop more deeply.\\nConclusion: setTimeout and setInterval are an API of the runtime environment rather than part of the core of JavaScript, which it matters to take into account in development for various platforms, in order to ensure the compatibility and the correct behaviour of the code.","// The use of setTimeout for postponed execution\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('This message will appear after 2 seconds');\\n}, 2000);\\n\\n// The use of setInterval for repeating actions\\nlet counter = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  counter++;\\n  console.log(\`The counter: \${counter}\`);\\n  if (counter >= 5) {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId); // Stop the interval\\n    console.log('The interval has been stopped');\\n  }\\n}, 1000);"],[1414,"setTimeout and setInterval are not part of the ECMAScript standard (the core of JavaScript). They are provided by the Web API in browsers and by the API of Node.js in the server-side environment. This means that their implementation depends on the environment, but they work in a similar way everywhere. In browsers, for example, they are governed by the event loop, and in Node.js by the timers module. Their behaviour is standardised de facto thanks to their wide support.","setTimeout and setInterval are functions for the postponed or periodic execution of code, which are often used in JavaScript for the management of intervals of time. It matters to understand, however, that they are not in the ECMAScript specification, which defines the core of the language (the syntax, the data types, the built-in objects, for example). Instead they are part of the APIs provided by the runtime environment.\\nWhere are these functions implemented?\\n\\n- In browsers: setTimeout and setInterval are methods of the Window object and part of the Web API, standardised by the W3C and the WHATWG. They are integrated with the event loop for the asynchronous execution.\\n- In Node.js: These functions are available through the global object and the timers module, which lets them be used on the server.\\nA practical example of the use\\nHere is a simple example that shows how setTimeout and setInterval work in a browser:\\n\\nThis code demonstrates the basic application: setTimeout carries a function out once after a delay, and setInterval repeatedly after the spans of time given, until it is stopped by means of clearInterval.\\nThe particulars of the implementation\\n\\n- The timers do not guarantee a precise time of execution because of JavaScript's single-threaded nature and the load on the event loop.\\n- In Node.js the timers are part of libuv and work asynchronously, analogously to the browser's.\\n- The minimal delay may vary (4 ms in older browsers, for example), but modern environments support more precise intervals.\\nConclusion: setTimeout and setInterval should be applied for tasks requiring postponed or periodic execution, such as animations, the polling of a server or the postponed handling of data, but it matters to take their asynchronous nature and the possible delays into account.","// The use of setTimeout for postponed execution\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log('This message will appear after 2 seconds');\\n}, 2000);\\n\\n// The use of setInterval for periodic execution\\nlet counter = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  counter++;\\n  console.log(\`The counter: \${counter}\`);\\n  if (counter >= 5) {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId); // Stop the interval\\n    console.log('The interval has been stopped');\\n  }\\n}, 1000);"],[1415,"JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language that makes web pages interactive. It is carried out in the user's browser, letting you change content dynamically, react to the user's actions and communicate with the server without a reload of the page. Originally created for the client side, it is now also widely used on the server (Node.js). Without JavaScript modern web applications would be static and unresponsive.","JavaScript is a multi-paradigm programming language, which is one of the three key technologies of the World Wide Web alongside HTML and CSS. Unlike HTML (the markup) and CSS (the styles), JavaScript adds behaviour and logic, turning static documents into interactive applications.\\n\\nThe main particulars and the application\\n\\nJavaScript was originally created for execution in the browser (the client side). Its main task is to manipulate the DOM (the Document Object Model) in order to react to events (clicks, input from the keyboard) and update the interface without a reload of the page. This is the foundation for the creation of sliders, forms with validation, animations and complex single-page applications (SPAs).\\n\\nWith the appearance of the Node.js runtime environment the area of application of JavaScript extended to server-side development, the creation of desktop applications (Electron) and even mobile development (React Native).\\n\\nA practical example: an interactive button\\n\\nLet us look at a simple example in which JavaScript changes the text on a page on a click:\\n\\nIn this example: the HTML defines the structure, and the JavaScript function \`changeText\` fires on the \`onclick\` event and changes the contents of the paragraph. This demonstrates the basic interaction with the DOM.\\n\\nThe key concepts of the language\\n\\n- Dynamic typing: The types of variables are determined during the execution.\\n\\n- Prototypal inheritance: Objects can inherit properties directly from other objects.\\n\\n- Asynchronous programming: Support for callbacks, promises and async/await for operations that require time (network requests, for example).\\n\\n- The ECMAScript standard: The language develops through annual updates of the standard (ES6+, ES2022), adding new syntactic capabilities and APIs.\\n\\nConclusion: JavaScript is a universal language, obligatory for front-end development and a powerful tool for the back end (Node.js). It is worth applying wherever you have to create an interactive, dynamic user interface in a browser or build a high-performance network application on a server.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<body>\\n    <p id=\\"demo\\">The original text.</p>\\n    <button onclick=\\"changeText()\\">Press me</button>\\n\\n    <script>\\n        function changeText() {\\n            // We find the element by its id and change its contents\\n            document.getElementById(\\"demo\\").innerHTML = \\"The text has been changed by means of JavaScript!\\";\\n        }\\n    <\/script>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[1416,"A Promise is an object in JavaScript representing the result of an asynchronous operation, which may be carried out successfully (resolved) or with an error (rejected). Without a Promise you can use the old approach with callbacks, but that often leads to \\"callback hell\\" — code that is difficult to read and maintain. A Promise lets you structure asynchronous code in chains of .then() and .catch(), making it more linear and more manageable. The modern async/await syntax is built on the foundation of Promise, which makes the code even more readable.","A Promise is a fundamental concept in JavaScript for work with asynchronous operations, such as network requests, the reading of files or timers. It is a wrapper object for a value that may be available now, in the future or never. A Promise has three states: pending, fulfilled (carried out successfully) and rejected (carried out with an error). This lets asynchronous code be handled more structurally than by means of traditional callbacks.\\n\\nHow does a Promise work?\\n\\nOn its creation a Promise takes an executor function, which receives two parameters: resolve and reject. Those functions are called to finish the Promise with a result or an error. After the creation of the Promise you can use the .then() method to handle the successful result, .catch() to handle the errors and .finally() to carry code out independently of the outcome.\\n\\nCan you get by without a Promise?\\n\\nYes; before the appearance of Promise in ES6 (2015) the main way of working with asynchronicity was callbacks. The setTimeout function or the old APIs of Node.js, for example, used callbacks. That approach has drawbacks, however:\\n\\n- Callback Hell: Nested callbacks create a pyramid of code that is difficult to read and maintain.\\n\\n- The difficulty of the handling of errors: You have to check the errors in every callback, which leads to the duplication of code.\\n\\n- Problems with parallel execution: The coordination of several asynchronous operations requires additional libraries or complex constructs.\\n\\nAn example with callbacks:\\n\\nThe modern alternatives and improvements\\n\\nThe async/await syntax, introduced in ES8 (2017), is built on the foundation of Promise and lets you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style, which improves the readability even further. Under the hood async/await uses Promise, so without Promise it does not work.\\n\\nPromise is applied wherever asynchronous handling is required: in web requests (the fetch API), operations with databases, the reading of files in Node.js and so on. It has become the standard for modern JavaScript libraries and frameworks.\\n\\nConclusion: Promise is a key tool for the management of asynchronous code in JavaScript, which eliminates the drawbacks of callbacks and provides the foundation for async/await. Without Promise you can use callbacks, but that complicates the code and lowers its maintainability, so in modern projects Promise is the preferable choice.","const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    const success = true;\\n    if (success) {\\n      resolve('The data has been loaded');\\n    } else {\\n      reject('An error in the loading');\\n    }\\n  }, 1000);\\n});\\n\\npromise\\n  .then(result => console.log(result))\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error))\\n  .finally(() => console.log('The operation has finished'));\\n\\nfunction fetchData(callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    callback(null, 'The data');\\n  }, 1000);\\n}\\n\\nfetchData((err, data) => {\\n  if (err) {\\n    console.error(err);\\n  } else {\\n    console.log(data);\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nasync function loadData() {\\n  try {\\n    const result = await promise;\\n    console.log(result);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nloadData();"],[1417,"Listing is the process of printing out contents — the list of the files in a directory or the elements of an array in a program, for example. It is often used for debugging, in order to see the current state of the data. On the command line 'ls' or 'dir' is the listing of files. In code a listing may be a simple loop printing the elements of a collection.","In programming the term listing usually means the action of displaying the contents of something in a readable format. That may be the printing of the list of the files in a file system, of the elements in a data structure (an array or a list, for example) or even of the lines of source code. The main goal of a listing is to provide the developer or the user with clear information for analysis, debugging or simple familiarisation.\\n\\nWhere is listing applied?\\n\\n- The command line / the terminal: Commands such as \`ls\` (Linux/macOS) or \`dir\` (Windows) carry out the listing of the files and folders in the current directory.\\n\\n- The debugging of code: The printing of the contents of variables, arrays or objects to the console in order to check their state during the program's execution.\\n\\n- Logging: The writing of a list of events or of data into a log file for subsequent analysis.\\n\\n- User interfaces: The display of a list of elements (of the goods in a catalogue, for example) in a web application or a mobile application.\\n\\nExamples of code\\n\\nThe simplest listing of an array's elements in JavaScript:\\n\\nThe listing of the files in the current directory by means of Node.js:\\n\\nConclusion: Listing is a basic but important tool for the inspection of data and of contents. It is worth applying in debugging, in the creation of command-line tools or in any scenarios in which a clear representation of a list of information is required.","const fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'];\\n// The listing (the printing) of every element\\nfruits.forEach(fruit => {\\n  console.log(fruit);\\n});\\n// The output to the console:\\n// apple\\n// banana\\n// orange\\n\\nconst fs = require('fs');\\n// The reading and the printing of the list of files\\nfs.readdir('.', (err, files) => {\\n  if (err) throw err;\\n  files.forEach(file => {\\n    console.log(file); // The listing of every file name\\n  });\\n});"],[1418,"The search for a bottleneck begins with the measurement of the key metrics, such as the page's loading time, the server's response time and the consumption of resources. The browser's tools (DevTools) help you analyse the rendering and the network requests. On the server side profilers and monitoring are used for the detection of slow queries or inefficient code. A systematic approach lets you localise the problem and apply targeted optimisations.","The search for bottlenecks in the performance of a web application is a systematic process of the measurement, the analysis and the localisation of the components that limit the overall speed of the working. The problem may be hiding at any level: in the client-side code (JavaScript, the rendering), the network requests, the server-side logic or the database.\\n\\nThe main stages of the search\\n\\n- Measurement and monitoring: The gathering of the metrics of performance (Core Web Vitals, the response time of the API, the consumption of CPU and memory).\\n\\n- The client-side analysis: The use of the browser's DevTools (the Performance and Network tabs) for the assessment of the loading time of the resources, the execution of the scripts and the painting of the page.\\n\\n- The server-side analysis: The profiling of the server-side code for the detection of slow functions, the analysis of the logs of slow queries, the monitoring of the database.\\n\\n- The network analysis: The checking of the delays, of the size of the data transferred and of the use of caching.\\n\\nThe practical tools and examples\\n\\nFor the client side, open Chrome DevTools and record the load:\\n\\nOn the server (Node.js) you can use the built-in \`perf_hooks\` module or profilers of the \`clinic.js\` kind. For the analysis of queries to a database, turn on the logging of slow queries.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nThis approach is used on a fall in the interface's speed, on an increase in the API's response time or in the preparation for high loads. It is relevant for full-stack developers, for DevOps and for performance engineers.\\n\\nConclusion: The search for a bottleneck is an iterative process, beginning with measurement. Apply it on a slowdown of an application's working, in order to determine the problem zone (the client, the network, the server, the database) precisely and optimise it rather than guessing.","// An example: the measurement of the time of the execution of a function\\nconsole.time('heavyCalculation');\\n// A slow operation\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1e7; i++) { Math.sqrt(i); }\\nconsole.timeEnd('heavyCalculation');\\n// The time of the execution will appear in the console"],[1419,"The choice depends on the type of the data and on your application's architecture. Apollo Client is a specialised solution for work with a GraphQL API, which provides caching and the management of the state of requests and subscriptions. Redux Toolkit is a universal library for the management of an application's global state, ideally suited for complex business logic not connected directly with network requests. React Query (TanStack Query) focuses on asynchronous state: the caching, the synchronisation and the updating of data obtained over REST, GraphQL or other protocols.","The choice between Apollo Client, Redux Toolkit and React Query is determined by your application's architectural needs. Every library solves different tasks, and they can be combined.\\nApollo Client: For GraphQL\\nApollo Client is a comprehensive solution for applications that use a GraphQL API. It provides a built-in normalised cache, which automatically updates the UI when the data changes. It is ideally suited for complex GraphQL operations, subscriptions, pagination and optimistic updates included.\\n\\nRedux Toolkit: For global state\\nRedux Toolkit (RTK) is the standard way of managing global state in React. It is excellently suited for state not connected with network requests: the state of the UI, a form's data, complex business logic that has to be available from different components. RTK Query (part of RTK) adds capabilities for work with an API, but RTK's main strength is in the predictable management of state.\\n\\nReact Query: For asynchronous state\\nReact Query (TanStack Query) specialises in the management of asynchronous state: caching, background updating, synchronisation, pagination and infinite queries. It does not depend on the type of the API (REST, GraphQL) and works excellently together with any manager of state, such as Redux or Context, taking on all of the work with the server's data.\\n\\nConclusion: Use Apollo Client if your back end is GraphQL and you need a deep integration with its capabilities. Choose Redux Toolkit for complex client-side state that requires strict control and predictability. React Query is the best choice for the management of the caching, the synchronisation and the lifecycle of the server's data in applications with REST, GraphQL or other APIs, especially when you want to simplify the code connected with the loading of data.","import { useQuery, gql } from '@apollo/client';\\nconst GET_USERS = gql\`\\n  query GetUsers {\\n    users {\\n      id\\n      name\\n    }\\n  }\\n\`;\\nfunction UsersList() {\\n  const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(GET_USERS);\\n  // Apollo manages the cache and the state of the loading and the errors\\n}\\n\\nimport { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: { value: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment(state) { state.value += 1; }\\n  }\\n});\\n// It holds state that does not depend on the server.\\n\\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\nfunction fetchUsers() {\\n  return fetch('/api/users').then(res => res.json());\\n}\\nfunction UsersList() {\\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['users'],\\n    queryFn: fetchUsers,\\n  });\\n  // Automatic caching, repeated requests on the focus of the window\\n}"],[1420,"The process of the reconnection of a WebSocket is the client's attempt to restore the connection after its unexpected break. The client usually tracks the 'onclose' or 'onerror' events and starts a timer for the repeated connection. To avoid a load on the server, exponential backoff is used, increasing the interval between the attempts. A 'heartbeat' (ping/pong) mechanism is also often implemented for the early detection of \\"quiet\\" breaks.","A WebSocket provides a full two-way connection, but network problems, a restart of the server or the idling of the client can break it. A robust application has to restore the connection automatically.\\nThe mechanism of the detection of a break\\nThe client-side logic tracks the events of the WebSocket object. The key events for the reconnection:\\n\\n- \`onclose\`: it fires on the closing of the connection (normal or abnormal).\\n- \`onerror\`: it indicates an error, after which a closing usually follows.\\nOn the occurrence of those events the procedure of the repeated connection is started.\\nThe strategy of the repeated attempts\\nA simple immediate repeat can create a load if the server is temporarily unavailable. A widespread strategy is exponential backoff. The interval between the attempts increases by a formula (it is doubled, for example) until a maximum is reached.\\n\\nHeartbeat (Ping/Pong) for \\"quiet\\" breaks\\nSometimes the connection is formally open but no data passes through (because of a NAT timeout, for example). For the detection of such cases the client periodically sends service messages (a ping), expecting a response (a pong). If the response has not come within a particular time, the connection is forcibly closed, which starts the standard reconnection.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe automatic reconnection of a WebSocket is an obligatory element for:\\n\\n- Chat applications and systems of instant messaging.\\n- Trading platforms and dashboards with live data.\\n- Online games and collaborative tools.\\n- Any systems in which the currency of the data in real time matters.\\nConclusion: The implementation of a reconnection with exponential backoff and a heartbeat makes a real-time application robust to temporary failures of the network and the server, improving the user experience.","let reconnectAttempts = 0;\\nlet maxReconnectDelay = 30000;\\n\\nfunction connectWebSocket() {\\n  const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/socket');\\n  \\n  ws.onclose = () => {\\n    const delay = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, reconnectAttempts), maxReconnectDelay);\\n    reconnectAttempts++;\\n    setTimeout(connectWebSocket, delay);\\n  };\\n  \\n  ws.onopen = () => {\\n    reconnectAttempts = 0; // The reset of the counter on a successful connection\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The initial call\\nconnectWebSocket();\\n\\n// An example of the implementation of a heartbeat on the client\\nlet heartbeatInterval;\\nlet pongTimeout;\\n\\nfunction setupHeartbeat(ws) {\\n  // We send a ping every 30 seconds\\n  heartbeatInterval = setInterval(() => {\\n    if (ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {\\n      ws.send(JSON.stringify({type: 'ping'}));\\n      // We wait for a pong for 5 seconds\\n      pongTimeout = setTimeout(() => {\\n        console.warn('Pong timeout, closing connection.');\\n        ws.close(); // This will cause onclose and the reconnection\\n      }, 5000);\\n    }\\n  }, 30000);\\n\\n  ws.onmessage = (event) => {\\n    const msg = JSON.parse(event.data);\\n    if (msg.type === 'pong') {\\n      clearTimeout(pongTimeout); // We have received the response, we reset the timeout\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  ws.onclose = () => {\\n    clearInterval(heartbeatInterval);\\n    clearTimeout(pongTimeout);\\n  };\\n}"],[1421,"The approaches divide into the local management of state (useState, Context) and the global kind (Redux, Zustand). For requests there are the built-in fetch and specialised libraries (React Query, SWR). The advantages of the local approach are its simplicity and the speed of the development for small applications; the disadvantage is the difficulty of scaling. The global solutions (Redux) ensure predictability and debuggability, but they add boilerplate code. Specialised libraries for requests (React Query) cache the data and manage the background updates, simplifying the logic, but they require their API to be learned.","A survey of the approaches to the management of state and of requests\\nIn modern web applications, especially in React, the management of state and of asynchronous requests is a key task of the architecture. The choice of the approach affects the maintainability, the performance and the speed of the development. The main division passes between the management of a component's local state, an application's global state and a separate layer for the data obtained from the server.\\nThe local management of state (useState, useReducer)\\nThis is the simplest approach, built into React. The state lives inside the component and is passed down through props.\\n\\n- The advantages: There are no external dependencies, it is fast to begin, and it is ideal for isolated UI logic (a form's state, for example).\\n- The disadvantages: \\"Prop drilling\\" with deep nesting; it is hard to share state between distant components.\\nThe global management of state (Redux, Zustand, MobX)\\nThe state is moved out into a separate store, available from any component.\\n\\n- The advantages of Redux: Predictability (pure reducers), powerful tools of debugging (Redux DevTools), a strict architecture for large teams.\\n- The disadvantages of Redux: A large volume of boilerplate code (actions, reducers), a learning curve.\\n- The alternatives (Zustand): Less boilerplate, a simpler API, but a less strict control of the flow of the data.\\nThe management of server state and of requests (React Query, SWR, Apollo)\\nThese libraries specialise in the data from the back end: caching, invalidation, background updating, pagination.\\n\\n- The advantages of React Query: The automatic caching and updating of the data, a minimum of the manual management of the state of the loading and the errors, powerful hooks for infinite queries and optimistic updates.\\n- The disadvantages: An additional dependency; the concepts (stale-while-revalidate) have to be learned. It does not replace global client-side state (the theme of the UI, for example).\\nA practical example: a comparison of the approaches\\nLet us look at a simple component that loads a list of users.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor small applications or isolated components useState/useEffect is enough. For complex client-side state that many components change (a shopping basket, for example), choose Zustand or Redux. For data from the server (lists, details) it is almost always worth using a specialised library (React Query/SWR), in order to avoid the manual management of the cache and to raise the performance.","// 1. The native approach with fetch and useState\\nconst [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\\nconst [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  setLoading(true);\\n  fetch('/api/users')\\n    .then(r => r.json())\\n    .then(setUsers)\\n    .finally(() => setLoading(false));\\n}, []);\\n\\n// 2. The approach with React Query\\nconst { data: users, isLoading } = useQuery('users', () =>\\n  fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json())\\n);\\n// The caching, the repeated requests on the focus of the window and the errors are handled automatically."],[1422,"On a partial failure of a micro front end you have to ensure the application's fault tolerance. The main approaches: show a fallback UI, use cached versions of the module, or hide the broken section. It is critical not to let the whole application fall completely because of one part that is not working. The implementation depends on the framework chosen (Module Federation in Webpack, for example) and has to include global handlers of errors of the loading.","In a micro-front-end architecture an application consists of independent remote modules, which are loaded at run time. If one of those modules (the catalogue of goods or the admin panel, for example) falls or becomes unavailable, that should not lead to the complete collapse of the whole application. Strategies of partial fault tolerance have to be implemented.\\nThe main strategies of the handling of failures\\n\\n- A fallback UI: The most widespread approach. On an error of the loading or the execution of the module, show the user a fallback interface — an error message, a spinner or a simplified but functional version of the component.\\n- Caching: Hold the previous successfully loaded version of the module (in localStorage or a Service Worker, for example) and use it on the next unsuccessful attempt at the loading.\\n- Conditional hiding: If the functionality is not critical for the main flow, you can simply hide the broken section of the interface, leaving the rest of the application working.\\n- A retry with exponential backoff: On temporary network failures you can make several attempts at reloading the module with increasing pauses between them.\\nThe practical implementation\\nLet us look at an example with the use of Webpack Module Federation and React. You have to wrap the loading of the remote module in a component with the handling of errors.\\n\\nAt the level of the container application it is also worth configuring a global handler of errors (by means of \`window.addEventListener('error', ...)\`, for example) for the interception of unsuccessful loadings of scripts.\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis approach is critically important in large distributed web applications, in which different teams independently develop and deploy their own parts. It ensures robustness and uninterrupted working for the end user even on failures in individual services.\\nConclusion: The handling of partial failures is an obligatory practice for production applications on micro front ends. It lets you isolate the failures while preserving the working capacity of the main functionality, and it improves the user experience. Use fallback interfaces and the monitoring of errors for the key remote modules.","import React, { Suspense, lazy, useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nconst RemoteWidget = () => {\\n  const [Component, setComponent] = useState(null);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(false);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The dynamic import of the remote module\\n    const loadComponent = async () => {\\n      try {\\n        // 'remoteApp' and 'Widget' are the configuration of Module Federation\\n        const comp = await import('remoteApp/Widget');\\n        setComponent(() => comp.default);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        console.error('Failed to load remote module:', err);\\n        setError(true);\\n        // Here the error can be sent to a monitoring system\\n      }\\n    };\\n    loadComponent();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  if (error) {\\n    // The fallback UI on an error\\n    return (\\n      <div>\\n        <p>This section is temporarily unavailable. Please try later.</p>\\n        <button onClick={() => window.location.reload()}>Refresh</button>\\n      </div>\\n    );\\n  }\\n\\n  if (!Component) {\\n    // The state of loading\\n    return <div>Loading the module...</div>;\\n  }\\n\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Component />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default RemoteWidget;"],[1423,"A WebSocket is a protocol for the full two-way exchange of data over a permanent connection. SSE (Server-Sent Events) lets the server send events to the client over one HTTP connection, but the client cannot send data through that same channel. Long polling is a technique in which the client sends a request and the server holds it open until new information appears, after which the client immediately sends a new request. A WebSocket is suitable for interactive applications, SSE for streaming from the server, and long polling is a simple but less efficient alternative.","To create real-time applications, such as chats, online games or financial dashboards, you have to choose a mechanism of communication that efficiently transfers data between the client and the server. The main approaches — WebSocket, Server-Sent Events (SSE) and long polling — solve that task in different ways, with different compromises in performance, complexity and support in browsers.\\nWebSocket: A two-way duplex channel\\nA WebSocket establishes a permanent TCP connection after an initial handshake over HTTP. After that both the client and the server can send data at any time independently of one another, without the need to create new HTTP requests. This makes the protocol ideal for scenarios with an intensive exchange of messages in both directions.\\n\\nServer-Sent Events (SSE): A one-way stream from the server\\nSSE uses one HTTP connection, through which the server can send the client a stream of events in textual format. The client connects once and listens to the incoming events. Data cannot be sent to the server through that same channel, however — for that a separate HTTP request (fetch, for example) would be required.\\n\\nLong Polling: The emulation of real time through HTTP\\nLong polling is a technique in which the client sends an HTTP request and the server does not answer immediately but waits until new information appears to send. As soon as the server answers, the client immediately sends the next request, creating the illusion of a permanent connection. This creates a large load because of the constant opening and closing of connections.\\n\\nA comparison and the application\\n\\n- A WebSocket is used when two-way communication with a low delay is needed: chats, collaborative editors, multiplayer games.\\n- SSE is excellently suited for notifications, a news feed or monitoring, in which the server is the main source of the data.\\n- Long polling is a fallback for older browsers or for simple scenarios in which a WebSocket or SSE cannot be used, but it is less efficient.\\nConclusion: Choose a WebSocket for interactive applications with two-way communication, SSE for the streaming of data from the server, and long polling as a simple but less performant alternative when the other variants are not available.","// An example of a client in JavaScript\\nconst socket = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/socket');\\nsocket.onopen = () => {\\n  socket.send('Hello, server!'); // The client sends data\\n};\\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log('From the server:', event.data); // The client receives data\\n};\\n// The server can send a message at any moment\\n\\n// The client-side code for SSE\\nconst eventSource = new EventSource('/updates');\\neventSource.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log('A new event:', event.data);\\n};\\n// To send data to the server we use a separate request:\\n// fetch('/send-data', { method: 'POST', body: data });\\n\\n// A simplified implementation of long polling on the client\\nfunction longPoll() {\\n  fetch('/poll')\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(data => {\\n      console.log('The data:', data);\\n      longPoll(); // We request again immediately\\n    })\\n    .catch(() => {\\n      setTimeout(longPoll, 5000); // A repeat on an error\\n    });\\n}\\nlongPoll();"],[1424,"Offline-first is an architectural approach in which an application is designed so as to work locally first of all, while the synchronisation with the server takes place when there is a network. For the implementation Service Workers are used for the caching of the critical resources and the interception of network requests. The user's data is saved locally in IndexedDB or localStorage. On the restoration of the connection background tasks (the Background Sync API, for example) send the accumulated data to the server, ensuring the recovery scenario.","Offline-first is a paradigm of development that puts an application's local working first and regards the connection with the server as an optional improvement. This is critically important for mobile applications and for web applications working in conditions of an unstable internet.\\n\\nThe key components of the implementation\\n\\n- The Service Worker: A script working in the background of the browser. It acts in the role of a proxy server, intercepting network requests. Its main task in offline-first is to serve requests from the cache when the network is unavailable.\\n\\n- Caching (the Cache API): It is used for holding static resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images) and even the responses of an API. The strategies of caching (Cache-First, Network-First, for example) determine where to take the data from first of all.\\n\\n- The local store of data (IndexedDB): For the saving of the user's data (forms, settings, the application's state) IndexedDB is used — a powerful client-side NoSQL database. It is preferable to localStorage for work with large volumes of structured data.\\n\\n- Background synchronisation (the Background Sync API): It lets you postpone the sending of data to the server until the moment the network is restored. The user can carry out an action offline, and the Service Worker will send it later, automatically.\\n\\nA practical example: the saving of a note offline\\n\\nLet us look at a simple application for notes. On the loss of the network, a new note has to be saved locally, and on the restoration of the connection be sent to the server.\\n\\nThe recovery scenario and the handling of conflicts\\n\\nRecovery is not simply the sending of the accumulated data. You have to provide for:\\n\\n- A queue of operations: The data to be sent has to be held in the order of its creation.\\n\\n- The handling of conflicts: If the data on the server has changed while the client was offline, a strategy for the resolution of the conflicts is needed (\\"the last one wins\\" or a manual merge, for example).\\n\\n- The notification of the user: The interface has to inform them of the status of the synchronisation (\\"Synchronising...\\", \\"All of the data is current\\").\\n\\nConclusion: The offline-first approach with a recovery scenario is worth applying in applications in which the continuity of the working is critical (mobile applications, tools for field work, editors). It substantially improves the user experience, making the application robust to failures of the network.","// 1. The registration of the Service Worker in the main script\\nif ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {\\n  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');\\n}\\n\\n// 2. The saving of a note in IndexedDB on the sending of the form\\nasync function saveNote(noteData) {\\n  // We check the online status\\n  if (!navigator.onLine) {\\n    // We save it in the local database\\n    await saveToIndexedDB('pendingNotes', noteData);\\n    // We register the task for the background synchronisation\\n    if ('sync' in registration) {\\n      await registration.sync.register('sync-notes');\\n    }\\n    alert('The note has been saved locally and will be sent later.');\\n  } else {\\n    // If we are online, we send it at once\\n    await sendToServer(noteData);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 3. In the Service Worker (sw.js) we handle the background synchronisation\\nself.addEventListener('sync', event => {\\n  if (event.tag === 'sync-notes') {\\n    event.waitUntil(syncNotes()); // The function for sending the data from IndexedDB\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// 4. The strategy of caching for the static files (Cache-First)\\nself.addEventListener('fetch', event => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request)\\n      .then(response => response || fetch(event.request))\\n  );\\n});"],[1425,"One large SPA is a single application in which all of the code is built and deployed together. A monorepo is a way of holding the code of several projects in one repository, but they can be built and deployed as separate applications or as one monolith. Module Federation is a technology of Webpack, which lets you dynamically load parts of an application (modules) from other, independently built and deployed applications at run time, creating the effect of a single application out of distributed sources.","In modern web development there are several strategies for the organisation of a code base and the building of applications, each of which solves different problems of scaling and of teamwork.\\n\\nOne large SPA (a monolith)\\n\\nThis is the classic approach: the whole application is one project, one build, one deployment. All of the components, pages and libraries are linked at the stage of the build. The pluses: the simplicity of the initial setup, easy refactoring within the bounds of one project. The minuses: as it grows the code base becomes cumbersome, the builds slow down, updates require the deployment of the whole application, and it is hard for teams to work independently.\\n\\nA monorepo\\n\\nA monorepo is the practice of holding the code of several projects or packages in one Git repository. That can be either several independent SPAs or a set of libraries with shared dependencies. Tools such as Lerna, Nx or Yarn Workspaces help you manage the dependencies and the scripts between the packages. The key difference: the build and the deployment can be either centralised (everything is built together) or isolated (every package is a separate build). A monorepo improves the joint work on shared code, but it does not solve the problem of independent deployment and execution.\\n\\nModule Federation\\n\\nThis is a technology built into Webpack 5, which lets one JavaScript application dynamically load code from another application at run time. In essence this is an implementation of the concept of Micro Frontends at the level of the build. The applications (the hosts and the remote modules) are built and deployed completely independently. The host application can load, for example, a React component or a whole route from another domain. This ensures the genuine independence of the teams and the ability to update the parts of a large application in stages.\\n\\nA comparison in practice\\n\\n- The coupling: An SPA has rigid coupling at the stage of the build. A monorepo has coupling at the level of the code and the dependencies. Module Federation has loose coupling at run time.\\n\\n- The deployment: In an SPA and in a monolithic monorepo there is the deployment of the whole application. In Module Federation there is the independent deployment of every micro front end.\\n\\n- The size of the bundle: An SPA may have an enormous initial bundle. Module Federation lets you load code on demand from different sources.\\n\\nAn example of the configuration of Module Federation\\n\\nConclusion: Module Federation is worth applying when you have to build a large application on whose different parts independent teams work, with the ability to deploy and update the components separately. A monorepo is better suited for the centralised management of a multitude of packages or projects with shared code but without requirements of independence at run time. One SPA remains a good choice for small or medium projects in which the complexity of a distributed architecture is not justified.","// webpack.config.js of the remote application\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'remote_app',\\n      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',\\n      exposes: {\\n        './Button': './src/components/Button', // We export the component\\n      },\\n      shared: { react: { singleton: true } }, // The shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// webpack.config.js of the host\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: 'host_app',\\n  remotes: {\\n    remote_app: 'remote_app@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n  },\\n  shared: { react: { singleton: true } },\\n});\\n\\n// The code in the host application for the dynamic loading\\nconst RemoteButton = React.lazy(() => import('remote_app/Button'));"],[1426,"Shared libraries are packages with common code (components, utilities, types) that are used by several micro front ends. They are used correctly through the centralised management of the versions — in a monorepo by means of tools such as Lerna or Nx, for example. This lets you synchronise the updates and avoid conflicts of versions. The key principle is that the libraries have to be as independent and as stable as possible, so that changes in them do not break the micro front ends.","Shared libraries in a micro-front-end architecture are a mechanism for moving repeating code out into separate packages, which can then be imported by the independent teams developing different parts of the application. This is necessary in order to avoid the duplication of such entities as UI components (buttons, modal windows), utilities for the formatting of data, API clients, constants or the definitions of TypeScript types.\\n\\nThe main approaches to the organisation\\n\\nThere are several strategies for the management of shared libraries:\\n\\n- A monorepo: Every micro front end and shared library is in one repository. Tools such as Nx, Lerna or Turborepo simplify the management of the dependencies, the scripts of the build and the versioning inside the repository. Changes in a library are immediately visible to every dependent project, which simplifies the synchronisation but requires discipline so as not to violate the backward compatibility.\\n\\n- Separate npm packages: The libraries are published to a private or a public npm registry. Every micro front end installs them as ordinary dependencies, specifying a particular version. This gives more isolation, but it requires the manual updating of the versions in every project and can lead to the situation in which different micro front ends use different major versions of one library.\\n\\n- Dynamic attachment (Module Federation): The Webpack 5 Module Federation technology lets you load the code of the shared libraries dynamically at run time from one source (the host). This reduces the size of the bundles and guarantees that every micro front end uses one and the same compiled version of the library at run time.\\n\\nThe rules of the use and the best practices\\n\\n- The minimisation of the dependencies: The shared library itself should have as few external dependencies as possible, especially heavy or conflicting ones.\\n\\n- Semantic versioning (SemVer): The strict observance of the rules of SemVer (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) for backward compatibility. Changes that break the API only in major versions.\\n\\n- The inversion of the dependencies: A library should not depend on the business logic of a specific micro front end. It provides abstractions and primitives.\\n\\n- Testing: The coverage of the library with unit and integration tests is obligatory, since its breakage will affect a multitude of projects.\\n\\nAn example of the structure in a monorepo\\n\\nConclusion: Shared libraries are worth applying for stable, well-tested code that is genuinely used in several teams. They help you maintain the uniformity of the interface and cut the costs of the development, but they add a complexity of the management of the dependencies. The optimal approach is a monorepo with tools of automation for medium and large projects in which the speed of the synchronisation of changes matters.","// The structure of a project with the use of Nx\\nmy-org/\\n├── apps/\\n│   ├── mf-auth/          # The \\"Authorisation\\" micro front end\\n│   └── mf-dashboard/     # The \\"Dashboard\\" micro front end\\n└── libs/\\n    └── shared-ui/        # The shared UI library\\n        ├── src/\\n        │   ├── Button.tsx\\n        │   └── index.ts  # The export of the components\\n        ├── project.json  # The Nx configuration\\n        └── jest.config.js\\n\\n// The code of the Button component from shared-ui\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\ntype ButtonProps = {\\n  label: string;\\n  onClick: () => void;\\n};\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onClick}>\\n      {label}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// The use in the mf-auth micro front end\\nimport { Button } from '@my-org/shared-ui';\\n\\nconst LoginForm = () => {\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      <Button\\n        label=\\"Log in\\"\\n        onClick={() => console.log('Clicked')}\\n      />\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n};"],[1427,"To solve the problems of a change of the structure of data in localStorage between versions of an application you have to introduce a system of versioning of the data's schema. On the start of the application you should check the version of the data saved and apply a sequence of migration functions to convert the old format into the new one. This lets users preserve their data after an update of the application without losses. The key steps: the holding of the version, the writing of the migrations, their safe execution and a rollback on errors.","In the development of client-side web applications that use \`localStorage\` to save state, the user's data or settings, sooner or later the need arises to change the structure of that data — to add a new field, rename an existing one or change the type of a value. If you simply release a new version of the application, the old data saved in the users' browsers may become unreadable or lead to errors, which will spoil the user experience. A mechanism of the smooth updating of the data is therefore needed.\\nThe main strategy: versioning and migrations\\nThe idea consists in holding the number of their version together with the data (\`dataVersion: 1\`, for example). On every change of the structure that breaks the backward compatibility, the version is incremented. On the start the application checks the current saved version and, if it is less than the one expected, successively applies migration functions, which convert the data from the format of version N into the format of version N+1.\\nThe practical implementation\\nLet us look at an example in JavaScript. Suppose we hold an object of the user's settings. In version 1 we had the field \`userName\`, and in version 2 we want to split it into \`firstName\` and \`lastName\`.\\n\\nWhere it is applied and the important aspects\\nSuch an approach is critically important for any application that holds state on the client and plans long-term development. It is applied not only in \`localStorage\` but also in work with IndexedDB, file formats or even a server's API (although there the migrations usually take place on the side of the database). The key points for reliability:\\n\\n- The idempotence of the migrations: A migration can be applied several times without a change in the result.\\n- The handling of errors: If a migration falls over, you have to have a strategy of rollback (not to overwrite the original data until the migration has finished successfully, for example).\\n- Backup: Before a migration the raw data can be saved under a separate key in case something goes wrong.\\n- Testing: The migrations have to be covered with tests with real data of the old versions.\\nConclusion: Use the versioning of data and step-by-step migrations when your application develops and changes the structure of the client-side data. This ensures a problem-free update for the users and spares you the need for the forcible resetting of their localStorage on every release.","// The constant with the current version of the data's schema in the application\\nconst CURRENT_DATA_VERSION = 2;\\n\\n// The function for obtaining the data from localStorage\\nfunction getAppData() {\\n  const rawData = localStorage.getItem('myAppData');\\n  if (!rawData) {\\n    // If there is no data, we return the default data with the current version\\n    return { dataVersion: CURRENT_DATA_VERSION, firstName: '', lastName: '' };\\n  }\\n  \\n  let data = JSON.parse(rawData);\\n  // If the version is not specified, we consider it outdated (version 0, for example)\\n  const storedVersion = data.dataVersion || 0;\\n  \\n  // We apply the migrations if the version is outdated\\n  if (storedVersion < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION) {\\n    data = runMigrations(data, storedVersion);\\n  }\\n  \\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\n// The collection of migration functions\\nconst migrations = {\\n  // The migration from version 0 to version 1 (if the version was not held)\\n  0: (data) => {\\n    // We add the dataVersion field\\n    data.dataVersion = 1;\\n    return data;\\n  },\\n  // The migration from version 1 to version 2\\n  1: (data) => {\\n    // We split userName into firstName and lastName\\n    if (data.userName) {\\n      const parts = data.userName.split(' ');\\n      data.firstName = parts[0] || '';\\n      data.lastName = parts.slice(1).join(' ') || '';\\n      delete data.userName;\\n    } else {\\n      data.firstName = '';\\n      data.lastName = '';\\n    }\\n    data.dataVersion = 2;\\n    return data;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfunction runMigrations(data, fromVersion) {\\n  let migratedData = { ...data };\\n  for (let v = fromVersion; v < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION; v++) {\\n    if (migrations[v]) {\\n      migratedData = migrations[v](migratedData);\\n    } else {\\n      console.error(\`Migration from version \${v} not found!\`);\\n      // In the event of an error the data can be reset or an exception thrown\\n      break;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  // We save the updated data back into the store\\n  localStorage.setItem('myAppData', JSON.stringify(migratedData));\\n  return migratedData;\\n}"],[1428,"The interpreter is responsible for the reading and the execution of a program's source code line by line or in parts. The Garbage Collector is a separate mechanism of the runtime environment, which automatically frees the memory occupied by objects that are no longer used by the program. The interpreter carries out the application's logic, while the garbage collector manages the resources, clearing the memory. This lets the developer not have to bother with the manual management of memory.","In a JavaScript runtime environment, such as V8 in Chrome or SpiderMonkey in Firefox, the interpreter and the garbage collector (GC) carry out fundamentally different but interconnected tasks for the ensuring of the application's working.\\nThe interpreter\\nThe interpreter is responsible for the direct execution of the source code. In modern engines it often works in concert with a compiler (JIT compilation). Its main function is to convert the high-level JavaScript code into machine instructions that the processor can carry out. It manages the flow of the execution, calls the functions, handles the expressions and interacts with the API of the browser or of Node.js.\\nThe Garbage Collector\\nThe garbage collector is an algorithm working in the background. Its goal is the automatic management of memory. It tracks every object created during the program's execution (variables, functions, DOM elements, for example) and determines which of them are no longer reachable from the root objects (the global scope, for example). Such unreachable objects are considered \\"garbage\\", and the GC frees the memory they occupy.\\nAn example of the interaction\\nLet us look at a simple example in which the working of both mechanisms is visible:\\n\\nThe interpreter carried out the function's code, and the GC took care of the cleanup after its execution.\\nWhere this is applied\\nThis is a fundamental mechanism of every managed language (JavaScript, Java, C#, Python). An understanding of the GC is critical for the writing of efficient code, the avoidance of memory leaks (when objects mistakenly remain reachable) and the optimisation of performance, especially in long-lived applications such as SPAs or servers on Node.js.\\nConclusion: The interpreter carries your code out, and the garbage collector manages the memory, freeing the developer from manual control. An understanding of their working helps you write more efficient and more stable applications, avoiding the widespread problems with memory leaks.","function createUser() {\\n  let user = { name: 'Alice' }; // The interpreter creates the object in memory.\\n  console.log(user.name);\\n  // The function finishes. The object \`user\` is no longer reachable,\\n  // since there are no references to it outside the function.\\n}\\n\\ncreateUser();\\n// After the call of the function the garbage collector will at some moment\\n// discover that the object { name: 'Alice' } has become garbage,\\n// and will free the memory it occupies."],[1429,"Yes, data can be passed in a GET request, but only in the form of the parameters of the query string, which are added to the URL after the '?' sign. For example, in the URL 'https://api.example.com/users?id=123&name=John' the parameters 'id' and 'name' are passed as data. GET requests are intended for the obtaining of resources, however, rather than for the sending of large or confidential data, since the parameters are visible in the browser's history and in the server's logs and can be cached.","The HTTP method GET is intended for requesting data from a server, but it also lets information be passed from the client to the server through the parameters of the query string. Those parameters are added to the URL after the question mark and are separated by ampersands.\\n\\nHow data is passed in a GET\\n\\nThe data is encoded in the form of key-value pairs and is included directly in the URL. For example, in a search for goods in an online shop the URL may look like this: \`https://store.com/products?category=books&sort=price\`. Here two parameters have been passed: category with the value 'books' and sort with the value 'price'.\\n\\nThe limitations and the best practices\\n\\n- The length of the URL: The maximum length of a URL is limited by browsers and servers (usually 2048 characters), so large volumes of data should not be passed.\\n\\n- Security: The parameters are visible in the address bar, in the browser's history and in servers' logs and can be cached. Do not use a GET for the passing of passwords, tokens or other confidential information.\\n\\n- The semantics: According to the HTTP standards, a GET should be idempotent and safe (it should not change the server's state). For operations that change data (creation, updating, deletion), use POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE.\\n\\nAn example of code\\n\\nBelow it is shown how to form a GET request with parameters in JavaScript by means of the Fetch API:\\n\\nIn this example the parameters \`query\`, \`limit\` and \`offset\` are automatically encoded and added to the URL. The server can read those parameters in order to filter the results of the search.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nUse GET requests for the passing of small, non-confidential data that is necessary for filtering, sorting or pagination in the obtaining of resources. For the sending of large volumes of data or of confidential information, apply POST or other methods.","// We form the URL with the parameters\\nconst baseUrl = 'https://api.example.com/search';\\nconst params = new URLSearchParams({\\n  query: 'JavaScript',\\n  limit: 10,\\n  offset: 0\\n});\\nconst url = \`\${baseUrl}?\${params.toString()}\`; // The complete URL\\n\\n// We carry out the GET request\\nfetch(url)\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[1430,"The operations of search, insertion and deletion in a dictionary have on average a complexity of O(1) thanks to the use of a hash table. The key is converted by a hash function into an index of an array (a bucket), which lets you obtain access to the value in a fixed time. In the worst case, with a multitude of collisions, the complexity can degrade to O(n), but with a good hash function and a resolution of the collisions (by the method of chaining, for example) the average complexity remains constant.","A dictionary (or an associative array) in most programming languages is implemented as a hash table. The main idea consists in converting the key into a numeric index by means of a hash function and then using that index for direct access to the cell (the bucket) in the internal array.\\n\\nHow a hash table works\\n\\nThe process can be broken down into steps:\\n\\n- For the key given a hash code (a whole number) is computed.\\n\\n- That hash code is mapped onto an index in the array of buckets, usually by means of the operation of taking the remainder of the division by the size of the array.\\n\\n- At that index the bucket is found, which may contain one or several key-value pairs.\\n\\n- If there is one pair in the bucket and the key coincides, the operation has finished. If there are several pairs (a collision), an additional comparison of the keys inside the bucket takes place.\\n\\nWhy the complexity is O(1) on average\\n\\nThe computation of the hash and the access by an index in an array are operations with a fixed time, not depending on the number of elements n. The resolution of the collisions adds a certain variable component, but with a competent implementation (the maintenance of the load factor, a good hash function) the length of the chains in the buckets remains a small constant on average. The overall time of the access therefore does not grow as n increases.\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nHash tables lie at the foundation of a multitude of structures: dictionaries in Python, objects in JavaScript (for string keys), HashMap in Java, unordered_map in C++. They are indispensable for tasks that require a fast search by a key: caching, indexing in databases, the counting of frequencies, the holding of configurations.\\n\\nConclusion: Dictionaries ensure a constant time for operations on average thanks to the hash table, which makes them the ideal choice when a frequent search for, insertion of or deletion of data by a unique key is required and the order of the elements does not matter.","# The creation of a dictionary\\nmy_dict = {\\"apple\\": 5, \\"banana\\": 3, \\"cherry\\": 8}\\n\\n# The operation of access - O(1) on average\\nvalue = my_dict[\\"banana\\"]  # The hash of \\"banana\\", the index, the access to the bucket\\nprint(value)  # 3\\n\\n# The insertion - also O(1) on average\\nmy_dict[\\"date\\"] = 12\\n\\n# The deletion - O(1) on average\\ndel my_dict[\\"apple\\"]"],[1431,"The main tools for the analysis of memory leaks are Chrome DevTools and the analogues in other browsers. In DevTools there are the Memory and Performance tabs, in which you can take heap snapshots and compare them in order to find the objects that are not deleted by the garbage collector. For Node.js applications you can use the built-in v8 module or third-party tools such as clinic.js. Monitors of performance that show the growth of the use of memory over time are also useful.","Memory leaks take place when objects remain in memory although the program no longer needs them, which over time can lead to a slowdown of the working or to the fall of the application. The analysis of such leaks is a key task for the maintenance of stability, especially in long-lived applications such as single-page applications (SPAs) or Node.js servers.\\nThe main tools in the browser\\nChrome DevTools provides several powerful tools on the Memory tab:\\n\\n- Heap snapshot — it takes an instantaneous snapshot of the objects in the heap. By comparing the snapshots before and after actions, you can find the objects that were not deleted.\\n- Allocation instrumentation on timeline — it records the allocation of memory over time, showing where objects are created and whether they remain.\\n- Allocation sampling — it profiles the allocation of memory using statistical sampling, which loads the performance less.\\nOn the Performance tab you can also record a session and observe the graph of the use of memory (the JS Heap). Constant growth without falls after the collection of garbage is a sign of a leak.\\nThe tools for Node.js\\nFor server-side JavaScript you can use:\\n\\n- The built-in v8 module: \`node --inspect\` lets you connect DevTools and use the same methods.\\n- clinic.js — a set of tools for the diagnosis of performance, the analysis of memory included.\\n- memwatch-next or heapdump — npm packages for the taking of dumps of the heap by hand.\\nA practical example of the taking of a heap snapshot\\nHere is how you can create a leak and analyse it in DevTools:\\n\\nIn DevTools: open Memory, choose \\"Heap snapshot\\", press \\"Take snapshot\\". Carry out \`createLeak()\` several times and take another snapshot. Compare the snapshots and filter by \\"Comparison\\" — you will see how many new objects were created and retained.\\nConclusion\\nThe tools of the analysis of memory are worth applying on a suspicion of a degradation of performance, especially in long-lived applications or after the addition of complex functions. Regular checking by means of heap snapshots and monitoring helps you find leaks at the early stages.","// An example of code with a potential leak (the saving of references in an array)\\nlet leaks = [];\\nfunction createLeak() {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    leaks.push({ data: new Array(1000).join('*') });\\n  }\\n}\\n// We call it several times\\ncreateLeak();\\ncreateLeak();\\n// The objects in the leaks array will not be deleted, even if they are no longer used."],[1432,"The complexity of operations with a dictionary in Python is usually O(1) for the insertion, the retrieval and the deletion of an element. It can worsen to O(n) in the worst case because of collisions of hashes, when many keys land in one cell of the internal array. This happens if the keys are not hashable or if an attacker deliberately chooses keys that cause collisions (a hashDoS attack). The performance also falls on the frequent changing of the size of the internal array (rehashing), although that is an amortised operation.","A dictionary (dict) in Python is an implementation of a hash table. In the ideal case, when the hash function distributes the keys evenly, the main operations (get, set, delete) are carried out in a constant time of O(1). There are situations, however, in which the performance falls sharply.\\n\\nThe causes of the worsening of the complexity\\n\\n- Collisions of hashes: If different keys have the same hash, they are placed in one cell (a bucket). Inside the bucket the search becomes linear (O(n) for that bucket). With a large quantity of collisions the overall complexity tends towards O(n).\\n\\n- A bad hash function: For user classes, if the \`__hash__\` method is written badly and returns identical values for different objects.\\n\\n- A hashDoS attack: An attacker can pass a multitude of deliberately chosen keys that cause collisions, which will lead to a denial of service.\\n\\n- The change of the size (resize/rehash): On the addition of elements, when the fullness exceeds a threshold, the internal array is enlarged, and every existing key-value pair has to be rehashed and moved. This is an O(n) operation, but it happens infrequently and is amortised to O(1).\\n\\nAn example of code demonstrating collisions\\n\\nIn the modern versions of Python (from 3.3+) the randomisation of hashes for strings and some types is used, which makes the carrying out of hashDoS attacks harder. Also, for the resolution of collisions open addressing is used (from CPython 3.6), which improves the performance overall.\\n\\nConclusion: The complexity of operations with a dictionary worsens with a high level of collisions of hashes, which can be caused by a bad hash function or by malicious input. For the prevention it matters to use standard hashable types (strings, numbers, tuples) as the keys and to update Python to a current version with protection against hashDoS.","# A user class with a bad hash function\\nclass BadKey:\\n    def __init__(self, value):\\n        self.value = value\\n    def __hash__(self):\\n        return 1  # Every object has the same hash!\\n    def __eq__(self, other):\\n        return self.value == other.value\\n\\n# The creation of a dictionary with such keys\\nbad_dict = {}\\nfor i in range(1000):\\n    key = BadKey(i)\\n    bad_dict[key] = i  # Every insertion will cause a linear search in the chain of collisions.\\n# The operations with this dictionary will have a complexity of O(n)."],[1433,"Authentication data is usually passed in the headers of the HTTP request. The most widespread way is the Authorization header — with the Bearer type for tokens, for example. Cookies are also often used, which the browser sends automatically for the maintenance of the session. More rarely the data may be passed in the body of the request (in the sending of a login form, for example) or as the parameters of the URL, but the latter way is considered insecure.","Web applications have to identify the user on every request to protected resources. For that the authentication data is passed from the client (the browser or another client) to the server. The main places of its passing are the HTTP headers, the body of the request and, more rarely, the parameters of the URL.\\nThe main ways of passing\\n\\n- The Authorization header: This is the standard and recommended way for an API. The format: \`Authorization: <Type> <Data>\`. For a JWT token, for example: \`Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...\`.\\n- Cookies: After a successful log-in the server sets a cookie (\`sessionid\`, for example), which the browser automatically attaches to every subsequent request in the \`Cookie\` header. This is the classic approach for websites with sessions.\\n- The body of the request: In the initial authentication (the log-in) the data (the login and the password) is usually sent in the body of a POST request, often in JSON or \`application/x-www-form-urlencoded\` format.\\n- The query parameters: The passing of a token in the URL (\`?token=abc\`, for example) is extremely inadvisable, since the URL may land in the server's logs and the browser's history.\\nAn example of code (the client-side sending)\\nHere is how the sending of an authenticated request with the use of JavaScript (the Fetch API) may look:\\n\\nWhat is applied where?\\n\\n- SPAs and mobile applications (APIs): They use predominantly the \`Authorization\` header with Bearer tokens (JWT, OAuth).\\n- Traditional server-side web applications: They work on the basis of sessions, holding the identifier in a cookie.\\n- Basic authentication (Basic Auth): It also uses the Authorization header, but in the format \`Basic <base64(login:password)>\`. It is now applied rarely, mainly in simple scenarios or in internal systems.\\nConclusion: For modern APIs and single-page applications the preferable method is the passing of the token in the \`Authorization\` header. For classic websites with server-side rendering it is convenient to use session cookies. The key rule is: never pass sensitive authentication data (especially long-lived tokens) through the URL, because of the risks to security.","// The use of a Bearer token in the Authorization header\\nconst token = 'your_jwt_token_here';\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/protected-data', {\\n    method: 'GET',\\n    headers: {\\n        'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`,\\n        'Content-Type': 'application/json'\\n    }\\n})\\n.then(response => response.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));\\n\\n// A request with cookies is sent automatically by the browser,\\n// if the cookies are set for the domain api.example.com."],[1434,"In most high-level languages (Java, C#, Python, Go) the garbage collector (GC) works automatically, and direct management of it is impossible or extremely inadvisable. You can influence its working indirectly, however: by calling \`System.gc()\` in Java (which is merely a hint for the JVM), by using \`GC.Collect()\` in .NET, or by managing the references (by nulling them, for example). In languages with the manual management of memory (C, C++, Rust) the developer controls the allocation and the freeing of memory themselves through \`malloc/free\` or smart pointers, which completely replaces a GC.","Garbage collection (GC) is an automatic process of the management of memory, which frees the objects no longer used by the program. In languages with the automatic management of memory (Java, C#, Python, Go, JavaScript) the GC works in the background, and direct \\"manual\\" management of it is usually not provided for by the design of the language, since that can disrupt the optimisations and lead to unpredictable pauses.\\nIndirect influence on the GC\\nDevelopers can give hints to the collector or influence its behaviour:\\n\\n- Java: The \`System.gc()\` or \`Runtime.getRuntime().gc()\` method is a request to the JVM to run the collection of garbage, but the JVM may ignore it. It is not recommended for use in production.\\n- .NET (C#): The \`GC.Collect()\` method forcibly runs the collection. It can be used in narrow scenarios — in testing, for example, or at points at which it is known that a lot of memory will be freed.\\n- Python: The \`gc\` module provides the functions \`gc.collect()\` for a manual run and \`gc.disable()\`/\`gc.enable()\` for the disabling and enabling of the GC. This is useful in real time or in work with circular references.\\n- Go: The \`runtime\` package lets you call \`runtime.GC()\` for a forcible collection, and also configure the percentage of CPU for the GC through \`debug.SetGCPercent()\`.\\nAn example of code in C#\\n\\nLanguages with the manual management of memory\\nIn C, C++ and Rust there is no built-in GC. The developer completely controls the lifecycle of the objects:\\n\\n- C: \`malloc()\` and \`free()\`.\\n- C++: The \`new\`/\`delete\` operators and smart pointers (\`std::unique_ptr\`, \`std::shared_ptr\`), which automatically free the memory on the exit from the scope.\\n- Rust: The system of ownership and borrowing guarantees the safety of memory without a GC.\\nConclusion: Direct management of the GC is rarely required, mainly for fine optimisation in highly loaded applications (games, real time) or in work with unmanaged resources. In ordinary applications it is better to rely on the automatic GC, and to use profilers of memory for the control.","// The forcible run of the collection of garbage for generations 0 and 1\\nGC.Collect(0, GCCollectionMode.Forced);\\n// Waiting for the finalizers to finish (optional)\\nGC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();\\n// The collection for every generation\\nGC.Collect();\\n// The freeing of unmanaged resources by hand - the key pattern\\nusing (var resource = new FileStream(\\"test.txt\\", FileMode.Open))\\n{\\n    // the work with the resource\\n} // Dispose will be called automatically, helping the GC"],[1435,"The Event Loop in Node.js is the mechanism that handles asynchronous operations in one thread. CPU-bound tasks are computations that use the processor intensively (complex mathematical calculations or synchronous loops, for example). Since the Event Loop works in one main thread, while it is carrying out such a task it cannot handle other requests, timers or events of input and output. This leads to the blocking of the whole application, an increase in the response time and a fall in performance.","The Event Loop in Node.js is the core of its asynchronous, non-blocking architecture. It works in one thread and coordinates the execution of the code, handling the callbacks from the queue after the operations of input and output (I/O) have finished. If, however, a lengthy synchronous task requiring intensive computations (CPU-bound) is being carried out in that single thread, the Event Loop cannot move on to the next iteration until that task has finished.\\n\\nWhy does the blocking take place?\\n\\nThe main JavaScript thread, in which the Event Loop works, can carry out only one operation at a time. CPU-bound tasks, such as the sorting of enormous arrays, cryptographic computations or synchronous loops without points of release, occupy that thread for a lengthy time. During that period:\\n\\n- Incoming HTTP requests are not handled.\\n\\n- Timers (setTimeout, setInterval) do not fire.\\n\\n- The callbacks from finished asynchronous operations (the reading of a file or a query to a database, for example) are not carried out.\\n\\nThis leads to a \\"freeze\\" of the application, which is unacceptable for servers that require a high level of parallel handling.\\n\\nAn example of blocking code\\n\\nLet us look at a simple server with a CPU-bound operation:\\n\\nOn a request to \`/compute\` the server will \\"hang\\" for several seconds, and every other request (even to other routes) will wait for the loop to finish.\\n\\nHow do you avoid the blocking?\\n\\nFor work with CPU-bound tasks in Node.js several approaches are used:\\n\\n- The allocation of child processes: The running of the task in a separate process through \`child_process.fork()\`.\\n\\n- Worker Threads: The use of the \`worker_threads\` module to carry code out in separate threads (but not in the main Event Loop).\\n\\n- The splitting of the task into parts: By means of \`setImmediate()\` or \`process.nextTick()\` you can split a large task into small chunks, giving the Event Loop the ability to handle other events between them.\\n\\n- The use of external services: The moving of heavy computations out into a separate microservice in a language more suited to CPU-bound work (Go or Rust, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: CPU-bound tasks block the Event Loop because it is single-threaded. To maintain the server's responsiveness, such tasks have to be moved out of the main thread, using processes, threads or the splitting into parts. This approach is critically important for the building of scalable applications on Node.js.","const http = require('http');\\n\\n// A lengthy synchronous task (the imitation of CPU-bound work)\\nfunction heavyCalculation() {\\n    let sum = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) { // 1 billion iterations\\n        sum += i;\\n    }\\n    return sum;\\n}\\n\\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\\n    if (req.url === '/compute') {\\n        const result = heavyCalculation(); // It blocks the Event Loop!\\n        res.end(\`Result: \${result}\`);\\n    } else {\\n        res.end('OK');\\n    }\\n});\\n\\nserver.listen(3000);"],[1436,"JWTs (JSON Web Tokens) are often passed in HTTP headers and can be intercepted or stolen. The tokens are usually held in the browser's localStorage, which makes them vulnerable to XSS attacks. Although a JWT is signed, its contents are easily decoded (the payload is in Base64), so anyone who obtains the token can read the data. Sensitive data (passwords, card numbers) should be held only on the server in a protected database.","JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) have become a popular standard for authentication and for the passing of information between parties in the form of a JSON object. A token consists of three parts: the header, the payload and the signature, separated by dots and encoded in Base64URL.\\n\\nWhy JWTs are vulnerable to the disclosure of data\\n\\nThe main problem consists in the fact that the payload of a JWT is not encrypted by default — it is only encoded in Base64. Anyone who obtains the token can easily decode it and read the contents. For example, the token \`eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIiwibmFtZSI6IkpvaG4gRG9lIiwiaWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.SflKxwRJSMeKKF2QT4fwpMeJf36POk6yJV_adQssw5c\` can be decoded with online tools, obtaining the user's data.\\n\\nWhere JWTs are held and the risks\\n\\n- LocalStorage/SessionStorage: They are vulnerable to XSS attacks, in which a malicious script can steal the token.\\n\\n- Cookies: They are safer with the HttpOnly flag, but they are still sent with every request.\\n\\n- The transfer over the network: Tokens can be intercepted with the use of unencrypted HTTP.\\n\\nAn example of a vulnerable approach\\n\\nThe correct practice\\n\\nInstead of holding sensitive data in a JWT you should:\\n\\n- Hold only the user's identifier (the userId) or the session ID in the token.\\n\\n- Hold the sensitive data in a protected database on the server.\\n\\n- When it is necessary to pass confidential information, use encryption (JWE) or protected channels (HTTPS).\\n\\n- Use a short lifetime for the tokens and a mechanism of refresh tokens.\\n\\nAn example of a safe approach\\n\\nConclusion: JWTs are intended for the passing of verifiable claims rather than for the holding of secrets. Hold in the tokens only the minimal information necessary for the authentication, and all of the sensitive data in a protected store on the server. This especially matters in production environments with requirements of compliance (PCI DSS, GDPR).","// WRONG - the holding of sensitive data in a JWT\\nconst payload = {\\n  userId: 123,\\n  email: \\"user@example.com\\",\\n  creditCard: \\"4111-1111-1111-1111\\", // Danger!\\n  role: \\"admin\\",\\n  exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 3600\\n};\\n\\n// After the signing the token will be passed to the client\\n// Anyone who obtains the token will see the card number\\n\\n// RIGHT - minimal data in the JWT\\nconst safePayload = {\\n  sub: \\"user_123\\", // only the identifier\\n  iat: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),\\n  exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 900 // 15 minutes\\n};\\n\\n// On the server we obtain the data from the database by the user's ID\\nasync function getUserData(userId) {\\n  return db.query(\\n    \\"SELECT email, preferences FROM users WHERE id = $1\\", \\n    [userId]\\n  );\\n}"],[1437,"In Python there is the GIL (Global Interpreter Lock), which lets only one Python thread be carried out at a time, even on a multi-core processor. For tasks limited by the processor's performance (CPU-bound) this means that additional threads do not speed the computations up but merely add the overheads of the switching. To use several cores for CPU-bound tasks efficiently, you have to use multiprocessing or external libraries written in C, which can release the GIL.","In Python the mechanism of multithreading is often misleading, especially in work with tasks that use the processor intensively (CPU-bound). The key limiter here is the GIL — the Global Interpreter Lock.\\n\\nWhat the GIL is and how it works\\n\\nThe GIL is a mutex (a lock) that protects the access to Python objects, preventing the simultaneous execution of bytecode by several native threads. This simplifies the management of memory (the collection of garbage) and makes the implementation of CPython simpler and more stable. The side effect, however, is that at any moment in time only one thread can execute Python code.\\n\\nWhy this is a problem for CPU-bound tasks\\n\\nCPU-bound tasks are computations in which the main time goes on the processor's operations (mathematical calculations, the handling of images, simulations, for example). Ideally such tasks should be distributed evenly over every available CPU core. Because of the GIL, however, even if you create several threads, they will not be carried out in parallel on different cores. Instead they will take the GIL in turn, being carried out in fact sequentially (with a quasi-parallelism through the switchings). This gives no gain in performance, and it often even slows the working down because of the overheads of the creation of the threads and the switching of the context.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at a simple CPU-bound example — the computation of the sum of the squares of numbers.\\n\\nOn a multitasking processor the multithreaded version will most likely be carried out in approximately the same time or even longer than the single-threaded one, because of the GIL and the overheads.\\n\\nThe alternative solutions\\n\\n- Multiprocessing: Every process has its own Python interpreter and its own memory, and so its own GIL. This lets several CPU cores be used genuinely in parallel.\\n\\n- The use of libraries in C/C++: Code written in C extensions (NumPy or SciPy, for example) can release the GIL for the duration of intensive computations, letting other threads be carried out.\\n\\n- Asynchronicity (asyncio): It is useful for I/O-bound tasks but not for CPU-bound ones, since it works in one thread.\\n\\n- The use of interpreters without a GIL: Jython or IronPython, for example, but they are less widespread and have other limitations.\\n\\nConclusion: Multithreading in Python does not solve the problem of CPU-bound tasks because of the GIL. For the real parallelism of computations you have to use the multiprocessing module or move the heavy computations out into C extensions, which can get round that limitation.","import threading\\nimport time\\n\\ndef compute_sum(start, end):\\n    total = 0\\n    for i in range(start, end):\\n        total += i * i\\n    return total\\n\\n# The single-threaded version\\nstart_time = time.time()\\nresult = compute_sum(1, 10_000_000)\\nprint(f\\"One thread: {time.time() - start_time:.2f} sec\\")\\n\\n# The multithreaded version (2 threads)\\nstart_time = time.time()\\nthread1 = threading.Thread(target=compute_sum, args=(1, 5_000_000))\\nthread2 = threading.Thread(target=compute_sum, args=(5_000_000, 10_000_000))\\nthread1.start()\\nthread2.start()\\nthread1.join()\\nthread2.join()\\nprint(f\\"Two threads: {time.time() - start_time:.2f} sec\\")"],[1438,"The back end checks a JWT by extracting it from the request's header. Then it divides the token into three parts: the header, the payload and the signature. The key step is the verification of the signature using a secret key (for HMAC) or a public key (for RSA/ECDSA). If the signature is correct, the server can additionally check the standard claims, such as the term of validity (exp) and the audience (aud). Only after all of the checks is the token considered valid.","A JSON Web Token (JWT) is a popular standard for the secure passing of claims between parties. Once the client has received a token (after logging in to the system, for example), it sends it to the server in the \`Authorization: Bearer <token>\` header. The server's task is to make sure that this token is authentic, has not been changed and is still valid.\\nThe process of the checking\\nThe checking usually consists of the following steps:\\n\\n- The extraction and the parsing: The token is extracted from the request and divided by the dots into three segments: the header, the payload and the signature.\\n- The verification of the signature: This is the most important stage. The server computes the signature anew, using the algorithm specified in the header (HS256 or RS256, for example) and the secret (or public) key. If the computed signature coincides with the signature from the token, that confirms that the token was issued by this server (or by a trusted party) and was not forged.\\n- The checking of the claims: After the successful checking of the signature the data from the payload is considered trusted. The server checks the standard claims:\\n- \`exp\` (expiration time) — the token has not expired.\\n- \`nbf\` (not before) — the token is already active.\\n- \`iss\` (issuer) — the issuer corresponds to the one expected.\\n- \`aud\` (audience) — the token is intended for this service.\\n\\nAn example of code in Node.js with the jsonwebtoken library\\n\\nIn this example the \`jwt.verify()\` method carries out all of the key checks: the validity of the signature, the term of validity and the optional parameters of the issuer and the audience. In the event of success the decoded payload is returned, which can be used to identify the user.\\nWhere it is applied and the important aspects\\nThis approach is applied in microservice architectures and in mobile and single-page applications (SPAs). It matters to hold the secret key securely on the server (in environment variables, for example) and to use HTTPS for the passing of the token. For the revocation of tokens before their term of validity expires, an additional infrastructure is required (a blacklist of tokens, for example).\\nConclusion: The checking of a JWT on the back end is a fundamental mechanism for stateless authentication. It is worth applying when you need a scalable and decomposed way of managing sessions without holding state on the server.","const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\nconst SECRET_KEY = 'your-secret-key';\\n\\nfunction verifyToken(req, res, next) {\\n    // 1. The extraction of the token from the header\\n    const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;\\n    if (!authHeader || !authHeader.startsWith('Bearer ')) {\\n        return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token not provided' });\\n    }\\n    const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1];\\n\\n    try {\\n        // 2. The verification of the signature and the checking of the claims\\n        const decoded = jwt.verify(token, SECRET_KEY, {\\n            algorithms: ['HS256'], // The explicit specification of the algorithm\\n            issuer: 'my-auth-server',\\n            audience: 'my-api'\\n        });\\n        // 3. The user's data is available in decoded\\n        req.user = decoded;\\n        next(); // We pass control to the next middleware\\n    } catch (error) {\\n        // The handling of the errors: expired, an incorrect signature and so on\\n        return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });\\n    }\\n}"],[1439,"A JWT (JSON Web Token) consists of three parts: the header, the payload and the signature. The signature is created using a secret key and a cryptographic algorithm (HMAC SHA256, for example) on the basis of the first two parts. Without the secret key it is impossible to compute the signature correctly for changed data. The server checks the signature on every request, and if the token has been forged, the signatures will not coincide, which will lead to the rejection of the request.","A JWT (JSON Web Token) is a standard way of securely passing information between parties in the form of a JSON object. The token consists of three parts separated by dots: the header, the payload and the signature. It is precisely the signature that ensures the protection against forgery.\\n\\nHow a JWT's signature works\\n\\nThe signature is created by the hashing of the combined data of the header and the payload using a secret key and the algorithm chosen (HMAC SHA256, for example). The formula looks like this: \`HMACSHA256(base64UrlEncode(header) + \\".\\" + base64UrlEncode(payload), secret)\`. The result is encoded in Base64URL and added to the token.\\n\\nThe checking of the integrity\\n\\nWhen the server receives a JWT, it:\\n\\n- Divides the token into three parts.\\n\\n- Takes the header and the payload (the first two parts).\\n\\n- Computes the signature anew, using the same algorithm and its own secret key.\\n\\n- Compares the computed signature with the signature from the token.\\n\\nIf an attacker tries to change the data in the header or the payload (to raise their own rights, for example), they will not be able to recompute the signature correctly without a knowledge of the secret key. The server will discover the mismatch and reject the token.\\n\\nAn example of the creation and the checking of a JWT\\n\\nWhere it is applied and what nuances there are\\n\\nJWTs are widely used for authentication in REST APIs, single-page applications (SPAs) and microservice architectures. It matters to remember:\\n\\n- The secret key has to be held securely on the server and never passed to the client.\\n\\n- For asymmetric encryption you can use a public/private key pair (the RS256 and ES256 algorithms).\\n\\n- A JWT ensures integrity but not confidentiality — the data in the token is encoded but not encrypted (unless JWE is used).\\n\\n- The tokens have to be passed over HTTPS for protection against interception.\\n\\nConclusion: A JWT cannot be forged without the secret key thanks to the cryptographic signature, which ties the token's contents to the key. This makes a JWT a reliable mechanism for the passing of verifiable claims between parties, especially in scenarios of stateless authentication.","// An example in Node.js with the use of the jsonwebtoken library\\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\n\\n// The secret key, known only to the server\\nconst SECRET_KEY = 'my-super-secret-key';\\n\\n// The creation of the token\\nconst payload = { userId: 123, role: 'user' };\\nconst token = jwt.sign(payload, SECRET_KEY, { algorithm: 'HS256' });\\nconsole.log('The JWT created:', token);\\n\\n// The checking of the token\\njwt.verify(token, SECRET_KEY, (err, decoded) => {\\n  if (err) {\\n    console.log('The token is invalid:', err.message);\\n  } else {\\n    console.log('The token data:', decoded); // { userId: 123, role: 'user' }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// An attempt at forgery: the change of the payload without the key\\nconst fakeToken = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ1c2VySWQiOjEyMywicm9sZSI6ImFkbWluIn0.INVALID_SIGNATURE';\\n// The check will show an error, since the signature does not coincide"],[1440,"Collisions arise when different keys are hashed into one index. The main methods of resolution: the method of chaining and open addressing. In the method of chaining every slot of the table contains a linked list of the elements with the same hash. With open addressing the elements look for a free slot in the table itself by a particular algorithm — linear probing, for example. The choice of the method affects the performance under a high load.","Collisions are an unavoidable phenomenon in hash tables, when two different key-value pairs receive the same index after the application of the hash function. For the correct working of the data structure you have to have a strategy for the handling of such situations.\\nThe method of chaining (Separate Chaining)\\nIn this approach every slot (bucket) of the hash table does not hold one element but contains a data structure capable of holding several elements. Most often this is a linked list. On the occurrence of a collision the new element is simply added to the list of the corresponding slot.\\n\\n- The advantages: The simplicity of the implementation, the table never overflows (as long as there is memory for the lists), the deletion of elements is carried out easily.\\n- The disadvantages: It requires additional memory for the pointers, and the performance can degrade to O(n) if many keys land in one bucket.\\n\\nOpen addressing\\nWith this method every element is held directly in the table's array. If the target slot is occupied, the algorithm looks for the next free slot according to a particular sequence of probes.\\n\\n- Linear probing: The next slot is sought by the formula (hash(key) + i) % size, where i is the number of the attempt.\\n- Quadratic probing: The formula (hash(key) + c1*i + c2*i²) % size is used in order to reduce the clustering.\\n- Double hashing: For the computation of the step a second hash function is used: (hash1(key) + i * hash2(key)) % size.\\n\\nThe method of chaining is simpler and more reliable, and it is widely used in the standard libraries (in the dictionaries of Python up to version 3.6, for example). Open addressing can be more efficient in memory and in the processor's cache with a low load factor, but it requires careful handling of deletions (a \\"deleted\\" marker is often used) and is prone to clustering.\\nConclusion: The method of chaining is preferable when the number of elements is unknown in advance or deletions happen often. Open addressing with double hashing often gives better performance for static or rarely changed tables held in memory.","// A simplified example in Python\\nclass HashTableChaining:\\n    def __init__(self, size):\\n        self.size = size\\n        self.table = [[] for _ in range(size)]  # A list of lists\\n\\n    def insert(self, key, value):\\n        index = hash(key) % self.size\\n        bucket = self.table[index]\\n        # We check whether we are updating an existing key\\n        for i, (k, v) in enumerate(bucket):\\n            if k == key:\\n                bucket[i] = (key, value)\\n                return\\n        bucket.append((key, value))\\n\\n    def get(self, key):\\n        index = hash(key) % self.size\\n        for k, v in self.table[index]:\\n            if k == key:\\n                return v\\n        return None\\n\\n// An example of linear probing\\nclass HashTableOpenAddressing:\\n    def __init__(self, size):\\n        self.size = size\\n        self.table = [None] * size  # An array for (key, value) pairs or None\\n\\n    def insert(self, key, value):\\n        index = self._hash(key)\\n        for i in range(self.size):\\n            probe_index = (index + i) % self.size\\n            if self.table[probe_index] is None or self.table[probe_index][0] == key:\\n                self.table[probe_index] = (key, value)\\n                return\\n        raise Exception(\\"The hash table has overflowed\\")\\n\\n    def _hash(self, key):\\n        return hash(key) % self.size"],[1441,"Generators are functions that can be suspended and resumed using the yield keyword. That capability lets asynchronicity be implemented, since you can suspend the execution of a function, wait for an asynchronous operation to finish (a request to a server, for example) and then resume the execution with the result obtained. This is precisely how async/await works under the hood: they are converted into code with generators and promises, creating the illusion of the synchronous execution of asynchronous code. This makes code more readable and more manageable compared with chains of then.","Generators in JavaScript are a particular kind of function, declared through \`function*\`, which can suspend their execution by means of the \`yield\` keyword and resume it later. That pause does not block the main thread, which makes generators the ideal tool for the management of asynchronous operations. Instead of using callbacks or chains of promises, you can write code that looks synchronous but is carried out asynchronously.\\n\\nHow does this work with asynchronicity?\\n\\nImagine that you have an asynchronous task — the reading of a file, for example. Instead of passing a callback, the generator can \`yield\` a promise representing that task. The external \\"managing\\" code (often called a \\"runner\\" or a \\"coroutine\\") receives that promise, waits for it to be resolved and then resumes the generator, passing the result back through \`next(value)\`. In this way the generator is suspended for the duration of the asynchronous operation, but the thread of the execution is not blocked and other tasks can be carried out.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at a simple implementation of an asynchronous runner for generators:\\n\\nIn this example \`asyncRunner\` takes a generator function. Every time the generator yields a promise, the runner waits for it to be resolved and then resumes the generator with the result. The code inside the generator looks linear and synchronous, although in fact it is carried out asynchronously.\\n\\nThe connection with async/await\\n\\nThe \`async\`/\`await\` keywords, introduced in ES2017, are syntactic sugar over generators and promises. Under the hood the JavaScript engine converts an \`async\` function into a generator, and \`await\` into \`yield\`. This makes the code even cleaner, sparing you the need to write an explicit runner. For example, the previous code can be rewritten like this:\\n\\nThis works on the same principle: the function is suspended at every \`await\`, letting other tasks be carried out, and is then resumed with the result.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- The implementation of asynchronous streams of data (by means of libraries of the Redux-Saga kind, for example).\\n\\n- The creation of custom schedulers for complex asynchronous scenarios.\\n\\n- An understanding of the internal arrangement of the modern asynchronous constructs in JavaScript.\\n\\nConclusion: Generators and \`yield\` provide a low-level mechanism for the implementation of asynchronicity through the suspension and the resumption of the execution, which lies at the foundation of the more convenient \`async\`/\`await\` syntax. This approach is useful when you have to create custom asynchronous patterns or understand deeply how asynchronous code works in JavaScript.","function asyncRunner(generatorFunc) {\\n  const generator = generatorFunc();\\n  function handle(result) {\\n    if (result.done) return Promise.resolve(result.value);\\n    return Promise.resolve(result.value)\\n      .then(res => handle(generator.next(res)))\\n      .catch(err => handle(generator.throw(err)));\\n  }\\n  return handle(generator.next());\\n}\\n\\n// The use with a generator\\nasyncRunner(function* fetchData() {\\n  const response = yield fetch('https://api.example.com/data'); // the yield of a promise\\n  const data = yield response.json(); // the yield of another promise\\n  console.log(data);\\n  return data;\\n});\\n\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  console.log(data);\\n  return data;\\n}"],[1442,"The signature in a JWT is the third part of the token, which is created by the hashing of the first two parts (the header and the payload) using a secret key or a private key. It serves to check that the token's contents were not changed after it was issued. If somebody tries to change the data in the header or the payload, the signature will become invalid and the server will reject such a token. This is the main mechanism of a JWT's security, protecting against forgery.","A JSON Web Token (JWT) is a compact way of securely passing information between parties in the form of a JSON object. The signature is the critically important component that turns a JWT from a simple container of data into a protected token.\\n\\nHow the signature is formed\\n\\nA JWT consists of three parts separated by dots: Header.Payload.Signature. The signature is created on the basis of the first two parts.\\n\\n- The encoding: The Header and the Payload are encoded into Base64Url format.\\n\\n- The concatenation: Those two encoded strings are joined with a dot: \`encodedHeader + \\".\\" + encodedPayload\`.\\n\\n- The signing: A cryptographic algorithm (HMAC SHA256 or RSA SHA256, for example) is applied to that combined string together with the secret key (for symmetric algorithms) or the private key (for asymmetric ones). The result is also encoded in Base64Url, forming the third part of the token.\\n\\nThe algorithms of the signature\\n\\nIn a JWT's header the algorithm (\`alg\`) used for the signature is specified. The main types:\\n\\n- HMAC with SHA-256 (HS256): A symmetric algorithm. One and the same secret key is used for the creation and the checking of the signature. It is simple to use, but it requires the secure exchange of the secret between the parties.\\n\\n- RSA with SHA-256 (RS256): An asymmetric algorithm. The signature is created by means of the issuer's private key and checked by means of the corresponding public key. This is more secure for distributed systems, since the public key can be distributed freely.\\n\\nAn example of the code for the creation and the checking of a signature (Node.js)\\n\\nWhere and how it is applied\\n\\nA JWT's signature is the foundation for authentication and authorisation in modern web applications and APIs (REST, GraphQL). After a user's successful log-in the server creates a JWT with a signature and sends it to the client. The client then sends that token in the \`Authorization: Bearer <token>\` header on every subsequent request. The server, on receiving the token, checks the signature. If the check passes, the server trusts the data inside the token (the user's identifier, for example) and provides access to the protected resources. This spares the server the need to hold the session in memory or in a database.\\n\\nConclusion: The signature in a JWT is a cryptographic mechanism guaranteeing the integrity and the authenticity of the token's data. It is worth applying whenever you have to create a self-sufficient, stateless token for the passing of trusted information between a client and a server, especially in microservice architectures and single-page applications (SPAs).","// The use of the jsonwebtoken library\\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\n\\nconst secretKey = 'my-super-secret-key';\\nconst payload = { userId: 12345, role: 'admin' };\\n\\n// 1. THE CREATION OF A JWT WITH A SIGNATURE (HS256)\\nconst token = jwt.sign(payload, secretKey, { algorithm: 'HS256' });\\nconsole.log('The token created:', token);\\n// It looks like: xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz\\n\\n// 2. THE CHECKING OF THE SIGNATURE AND THE DECODING\\njwt.verify(token, secretKey, (err, decodedPayload) => {\\n    if (err) {\\n        // The signature is invalid! The token has been changed or the key is wrong.\\n        console.error('The token did not pass the check:', err.message);\\n    } else {\\n        // The signature is correct. The data can be trusted.\\n        console.log('The token data:', decodedPayload); // { userId: 12345, role: 'admin' }\\n    }\\n});\\n\\n// 3. THE FORGERY OF A TOKEN (AN EXAMPLE OF AN UNSUCCESSFUL ATTEMPT)\\n// If an attacker takes the token and changes the payload by hand,\\n// without knowing the secretKey they will not be able to recompute the signature correctly.\\n// The server, checking the new signature, will discover the mismatch at once."],[1443,"The 'Decorator' pattern lets you dynamically add new behaviour to objects without changing their source code. It wraps the original object in a decorator object, which has the same interface and delegates the calls to the object wrapped, adding its own logic before or after. This is useful when you have to extend a class's functionality but inheritance is not suitable because of an explosive growth of subclasses. The Decorator follows the open/closed principle: classes are open for extension but closed for modification.","The 'Decorator' pattern is a structural design pattern that lets you dynamically add new functionality to objects by wrapping them in special decorator objects. Instead of changing the original class (which violates the open/closed principle), you create a chain of wrappers, each of which adds its own behaviour.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nA decorator implements the same interface as the object being wrapped. On the call of a method the decorator carries out additional logic (before or after) and then delegates the call to the object wrapped. This lets you combine behaviour on the fly, creating flexible configurations.\\n\\nAn example in TypeScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- In UI libraries for the addition of styles or behaviour to components (border, padding, for example).\\n\\n- In streaming APIs (Java I/O, .NET Streams) for the addition of filters (buffering, compression).\\n\\n- In the middleware of web frameworks (Express.js, NestJS) for the addition of logging and authentication.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the 'Decorator' pattern when you have to add responsibilities to objects flexibly at run time, avoiding a rigid hierarchy of inheritance and keeping the code clean and extensible.","// The base interface of the component\\ninterface Coffee {\\n    cost(): number;\\n    description(): string;\\n}\\n\\n// A concrete component\\nclass SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {\\n    cost() { return 5; }\\n    description() { return 'Simple coffee'; }\\n}\\n\\n// The base decorator\\nabstract class CoffeeDecorator implements Coffee {\\n    constructor(protected coffee: Coffee) {}\\n    cost() { return this.coffee.cost(); }\\n    description() { return this.coffee.description(); }\\n}\\n\\n// The concrete decorators\\nclass MilkDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 2; }\\n    description() { return super.description() + ', milk'; }\\n}\\n\\nclass SugarDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 1; }\\n    description() { return super.description() + ', sugar'; }\\n}\\n\\n// The use\\nlet myCoffee: Coffee = new SimpleCoffee();\\nmyCoffee = new MilkDecorator(myCoffee);\\nmyCoffee = new SugarDecorator(myCoffee);\\nconsole.log(myCoffee.description()); // Simple coffee, milk, sugar\\nconsole.log(myCoffee.cost()); // 8"],[1444,"A static array has a fixed size, which is determined at the stage of the compilation and cannot be changed during the program's execution. A dynamic array (a vector in C++ or a list in Python, for example) can change its size in the course of the program's working, allocating and freeing memory as it is needed. The use of static arrays is simpler and faster but less flexible, whereas dynamic arrays are convenient when the size of the data is unknown in advance.","Arrays are a fundamental data structure, and an understanding of the differences between static and dynamic arrays is critical for the choice of the right tool depending on the program's requirements.\\nA static array\\nA static array allocates memory on the stack or in the static area of memory at the moment of the compilation. Its size has to be known in advance and is a constant. This means that you cannot add or delete elements beyond that limit at run time.\\n\\nThe advantages: fast access to the elements (by index), the absence of the overheads of the management of memory at run time. The disadvantages: inflexibility, the risk of overflow or of under-filling.\\nA dynamic array\\nA dynamic array allocates memory in the heap during the program's execution. Its size can be changed: enlarged on the addition of elements and reduced on their deletion. In different languages this is implemented differently: \`std::vector\` in C++, \`ArrayList\` in Java, an ordinary list in Python.\\n\\nInside, a dynamic array usually manages a buffer: when there is not enough space a new, larger block of memory is allocated and the elements are copied. This creates certain overheads, but it ensures flexibility.\\nThe key differences and the application\\n\\n- The time of the determination of the size: static at the stage of the compilation, dynamic at run time.\\n- The place of the storage: static usually on the stack or in static memory, dynamic in the heap.\\n- The flexibility: a dynamic array lets you adapt to a changing volume of data.\\n- The performance: a static array is often faster because of the predictability of the placement in memory.\\nConclusion: use static arrays when the size of the data is precisely known and does not change (the days of the week or the size of a chessboard, for example). Dynamic arrays are preferable in most other cases, especially when the number of elements is determined by the user's input or by the results of computations.","// An example in C++\\nint staticArray[10]; // An array of a fixed size of 10 whole numbers\\n// staticArray[10] = 5; // An error: going beyond the array's bounds\\n\\n// An example in C++ with the use of std::vector\\n#include <vector>\\nstd::vector<int> dynamicArray; // The initial size is 0\\ndynamicArray.push_back(10); // We add an element, the size increases\\ndynamicArray.push_back(20); // Now the size = 2\\n// You can carry on adding elements"],[1445,"The Open/Closed Principle (OCP) states that software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension but closed for modification. This means that you can add new behaviour without changing the existing code. For example, instead of adding conditions to an old method, you create a new class implementing a common interface. Such an approach lowers the risk of introducing errors into an already working system and simplifies the addition of new functions.","The Open/Closed Principle (OCP) is the second principle of the SOLID set, which is aimed at the creation of systems robust to changes. Its main idea consists in the fact that a module's behaviour can be extended without bringing changes into its source code. This is achieved through design with the use of abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes) and the delegation of the concrete implementation to derived classes.\\n\\nThe problem without the OCP\\n\\nLet us look at a typical example: a system that computes the area of various shapes. Without following the OCP the code may look like a large conditional statement, which has to be modified every time a new shape is added.\\n\\nThe solution with the use of the OCP\\n\\nTo follow the OCP we define an abstraction (an interface or an abstract class) for a shape with a \`calculateArea()\` method. Every concrete shape implements that interface itself.\\n\\nWhere and how it is applied\\n\\nThe OCP is widely applied in the architecture of plugins, in middleware chains (in Express.js or Nest.js, for example), in strategies (the Strategy pattern) and in any systems in which the frequent addition of new functionality is required. It lies at the foundation of many frameworks, which let you extend their core through user modules without touching the framework's source code.\\n\\nSummary: The Open/Closed Principle is worth applying in projects in which active development and the addition of new functions are expected. It helps you lower the coupling of the code, minimises the risk of regression errors and makes a system more flexible and more convenient to test.","class AreaCalculator {\\n    calculateArea(shape) {\\n        if (shape.type === 'circle') {\\n            return Math.PI * shape.radius * shape.radius;\\n        } else if (shape.type === 'square') {\\n            return shape.side * shape.side;\\n        }\\n        // The addition of a new type ('triangle', for example) requires a change in this method!\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The abstraction (open for extension)\\ninterface Shape {\\n    calculateArea(): number;\\n}\\n\\n// The concrete implementations (new shapes are added as new classes)\\nclass Circle implements Shape {\\n    constructor(private radius: number) {}\\n    calculateArea() { return Math.PI * this.radius * this.radius; }\\n}\\n\\nclass Square implements Shape {\\n    constructor(private side: number) {}\\n    calculateArea() { return this.side * this.side; }\\n}\\n\\n// The calculator class is now closed for modification\\nclass AreaCalculator {\\n    calculateArea(shape: Shape) {\\n        return shape.calculateArea(); // It does not depend on the shape's concrete type\\n    }\\n}"],[1446,"A dynamic array internally holds the data in an ordinary array of a fixed size (the capacity). When there is not enough space on the addition of an element, the operation of extension (a resize) takes place. A new array of a larger size is usually allocated (often 1.5 or 2 times larger), every existing element is copied into it, and the old array is freed. This lets you have a convenient interface of a list with the automatic management of memory.","A dynamic array is a data structure that provides the interface of a list (addition, deletion, access by index) but internally uses an ordinary array of a fixed size. The key idea is to hide the manual management of memory from the user, automatically enlarging the internal buffer when it is needed.\\n\\nHow the extension works\\n\\nOn the creation of a dynamic array an initial buffer is allocated (for 10 elements, for example). While there is free space, new elements are added to the end in O(1). When the array fills up, the following takes place:\\n\\n- A new array of a larger size is allocated. The growth factor is often equal to 2 (a doubling) or 1.5 (as in Java's ArrayList).\\n\\n- Every element from the old array is copied into the new one.\\n\\n- The old array is freed (the collection of garbage or a manual freeing).\\n\\n- The element being added is placed in the new array, and the pointer to the internal buffer begins to refer to the new array.\\n\\nThe amortised complexity\\n\\nThe operation of extension is expensive — O(n), since it requires the copying of every element. If, however, you extend the array in a geometric progression (multiplying the size), then such expensive operations happen rarely. On average (amortised) the cost of the addition of one element remains O(1), which is a great advantage.\\n\\nAn example in Python (a simplified implementation)\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nDynamic arrays are the foundation of many standard collections: \`ArrayList\` in Java, \`vector\` in C++, \`list\` in Python (although there the implementation is a little different), \`List<T>\` in .NET. They are ideal when you need frequent access by index and sequential addition to the end, but the number of elements is unknown in advance.\\n\\nConclusion: An understanding of the mechanism of the extension helps you write efficient code, avoiding frequent resizes (by setting the initial capacity if the approximate number of elements is known, for example) and assessing the amortised complexity of the operations correctly.","class DynamicArray:\\n    def __init__(self, initial_capacity=4):\\n        self.capacity = initial_capacity\\n        self.size = 0\\n        self.data = [None] * self.capacity\\n\\n    def append(self, value):\\n        # If there is no space, we extend it\\n        if self.size == self.capacity:\\n            self._resize()\\n        self.data[self.size] = value\\n        self.size += 1\\n\\n    def _resize(self):\\n        # We increase the capacity by a factor of 2\\n        self.capacity *= 2\\n        new_data = [None] * self.capacity\\n        # We copy the old elements\\n        for i in range(self.size):\\n            new_data[i] = self.data[i]\\n        self.data = new_data\\n        print(f\\"The array has been extended to {self.capacity} elements\\")"],[1447,"Atomicity is a property of an operation meaning that it is carried out as a single whole: either completely, or it is not carried out at all. In multithreaded programming an atomic operation cannot be interrupted by another thread, which prevents a race condition. For example, the increment of a variable in Java by means of AtomicInteger is atomic. In databases atomicity is the first letter of ACID, guaranteeing that a transaction is either committed in its entirety or rolled back.","Atomicity is a fundamental concept in programming, especially in the areas connected with parallelism and the management of data. It guarantees that a particular operation or group of operations is carried out as an indivisible unit of work.\\n\\nAtomicity in multithreaded programming\\n\\nIn an environment with several threads of execution an atomic operation is an operation that finishes in one step from the point of view of the other threads. This means that no other thread can see the intermediate state of such an operation. Without atomicity race conditions arise. For example, the simple operation of an increment (\`i++\`) in fact consists of three steps: the reading of the value, the increase, the writing. If two threads carry it out simultaneously, the result may be incorrect.\\n\\nAtomicity in databases (ACID)\\n\\nIn the context of databases atomicity is the first component of the ACID acronym, describing the properties of transactions. The atomicity of a transaction guarantees that every operation inside it will be carried out as a single whole. If any part of the transaction fails, the whole transaction is rolled back and the database returns to the state that was there before it began. This is critically important for financial operations or for any update affecting several records.\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- Multithreaded data structures: The use of atomic types (AtomicInteger, AtomicReference) or synchronized blocks in Java, std::atomic in C++.\\n\\n- The handling of transactions: Transfers of money, the updating of inventory, in which several changes have to be consistent.\\n\\n- Operating systems: Atomic system calls for work with files or semaphores.\\n\\nConclusion: The atomicity of operations should be applied wherever the simultaneous access to shared data from several threads or processes is possible, in order to guarantee the correctness and the integrity of the data. This is a key concept for the writing of reliable multithreaded code and the design of robust systems working with transactions.","// A non-atomic increment in Java (a potential race condition)\\npublic class Counter {\\n    private int value = 0;\\n    public void increment() { value++; } // NOT atomic!\\n}\\n\\n// An atomic increment with the use of AtomicInteger\\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\\npublic class SafeCounter {\\n    private AtomicInteger value = new AtomicInteger(0);\\n    public void increment() { value.incrementAndGet(); } // Atomic\\n}\\n\\n-- An example of an SQL transaction with atomicity\\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE user_id = 1;\\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE user_id = 2;\\n    -- If an error takes place here, both UPDATEs will be rolled back\\nCOMMIT;"],[1448,"Type safety is a property of a programming language that guarantees that operations are carried out only on data of permissible types. This prevents errors such as an attempt to add a number to a string. In languages with strict static typing (TypeScript or Java, for example) these checks take place at the stage of the compilation, which helps you find bugs earlier. In dynamic languages (JavaScript, for example) many checks are postponed until the program's execution, which can lead to errors at run time.","Type safety is a fundamental concept in programming, which means that the language will not let an operation be carried out on a value if that operation is not defined for its data type. The main goal is to prevent a whole class of errors arising from the unexpected behaviour of data — an attempt to call a method that does not exist on an object, for example, or the carrying out of an arithmetic operation on a string.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nThe language's type system controls the correspondence of the types. This can be done at different stages:\\n\\n- Static type checking: The analysis takes place before the program is run, at the stage of the compilation or the linting. The examples: TypeScript, Java, C#.\\n\\n- Dynamic type checking: The checks are carried out during the program's working (at run time). The examples: Python, JavaScript, Ruby.\\n\\nStatically typed languages often ensure a higher level of type safety, since many errors are caught before the code is even run.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at the difference between JavaScript (dynamic typing) and TypeScript (static typing).\\n\\nWhere it is applied and what it is needed for\\n\\nType safety is critically important in large projects and teams, because it:\\n\\n- Serves as a form of documentation, making the contracts of functions explicit.\\n\\n- Lets tools (the IDE) provide intelligent autocompletion and navigation through the code.\\n\\n- Substantially reduces the quantity of errors connected with typos or the wrong use of an API, which are hard to catch in dynamic languages.\\n\\n- Eases refactoring — the compiler helps you find every place that has to be updated.\\n\\nConclusion: Type safety is a powerful mechanism for raising the reliability and the maintainability of code. It is worth applying in any projects in which stability matters, especially in work in a team or in the long-term support of an application. The use of languages or supersets with static typing (TypeScript for the front end, languages of the Go or Rust kind for the back end) is an investment in the quality of the code, which pays off in the reduction of the time spent on debugging.","// JavaScript (there is no check at the stage of the writing)\\nfunction calculateTotal(price, quantity) {\\n    return price * quantity;\\n}\\n// This will raise an error only at run time, if a string is passed\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\\"10\\", 5)); // \\"10\\" * 5 = 50 (an implicit conversion, which may be unobvious)\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\\"ten\\", 5)); // NaN (an error at run time)\\n\\n// TypeScript (static type checking)\\nfunction calculateTotal(price: number, quantity: number): number {\\n    return price * quantity;\\n}\\n// The TypeScript compiler will give an error at once, not letting the code be compiled:\\n// calculateTotal(\\"10\\", 5); // An argument of the type 'string' cannot be assigned to a parameter of the type 'number'.\\n// The correct call:\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(10, 5)); // 50"],[1449,"MVC divides an application into three components with clear responsibilities. The Model manages the data and the business logic. The View is responsible for the display of the information to the user. The Controller handles the user's input, updating the Model and the View. This simplifies the testing, lets the interface and the logic be changed independently, and also helps you distribute the work in a team.","The Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern is an architectural approach that divides an application into three interconnected components. The main goal is to separate the internal representation of the data (the Model) from the way it is displayed (the View) and from the logic of the handling of the user's actions (the Controller). This separation of responsibilities makes code better organised and more manageable.\\nThe components of MVC\\n\\n- The Model: It contains the application's business logic and data. It knows nothing of the existence of the View or the Controller. The Model notifies the observers (often the View) of changes in the data.\\n- The View: It is responsible for the representation of the data to the user. The View obtains the data from the Model and displays it. It is usually passive and contains no complex logic.\\n- The Controller: It takes the user's input (clicks, presses of keys, for example). It interprets those actions, updates the Model and, when it is necessary, chooses the suitable View for the display.\\nThe practical application and an example\\nMVC is widely used in web frameworks (Ruby on Rails, Django, Spring MVC) and in desktop applications. Let us look at a simple example in JavaScript for the management of a list of tasks.\\n\\nIn this example the Model holds the array of tasks and notifies the View through the Controller on changes. The View paints the list and passes the user's actions (a click on the button) to the Controller. The Controller handles the action, validates the input and updates the Model.\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Maintainability: A change of the interface (the View) does not affect the business logic (the Model).\\n- Testability: The components can be tested in isolation. The Model is easy to test without a UI.\\n- Reuse: One Model can be used with different Views (a web page, a mobile application).\\n- Parallel development: Different developers can work on the Model, the View and the Controller at the same time.\\nConclusion: MVC is worth applying in the creation of complex applications with a user interface, in which the separation of responsibility, ease of testing and the long-term maintainability of the code matter. It is especially useful in team development and in projects that will be scaled.","// Model\\nclass TaskModel {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.tasks = [];\\n    this.listeners = [];\\n  }\\n  addTask(text) {\\n    this.tasks.push({ text, completed: false });\\n    this.notify();\\n  }\\n  // ... other methods for work with the data\\n  notify() { this.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(this.tasks)); }\\n}\\n\\n// View\\nclass TaskView {\\n  constructor(controller) {\\n    this.controller = controller;\\n    this.listElement = document.getElementById('taskList');\\n    this.inputElement = document.getElementById('taskInput');\\n    this.buttonElement = document.getElementById('addButton');\\n    this.buttonElement.addEventListener('click', () => {\\n      this.controller.addTask(this.inputElement.value);\\n    });\\n  }\\n  render(tasks) {\\n    this.listElement.innerHTML = tasks.map(t => \`\\n      <li>\${t.text} - \${t.completed ? 'Done' : 'Pending'}</li>\\n    \`).join('');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Controller\\nclass TaskController {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n    this.model.listeners.push((tasks) => this.view.render(tasks));\\n  }\\n  addTask(text) {\\n    if (text.trim()) {\\n      this.model.addTask(text);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The initialisation\\nconst model = new TaskModel();\\nconst controller = new TaskController(model);\\nconst view = new TaskView(controller);"],[1450,"The browser does not let you pass custom headers (such as Authorization) directly in the downloading of a file by an ordinary link or through window.location, because that action is initiated by the user rather than by a script. Browsers strictly control the headers that can be set in such requests, in order to prevent the leaking of sensitive data or the forging of requests. To pass headers you have to use programmatic requests — through the Fetch API or XMLHttpRequest, for example, in which you completely control the HTTP request.","When the user clicks on an ordinary link, or you use \`window.location.href\` for a navigation, the browser creates a simple GET request, into which it itself adds the standard headers (User-Agent, Accept and so on). It is impossible to add arbitrary headers, such as \`Authorization: Bearer <token>\`, to that request, because that would violate the browser's model of security.\\n\\nWhy does the browser forbid this?\\n\\n- Security: If any page could add headers to any requests (navigations to other sites included), that would let attackers forge requests with the user's credentials.\\n\\n- The control of the origin: The browser follows the Same-Origin Policy and CORS. Headers such as Authorization are considered \\"headers requiring a preflight check\\", and their sending in a cross-domain context requires the server's explicit permission.\\n\\n- The separation of responsibility: Ordinary navigational requests are governed by the browser on the user's behalf, while programmatic requests (through an API) are governed by the page's code.\\n\\nHow do you correctly download a file with an Authorization header?\\n\\nYou have to use JavaScript to create a programmatic request, obtain the data as a Blob, and then create a link for the download.\\n\\nThis method is safe, because the headers are controlled in the context of the current page and obey the rules of CORS.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\nSuch an approach is necessary in modern SPA applications (React, Angular, Vue), in which the authentication takes place through JWT tokens and the API requires the token to be passed in the Authorization header for access to protected resources, files included.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the Fetch API (or its analogues) for the downloading of files that require authorisation, since only programmatic requests let you completely control the HTTP headers while observing the browser's policies of security.","async function downloadFileWithAuth(url, token) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url, {\\n    headers: {\\n      'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`\\n    }\\n  });\\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Download failed');\\n  const blob = await response.blob();\\n  const downloadUrl = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);\\n  const link = document.createElement('a');\\n  link.href = downloadUrl;\\n  link.download = 'file.pdf'; // the name of the file\\n  document.body.appendChild(link);\\n  link.click();\\n  document.body.removeChild(link);\\n  window.URL.revokeObjectURL(downloadUrl);\\n}"],[1451,"The main approach is the use of a WebView, which displays web content inside a native application. For the interaction between the JavaScript in the WebView and the native code, bridges are used — through the methods evaluateJavascript on Android and stringByEvaluatingJavaScriptFromString on iOS, for example. Custom URL schemes are also applied, which let JavaScript initiate native actions, and the postMessage mechanism for the exchange of messages. These methods let the web part call native functions (the camera, geolocation) and vice versa.","The interaction between a front end (usually HTML/JS loaded into a WebView) and a native application (Android/iOS) is a key task for hybrid applications, progressive web applications (PWAs) or simply the embedding of web content. The main component is the WebView, which acts as a mini-browser inside the application.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- A WebView with a JavaScript bridge: The native side provides an interface (a bridge), which is injected into the global JavaScript context. JS can call the methods of that bridge, which carry out native code.\\n- A custom URL scheme / deep linking: JavaScript can initiate a navigation by a special URL scheme (\`myapp://action?data=value\`, for example), which the native application intercepts and carries out the corresponding action.\\n- The postMessage API: Modern WebViews support channels of messages between the native context and the web context, letting data be exchanged safely.\\n- The React Native / Flutter bridge: In cross-platform frameworks there are built-in bridges for the calling of native modules from JavaScript.\\nAn example of the implementation of a simple bridge on Android\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an interaction is used in hybrid applications (Cordova/Ionic), when part of the logic is implemented in web technologies and the access to the hardware functions is through native code. Also in applications that dynamically load content from a server (news applications with web articles, for example) but require native navigation or payments.\\nConclusion: Use a WebView with a bridge when you have to develop a cross-platform application with updatable web content quickly, but with access to the native APIs. For a closer integration and better performance, consider React Native or native development.","// The native part (Kotlin/Android)\\nclass JsBridge(private val webView: WebView) {\\n    @JavascriptInterface\\n    fun showToast(message: String) {\\n        Toast.makeText(webView.context, message, Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// In the Activity, configuring the WebView:\\nwebView.settings.javaScriptEnabled = true\\nwebView.addJavascriptInterface(JsBridge(webView), \\"AndroidBridge\\")\\n\\n// The JavaScript part in the WebView\\nfunction callNativeToast() {\\n    // We call the native method through the injected object\\n    if (window.AndroidBridge) {\\n        window.AndroidBridge.showToast(\\"Hello from JS!\\");\\n    }\\n}"],[1452,"A WebView is a component for the display of web content inside a native application, while a browser is an independent application. The main difference in the handling of events such as resize consists in the fact that a WebView may not generate the standard events of a browser on a change of the size if that change was initiated by native code. In a browser the resize event fires on a change of the window's dimensions, while in a WebView that depends on the platform's implementation and may require the manual call of JavaScript. A WebView may also have limitations on timers and animation in a minimised state, which affects the interface's responsiveness.","A WebView is a built-in component in mobile operating systems (Android, iOS), which lets you display web content inside a native application. Unlike a full browser, a WebView works in the context of the application and has a number of limitations and particulars in the handling of events.\\nThe key differences in the handling of events\\n\\n- The resize event: In a browser the \`resize\` event of the \`window\` object fires on a change of the dimensions of the browser's window. In a WebView that event may not be generated automatically if the change of the size takes place programmatically from the side of the native code (on the rotation of the device or a change of the layout, for example). The developer often has to call JavaScript code by hand in order to simulate the event or to recompute the layout.\\n- The lifecycle: A WebView may suspend the execution of JavaScript and the handling of events when the application is minimised or the WebView is not visible. A browser usually carries on working in the background, although with limitations.\\n- The access to APIs: A WebView may have limited access to some Web APIs (geolocation or the camera, for example) compared with a browser, which affects the events connected with those APIs.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at the situation in which you have to handle a change of the device's orientation in a hybrid application. In a browser you can simply listen to the \`resize\` event. In a WebView on Android that may not work, and an interaction with the native code will be required.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nAn understanding of these differences is critical in the development of hybrid applications with the use of frameworks of the Ionic, Capacitor or React Native kind (in which a WebView is used). It affects the correctness of the display, the handling of the user's input and the performance.\\nConclusion: A WebView requires particular attention to events, especially resize, since their behaviour may differ from a browser's. In the development of hybrid applications you have to test the handling of events on the target platforms and possibly implement bridges between the native code and the JavaScript for the correct working.","// The JavaScript code in the WebView\\n// This handler may not fire on a programmatic change of the WebView's size\\nwindow.addEventListener('resize', function() {\\n    console.log('The size has changed (it may not be logged in a WebView)');\\n    adjustLayout();\\n});\\n\\n// The alternative: use an event from the native code\\n// The native part (Java/Kotlin for Android) has to call:\\n// webView.evaluateJavascript(\\"onNativeResize()\\", null);\\nfunction onNativeResize() {\\n    console.log('The size has been changed from the native code');\\n    adjustLayout();\\n}"],[1453,"JavaScript in a browser has no direct access to the user's file system, for reasons of security. This prevents the reading or the writing of files without the user's explicit permission. For work with files special APIs are used, such as the File System Access API, which require an interaction with the user (through a dialogue for the choice of a file, for example). You can also use limited storage (IndexedDB, for example) for the application's data, but not for arbitrary files on the disk.","Browsers carry JavaScript out in a strict sandbox, which isolates web pages from the operating system and from the user's file system. This is a fundamental principle of security, preventing malicious sites from stealing or damaging files.\\n\\nThe main limitations\\n\\n- There is no direct access to the paths of files: JavaScript cannot read or write files at arbitrary paths (\`C:/Users/...\`, for example).\\n\\n- An explicit action of the user is required: Work with files is possible only after an interaction with the user — through an \`<input type=\\"file\\">\` element or the call of a dialogue for the choice of a file, for example.\\n\\n- Access only to the files chosen: Even after the choice, the script obtains access only to the specific files chosen rather than to the whole directory.\\n\\n- Limited permanent storage: For the saving of data between sessions APIs of the IndexedDB or localStorage kind are used, but they have quotas (usually several megabytes) and provide no access to the real file system.\\n\\nThe modern APIs for work with files\\n\\nWith the appearance of the File System Access API (previously known as the Native File System API) developers obtained the ability to request the user's permission for access to specific files or directories and to preserve that permission for subsequent sessions.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nThe limited access to the file system is used in every web application that works with files: online editors (textual, graphical), tools for the handling of data, IDEs in the browser (CodeSandbox or StackBlitz, for example). The secure model lets users control which files a site obtains access to.\\n\\nConclusion: The limitations of the access to the file system are a key element of a browser's security, protecting users. For legitimate work with files you should use the modern APIs, which request explicit permission and provide controlled access only to the resources chosen.","// An example of a request for the choice of a file\\nasync function pickFile() {\\n  try {\\n    // We open the dialogue for the choice of a file\\n    [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker();\\n    // We obtain the file itself\\n    const file = await fileHandle.getFile();\\n    const contents = await file.text();\\n    console.log(contents);\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.error('The user cancelled the choice or an error took place:', err);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of the writing to a file (permission to write is required)\\nasync function saveFile(contents) {\\n  const options = {\\n    types: [{\\n      description: 'Text files',\\n      accept: {'text/plain': ['.txt']},\\n    }],\\n  };\\n  const handle = await window.showSaveFilePicker(options);\\n  const writable = await handle.createWritable();\\n  await writable.write(contents);\\n  await writable.close();\\n}"],[1454,"The parallel upload of a file in parts (a chunked upload) splits a large file into several smaller fragments (chunks). Those fragments are then sent to the server simultaneously by means of several HTTP requests (through Promise.all, for example). The server receives the parts, saves them temporarily, and once it has received all of them gathers them into the original file. This approach speeds the upload up, especially with an unstable connection, and lets an interrupted transfer be resumed.","The parallel upload of a file in parts is a technique in which a large file is divided into several independent fragments (chunks), which are uploaded to the server simultaneously. This lets you get round the limitations on the size of a single HTTP request, increase the speed of the upload through the use of the channel's full bandwidth, and ensure the ability to resume the transfer on failures.\\nThe main steps of the implementation\\n\\n- The splitting of the file on the client: Using the File API (Blob.slice), the file is divided into parts of a fixed size (of 5 MB each, for example).\\n- The parallel sending: For every part a separate HTTP request is created (usually a PUT or a POST). Those requests are carried out in parallel — by means of Promise.all in JavaScript, for example.\\n- The identification of the parts on the server: Every part is sent with a unique identifier of the upload (an uploadId) and the number of the part (a chunkIndex). The server saves the parts in temporary storage.\\n- The assembly of the file on the server: After the successful upload of every part the client sends a final request, and the server combines all of the parts in the correct order into the resulting file.\\nAn example of the code in JavaScript (the client-side part)\\n\\nOn the server side (on Node.js with Express, for example) you have to implement two endpoints: one for the receiving of the individual parts, the other for their combination. The parts can be temporarily saved in the file system or in object storage (S3, for example, which supports Multipart Upload out of the box).\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis approach is widely used in cloud storage (Google Drive, Dropbox), in streaming platforms for the uploading of video, and in enterprise applications for the transfer of large volumes of data. It is especially useful in conditions of an unstable internet, since it lets you resume the upload of only the parts that failed rather than of the whole file in its entirety.\\nConclusion: The parallel upload in parts is worth applying in work with large files (from tens of megabytes), when the speed of the upload, the fault tolerance and the ability to resume the transfer matter.","async function uploadFileInParallel(file, chunkSize = 5 * 1024 * 1024) {\\n    const uploadId = generateUniqueId();\\n    const totalChunks = Math.ceil(file.size / chunkSize);\\n    const promises = [];\\n\\n    for (let i = 0; i < totalChunks; i++) {\\n        const start = i * chunkSize;\\n        const end = Math.min(start + chunkSize, file.size);\\n        const chunk = file.slice(start, end);\\n\\n        const formData = new FormData();\\n        formData.append('file', chunk);\\n        formData.append('uploadId', uploadId);\\n        formData.append('chunkIndex', i);\\n        formData.append('totalChunks', totalChunks);\\n\\n        // We send every chunk in a separate request\\n        const promise = fetch('/api/upload-chunk', {\\n            method: 'POST',\\n            body: formData\\n        }).then(res => res.json());\\n        promises.push(promise);\\n    }\\n\\n    // The parallel execution of every request for the upload of the chunks\\n    await Promise.all(promises);\\n    // The final request for the assembly of the file\\n    await fetch(\`/api/complete-upload?uploadId=\${uploadId}\`, { method: 'POST' });\\n    console.log('File uploaded successfully!');\\n}"],[1455,"The streaming loading of data in JavaScript lets you handle information in parts (chunks) as it arrives rather than waiting for the complete loading. This is implemented through the interfaces of the Streams API, such as ReadableStream, which can be obtained from a fetch request. For example, in the downloading of a large file you can begin its display or saving at once, saving memory and improving the application's responsiveness. Streams are especially useful for the streaming of video and for the handling of large JSON or CSV files.","The streaming loading of data in JavaScript is a mechanism that lets you receive and handle data in small fragments (chunks) as they arrive over the network or from another source. Instead of waiting until the whole file or response has loaded completely into memory, the application can begin work with the very first portion of the data. This substantially lowers the consumption of memory and reduces the delay before the first display of the content, which is critical for work with large volumes of information, such as video, audio, enormous JSON files or the results of an API.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nThe foundation is the Streams API, which provides interfaces for the creation, the composition and the consumption of streams of data. The key components:\\n\\n- ReadableStream: it represents a source of data from which chunks can be read.\\n\\n- WritableStream: it represents a sink into which chunks can be written.\\n\\n- TransformStream: it lets you transform data on the fly.\\n\\nMost often a streaming source is obtained from the Fetch API. When you make a fetch request, the body of the response (response.body) is a ReadableStream by default for many types of content.\\n\\nAn example of the use with the Fetch API\\n\\nSuppose we are loading a large text file and want to display its contents as it arrives.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The streaming of media: video and audio players load and play the data in parts.\\n\\n- The handling of large files: the loading and the parsing of enormous JSON, CSV or log files without the consumption of all of the memory.\\n\\n- The progressive loading of content: web pages can display content as it arrives, improving the perception.\\n\\n- Server-side rendering (SSR): frameworks such as Next.js can stream the HTML to the client.\\n\\n- Real time: the receiving of continuous data from a server through Server-Sent Events (SSE) or WebSockets.\\n\\nConclusion: The streaming loading is worth applying when you work with large volumes of data, in which the efficiency of the use of memory and the interface's responsiveness matter. This is an indispensable tool for modern web applications that require the handling of streaming content or work with files whose size may exceed the memory available.","async function streamTextFile(url) {\\n  // We carry out the request\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  // We obtain the body of the response as a ReadableStream\\n  const reader = response.body.getReader();\\n  const decoder = new TextDecoder('utf-8');\\n  let result = '';\\n\\n  try {\\n    while (true) {\\n      // We read the next chunk\\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read();\\n      if (done) break; // If the stream has finished, we exit\\n      // We decode the bytes into text and add them to the result\\n      result += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });\\n      // The chunk can be handled at once - the UI can be updated, for example\\n      console.log('A chunk has been received, the current length:', result.length);\\n    }\\n  } finally {\\n    reader.releaseLock(); // We release the reader's lock\\n  }\\n  console.log('The complete text has been loaded:', result);\\n}\\n\\n// The call of the function\\nstreamTextFile('https://example.com/large-file.txt');"],[1456,"The main way is the compilation of the C++ code into WebAssembly (Wasm) by means of tools of the Emscripten kind. The compiled Wasm module is loaded into the React application as an ordinary JavaScript module. The data is passed through the WebAssembly memory or by the call of the functions exported from the C++. For simple scenarios you can use Node.js Native Addons, if the application works in the Electron environment.","To obtain data from native C++ code in a React application that is carried out in a browser, the standard modern solution is the use of WebAssembly (Wasm). WebAssembly is a binary format of instructions, which lets code written in languages of the C/C++ kind be carried out with almost native performance straight in the browser.\\nThe main approach: the compilation into WebAssembly\\nThe Emscripten tool compiles C/C++ code into a .wasm module and generates JavaScript \\"glue\\" code, which ensures the loading and the interaction. In the React application you load that module and call its exported functions.\\nAn example of the steps and the code\\n1. Write the C++ code with the functions that have to be exported, using the Emscripten macros.\\n\\n2. Compile the code into WebAssembly by means of Emscripten.\\n\\nThis command will create the files \`native_lib.wasm\` and \`native_lib.js\`.\\n3. Integrate the module into a React component.\\n\\nThe alternative methods and the application\\n\\n- Node.js Native Addons + Electron: If your React application is packaged in Electron (a desktop application), you can create Native Addons in C++ and call them through the Node.js API. This gives direct access to the system's resources.\\n- A server-side proxy (Backend-for-Frontend): The C++ application works as a separate service (on localhost, for example). The React application sends HTTP/WebSocket requests to a server-side application (on Node.js/Python), which in its turn interacts with the C++ library through a Foreign Function Interface (FFI). This approach separates the native logic from the front end.\\nConclusion: Use the compilation of C++ into WebAssembly when you need high performance of computations (the handling of images or physical simulations, for example) straight in the browser. For desktop applications on the basis of Electron, Native Addons are preferable, since they provide full access to the system. A server-side proxy is suitable when the native code has to work on a separate server or requires access to resources not available from the browser.","// native_lib.cpp\\n#include <emscripten/emscripten.h>\\n\\n// The exported function, returning a number\\nEMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE\\nint getValueFromNativeLayer() {\\n    // Here there may be complex logic in C++\\n    return 42;\\n}\\n\\n// A function taking a parameter and returning a result\\nEMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE\\nint processData(int input) {\\n    return input * 2;\\n}\\n\\nemcc native_lib.cpp \\\\\\n     -o native_lib.js \\\\\\n     -s EXPORTED_FUNCTIONS=\\"['_getValueFromNativeLayer', '_processData']\\" \\\\\\n     -s MODULARIZE=1 -s EXPORT_ES6=1\\n\\nimport React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    const [nativeValue, setNativeValue] = useState(null);\\n\\n    useEffect(() => {\\n        // We dynamically import the module generated\\n        import('./native_lib.js').then(module => {\\n            // After the loading of the module we call the exported function\\n            const value = module._getValueFromNativeLayer();\\n            setNativeValue(value);\\n\\n            // An example of the call of a function with a parameter\\n            const processed = module._processData(10);\\n            console.log('Processed data:', processed);\\n        });\\n    }, []);\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <p>The value from the native layer (C++):</p>\\n            <p>{nativeValue !== null ? nativeValue : 'Loading...'}</p>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nexport default App;"],[1457,"On the ordinary call of the methods fetch().then(res => res.json()) or res.blob() the browser loads the whole response into memory before passing it into your code. If the file weighs gigabytes, it is placed in its entirety in the RAM, which can exhaust the resources available and lead to the fall of the tab or of the application. To avoid that you have to work with the response as with a stream, handling the data in parts as it arrives from the server, without accumulating all of it at once.","When you use \`fetch\` to obtain data, the browser by default buffers the whole body of the response in memory. This is convenient for small JSON responses or images, but it becomes a problem for large files, such as video, archives or dumps of databases.\\n\\nWhy does the overflow of memory take place?\\n\\nThe methods \`response.json()\`, \`response.text()\` and \`response.blob()\` read the whole body of the response and convert it into the corresponding format, holding the result completely in the JavaScript memory. If the file is 2 GB in size, then before the handling in your code the browser has to allocate at least 2 GB of RAM for that buffer. This may exceed the limits of memory available to a tab of the browser and lead to an \\"out of memory\\" error or simply to a freeze.\\n\\nThe solution: the use of the Streams API\\n\\nThe modern \`fetch\` API returns a \`Response\` object whose \`body\` property is a \`ReadableStream\`. That stream lets you read the data in chunks as it arrives from the server. You can handle every chunk and free the memory at once, without waiting for the loading of the whole file.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nHere is how you can download a large file and save it to the user's disk by means of streams, avoiding an overflow of memory:\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- The streaming of video or audio in web applications.\\n\\n- The loading and the preview of large logs or CSV files.\\n\\n- The gradual handling of data from a server (the progressive loading of JSON, for example).\\n\\n- The client-side handling of files that do not fit into memory in their entirety (encryption, hashing).\\n\\nConclusion: Use the Streams API through \`response.body\` in work with potentially large responses from a server, in order to handle the data in parts and avoid an overflow of memory. This is critically important for the creation of responsive web applications working with large volumes of data.","async function downloadLargeFile(url, filename) {\\n  // We request the resource\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  // We obtain the stream from the body of the response\\n  const readableStream = response.body;\\n  \\n  // We create a stream for the writing into a file through the File System Access API\\n  // (in real code a check of the browser's support is needed)\\n  const fileStream = await createWritableStream(filename);\\n  \\n  // We copy the data from the response into the file in a stream\\n  await readableStream.pipeTo(fileStream);\\n  \\n  console.log('The file has been saved!');\\n}\\n\\n// A simplified function for the demonstration (in reality use showSaveFilePicker)\\nasync function createWritableStream(filename) {\\n  // For the example: we create a Blob object and a link for the download\\n  // This is not the streaming saving to a disk but a demonstration of the handling of chunks.\\n  const chunks = [];\\n  const writer = {\\n    write(chunk) {\\n      // We handle every chunk of the data\\n      chunks.push(chunk);\\n      // Here you can, for example, send the chunk into a WebSocket\\n      // or compute a hash sum on the fly\\n      console.log(\`\${chunk.byteLength} bytes have been received\`);\\n    },\\n    close() {\\n      // At the end we gather the file (for the example; in reality there is no need to do this)\\n      const blob = new Blob(chunks);\\n      const link = document.createElement('a');\\n      link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\\n      link.download = filename;\\n      link.click();\\n    }\\n  };\\n  return writer;\\n}"],[1458,"One-time tokens for the downloading of files can be intercepted and reused if they are passed in the URL. If a token has no term of validity, the link becomes eternal. It is also hard to guarantee that a token has been used only once, especially on failures of the network. These problems can lead to the leaking of confidential files.","One-time tokens (or one-time links) are often used to provide temporary access to protected files — in the downloading of reports or personal documents, for example. Although this concept seems simple, its implementation involves a number of technical and architectural problems that can compromise a system's security.\\n\\nThe main problems of security\\n\\n- The interception of the token: If the token is passed as a parameter of the URL (\`https://example.com/download?token=abc123\`, for example), it can be intercepted through the web server's logs, the browser's history or means of the monitoring of the network. Having obtained the token, an attacker can obtain access to the file.\\n\\n- Reuse (a replay attack): The main idea of \\"one-time-ness\\" is often violated. If the server does not track the state of the use of the token strictly (by marking it as used right after the first successful request, for example), one and the same token can be used many times.\\n\\n- The absence of a term of validity: A token without a temporal limitation (a TTL) creates an \\"eternal\\" link, which remains active indefinitely, increasing the window for an attack.\\n\\nThe practical difficulties of the implementation\\n\\nIt is hard to guarantee that a token has been used exactly once in distributed systems. Let us look at a scenario: the user begins a download, but the connection breaks. The server may not know whether the file was completely transferred and whether the token should be considered used. The rigid invalidation of the token after the first request can lead to errors for legitimate users.\\n\\nAn example of code with a basic (vulnerable) implementation\\n\\nIn this example the token can be used an unlimited number of times, and it never expires. A more reliable implementation has to include a \`used\` flag, a TTL and atomic operations for the prevention of race conditions in the checking.\\n\\nThe recommendations for the application\\n\\nOne-time tokens are suitable for scenarios with a low level of risk or when the link is passed through a protected channel (an email with encryption, for example). For highly sensitive data it is better to use more complex mechanisms, such as the user's authenticated sessions or temporary credentials with a detailed audit of the access.\\n\\nSummary: Use one-time tokens for the downloading of files with care, always adding a strict term of validity, guaranteeing the one-time-ness through state on the server and passing the tokens over protected channels (through the Authorization headers instead of the URL, for example).","// A simplified example of the generation and the checking of a token (Node.js/Express)\\nconst tokens = new Map(); // The store of the tokens in memory\\n\\n// The generation of a link for the download\\napp.post('/generate-link', (req, res) => {\\n    const fileId = req.body.fileId;\\n    const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');\\n    // We save the token without a term of validity and without the tracking of the use\\n    tokens.set(token, { fileId, used: false });\\n    const downloadUrl = \`https://api.example.com/download/\${fileId}?token=\${token}\`;\\n    res.json({ url: downloadUrl });\\n});\\n\\n// The endpoint for the download\\napp.get('/download/:fileId', (req, res) => {\\n    const { token } = req.query;\\n    const record = tokens.get(token);\\n    if (!record || record.fileId !== req.params.fileId) {\\n        return res.status(403).send('Invalid token');\\n    }\\n    // THE PROBLEM: There is no check of whether the token has already been used (record.used)\\n    // THE PROBLEM: There is no term of validity for the token\\n    // We send the file...\\n    res.download(getFilePath(record.fileId));\\n    // The token is NOT marked as used; it can be applied again.\\n});"],[1459,"A pre-signed URL is a temporary link that gives the right to download or upload a file in cloud storage (Amazon S3, for example) without direct credentials. The link is generated on the server side using a secret key and contains a signature, a term of validity and the parameters of the access. This lets you share files safely with clients or external systems without opening public access to the whole bucket. Once the term has expired the link becomes invalid.","A pre-signed URL is a mechanism providing temporary and limited access to an object in protected cloud storage, such as Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage or Azure Blob Storage. Instead of making files public, the server generates a unique link containing a cryptographic signature, the parameters of the operation (GET for a download, for example) and a term of validity. This lets the client (the browser, a mobile application) turn to the storage directly, bypassing the server for the transfer of the files themselves, which lowers the load on the server-side application.\\nHow this works\\nThe process usually includes the following steps:\\n\\n- The authentication on the server: The user requests access to the file through your back-end API, passing the standard authentication (by a token, for example).\\n- The generation of the link: The server-side application, using the cloud provider's SDK and its own credentials (the secret key), creates a pre-signed URL for the specific file. In the URL the following are included: the storage's endpoint, the name of the bucket, the object's key, the term of validity (5 minutes, for example) and the digital signature.\\n- The issuing of the link to the client: The URL generated is returned to the client in the API's response.\\n- The direct access: The client uses that URL to download the file directly from the cloud storage. The storage checks the signature and the term of validity itself.\\nAn example of code (Node.js with the AWS SDK)\\n\\nIn this example \`getSignedUrlPromise\` creates a signed URL for the download of the file \`q3.pdf\`. The link will be valid for 5 minutes (300 seconds). Analogously you can generate a URL for an upload (PUT) by means of \`'putObject'\`.\\nWhere it is applied\\nPre-signed URLs are widely used in scenarios in which temporary access to private files has to be provided:\\n\\n- The download of purchased content: A user who has bought a digital product (a book, music) receives a temporary link for the download.\\n- The upload of the user's files: The client-side application obtains a pre-signed URL for the direct upload of large files into S3, bypassing the server.\\n- The exchange of documents in external integrations: The secure transfer of files to partners or other systems for a limited time.\\n- The generation of reports: The system forms a report, saves it in private storage and issues the user a temporary link for the download.\\nConclusion: Pre-signed URLs should be applied when you have to ensure secure, time-controlled access to files in cloud storage, unloading your own server from the transfer of large volumes of data. This is the standard and reliable pattern for work with private content in cloud applications.","const AWS = require('aws-sdk');\\nconst s3 = new AWS.S3({\\n  region: 'us-east-1',\\n  accessKeyId: process.env.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID,\\n  secretAccessKey: process.env.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\\n});\\n\\nasync function generatePresignedUrl(bucketName, objectKey, expiresIn = 300) {\\n  const params = {\\n    Bucket: bucketName,\\n    Key: objectKey,\\n    Expires: expiresIn // The lifetime of the link in seconds\\n  };\\n  \\n  try {\\n    // The generation of a URL for the GET operation (the download)\\n    const url = await s3.getSignedUrlPromise('getObject', params);\\n    return url;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Error generating pre-signed URL:', error);\\n    throw error;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The use\\nconst downloadUrl = await generatePresignedUrl('my-secure-bucket', 'reports/q3.pdf', 300);\\nconsole.log('Pre-signed URL:', downloadUrl);"],[1460,"To download protected files you have to pass the authorisation token (usually in the Authorization header) in the request to the protected endpoint. The server checks the token and, if the access is permitted, returns the file with the correct headers. On the client you can use fetch to obtain the binary data, create a Blob and a temporary link for the download through an anchor element. This lets you not show the file directly in the browser but initiate its saving.","The downloading of files whose access is limited by the user's rights requires the joint work of the client-side and the server-side parts of the application. The main idea consists in the fact that the request for the file has to be accompanied by proof that the user is authorised and has the necessary rights.\\n\\nThe client-side implementation (JavaScript)\\n\\nOn the client the \`fetch\` API is used for the carrying out of an authorised request. The token (a JWT, for example) is passed in the standard \`Authorization\` header. The response from the server is handled as binary data (a blob), which is then converted into a temporary link for the download.\\n\\nThe server-side implementation (an example in Node.js/Express)\\n\\nThe server has to:\\n\\n- Check the validity of the token in a middleware.\\n\\n- Make sure that the user has the rights to the file requested.\\n\\n- Set the correct HTTP headers for the response, so that the browser interprets it as a file for the download rather than for the display.\\n\\nThe key aspects and the alternatives\\n\\nIt matters to handle the errors (403, 404) correctly on the client. For very large files an implementation of resumption through the Range headers may be required. As an alternative, the server can generate a one-time signed link (a presigned URL), in S3 for example, and redirect the client to it, which unloads the main server.\\n\\nConclusion: This approach should be applied when you have to control the access to files at the level of individual users or roles in a web application. It ensures the security, since the file is never available by a public link and the passing of the token takes place over a protected HTTPS connection.","async function downloadProtectedFile(fileId) {\\n  const token = localStorage.getItem('authToken');\\n  const response = await fetch(\`/api/files/\${fileId}\`, {\\n    headers: {\\n      'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`\\n    }\\n  });\\n\\n  if (!response.ok) {\\n    throw new Error('File download failed');\\n  }\\n\\n  // We obtain the binary data of the file\\n  const blob = await response.blob();\\n  // We create a temporary URL for the blob\\n  const url = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);\\n  const a = document.createElement('a');\\n  a.href = url;\\n  // The name of the file can be taken from the response's header or set explicitly\\n  a.download = 'document.pdf';\\n  document.body.appendChild(a);\\n  a.click();\\n  // The cleanup\\n  window.URL.revokeObjectURL(url);\\n  document.body.removeChild(a);\\n}\\n\\n// The middleware for the checking of the JWT\\nconst auth = (req, res, next) => {\\n  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];\\n  if (!token) return res.sendStatus(401);\\n  // ... the checking of the token ...\\n  req.user = decodedUser;\\n  next();\\n};\\n\\napp.get('/api/files/:id', auth, async (req, res) => {\\n  const fileId = req.params.id;\\n  // The checking of the rights of access to the file (a query to the database)\\n  const hasAccess = await checkFileAccess(req.user.id, fileId);\\n  if (!hasAccess) return res.sendStatus(403);\\n\\n  // The path to the file in the file system\\n  const filePath = \`/secure/storage/\${fileId}.pdf\`;\\n  // We set the headers for the download\\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/pdf');\\n  res.setHeader('Content-Disposition', \`attachment; filename=\\"report_\${fileId}.pdf\\"\`);\\n  // We send the file\\n  res.sendFile(filePath);\\n});"],[1461,"The integration of a React application with native code in a WebView is carried out through a special \\"bridge\\". The native part provides an interface that can be called from JavaScript. In React you use that interface to send data or call native functions. In the other direction, the native code can carry JavaScript out in the WebView in order to pass data into a React component. The main limitations: the performance is lower than that of native screens, the access to some of the device's APIs may be limited or require additional configuration through the bridge, and delays in the communication between the layers are also possible.","Hybrid applications, in which a React interface works inside a WebView, require a reliable channel of communication between the JavaScript code and the native platform (Java/Kotlin for Android, Objective-C/Swift for iOS). That channel is called a bridge.\\nHow to organise the integration\\nThe process usually includes the following steps:\\n\\n- The configuration of the WebView: In the native application a WebView component is created, which loads your React application (either from a local server or from a static HTML file).\\n- The creation of the bridge: The native platform registers an interface, which becomes available in the global JavaScript context (\`window.NativeBridge\`, for example).\\n- The call of native methods from React: React components can call the methods of that interface for access to the device's functions (the camera, GPS, the file system).\\n- The feedback (from the native code to React): The native code can carry JavaScript out in the WebView's context — by calling callback functions defined in advance, for example — in order to pass data or notify of events.\\nAn example of code (schematically)\\nBelow a simplified example is given for Android (Kotlin) and the corresponding React code.\\n\\nThe limitations compared with a browser\\n\\n- The performance: A WebView may be slower, especially for complex animation or the handling of a large quantity of data. The scrolling and the responsiveness are sometimes inferior to native components.\\n- The access to APIs: The standard browser APIs (Service Workers or some properties of window, for example) may be unavailable or behave differently. Access to specific functions of the device (contacts, sensors) almost always requires an implementation through the bridge.\\n- The debugging: The debugging of code in a WebView can be harder than in a browser, although modern tools (Chrome DevTools for Android, the Safari Web Inspector for iOS) help.\\n- The loading and the caching: The management of the cache and of the loading of resources may differ, which affects the speed of the first start.\\n- Security: You have to check the data passed through the bridge thoroughly in order to avoid injections.\\nConclusion: Such an approach is worth applying when you have to develop a cross-platform interface using React quickly while some native capabilities are required. It is well suited for prototypes, internal tools or applications in which performance is not a critical factor. For high-performance applications or ones complex in terms of UI it is worth considering native solutions or frameworks of the React Native kind.","// Android (Kotlin) - the configuration of the WebView\\nclass MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\\n        val webView = WebView(this).apply {\\n            settings.javaScriptEnabled = true\\n            addJavascriptInterface(NativeBridge(this@MainActivity), \\"NativeBridge\\")\\n            loadUrl(\\"file:///android_asset/index.html\\")\\n        }\\n        setContentView(webView)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass NativeBridge(private val context: Context) {\\n    @JavascriptInterface\\n    fun showToast(message: String) {\\n        Toast.makeText(context, message, Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The React component\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nconst App = () => {\\n    const sendToNative = () => {\\n        if (window.NativeBridge && window.NativeBridge.showToast) {\\n            window.NativeBridge.showToast('Hello from React!');\\n        } else {\\n            console.warn('Native bridge not available');\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    return (\\n        <button onClick={sendToNative}>\\n            Show Native Toast\\n        </button>\\n    );\\n};\\n\\nexport default App;"],[1462,"The main way is the use of the Streams API together with the Fetch API for the streaming handling of the data. This lets you read and handle the file in parts without loading it in its entirety into memory. You can also use the \`download\` attribute of a link or the \`Content-Disposition: attachment\` header for the direct downloading of the file by the server. For very large files Service Workers are sometimes applied for the caching of the parts of the file.","In the downloading of large files in a browser the key task is to avoid the loading of the whole file into the user's RAM, which can lead to a fall in performance or to the crash of the tab. The traditional approach with \`XMLHttpRequest\` or a simple \`fetch()\` loads the whole response into memory as a Blob or a string, which is unacceptable for gigabyte files. The modern APIs provide mechanisms for streaming handling.\\n\\n1. The use of the Fetch API with Streams\\n\\nThe Fetch API can return the response as a readable stream (a \`ReadableStream\`). You can handle the data as it arrives from the server — write it straight to the user's disk, for example (if the browser supports the File System Access API), or carry out streaming handling.\\n\\n2. The direct download through a link or an iframe\\n\\nThe simplest way and the safest for memory is to make the browser handle the file as a download. This can be done by creating a link with the \`download\` attribute or by using an iframe. The file is saved directly in the downloads folder, bypassing the JavaScript memory.\\n\\nThis method works if the server sends the correct \`Content-Disposition: attachment\` header.\\n\\n3. The File System Access API (experimental)\\n\\nIn modern browsers (Chrome, Edge) you can use the File System Access API to create a file on the user's disk and write the data into it in a stream through Fetch. This lets you download files of any size, writing them to the disk in parts.\\n\\n4. The splitting into parts (a chunked download)\\n\\nIf the server supports ranges of bytes (the \`Accept-Ranges: bytes\` header), you can download the file in parts by means of the \`Range\` header. Every part can be handled separately and then combined on the client (by means of the File System Access API, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: For the downloading of large files without loading them into memory it is preferable to use the streaming APIs (Fetch + Streams) in combination with the File System Access API for the direct writing to the disk. If maximum compatibility is required, the method with the creation of a link or an iframe is suitable, which shifts the task of the downloading onto the browser.","async function downloadLargeFile(url) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  const reader = response.body.getReader();\\n  const chunks = [];\\n\\n  while (true) {\\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\\n    if (done) break;\\n    // value is a Uint8Array (a chunk of the data)\\n    chunks.push(value);\\n    // Here the chunks can be sent on - into another stream, for example\\n  }\\n  // Combining the chunks in memory is not worth it for very large files\\n  // It is better to use a WritableStream for the writing to the disk\\n}\\n\\n// Way 1: The creation of a temporary link\\nconst link = document.createElement('a');\\nlink.href = 'https://example.com/large-file.zip';\\nlink.download = 'file.zip';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(link);\\nlink.click();\\ndocument.body.removeChild(link);\\n\\n// Way 2: The use of an iframe\\nconst iframe = document.createElement('iframe');\\niframe.style.display = 'none';\\niframe.src = 'https://example.com/large-file.zip';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(iframe);\\n// The file will begin to download\\n\\nasync function saveLargeFileStream(url, fileName) {\\n  const response = await fetch(url);\\n  const fileHandle = await window.showSaveFilePicker({\\n    suggestedName: fileName\\n  });\\n  const writableStream = await fileHandle.createWritable();\\n  await response.body.pipeTo(writableStream); // The streaming transfer!\\n  console.log('The file has been saved to the disk.');\\n}\\n\\nasync function downloadInChunks(url, chunkSize = 1024 * 1024) {\\n  // First we find out the size of the file\\n  const headResponse = await fetch(url, { method: 'HEAD' });\\n  const totalSize = parseInt(headResponse.headers.get('Content-Length'), 10);\\n  \\n  for (let start = 0; start < totalSize; start += chunkSize) {\\n    const end = Math.min(start + chunkSize - 1, totalSize - 1);\\n    const response = await fetch(url, {\\n      headers: { 'Range': \`bytes=\${start}-\${end}\` }\\n    });\\n    const chunk = await response.arrayBuffer();\\n    // The handling of every chunk\\n  }\\n}"],[1463,"The base path is the part of the URL between the domain and the beginning of the path to a specific resource of the application. It can be determined from the \`window.location\` object. For example, if the application is deployed at the address \`https://example.com/my-app/\`, then the base path is \`/my-app/\`. For SPAs that use the HTML5 History API the base path is often set in the router's configuration (in React Router, for example, the \`basename\` prop). It can also be computed by comparing \`window.location.pathname\` with the known structure of the application's paths.","The base path (or public path) is the prefix of the URL under which a web application is deployed on a server. It separates the application's static resources and routes from the root of the domain. An understanding and the correct determination of the base path are necessary for the correct working of the routing on the client side, for the loading of resources (images, scripts, styles) and for the forming of links.\\n\\nThe main ways of determining it\\n\\nMost often the base path is extracted from the browser's global \`window.location\` object:\\n\\nWork with the modern frameworks\\n\\nIn the ecosystem of React, Vue or Angular the base path is usually configured in the router:\\n\\n- React Router: The \`basename\` prop in the \`<BrowserRouter>\` component.\\n\\n- Vue Router: The \`base\` option on the creation of the router's instance.\\n\\n- Angular: The configuration of \`APP_BASE_HREF\` in the providers.\\n\\nThese frameworks automatically take the base path into account in the generation of links and in the navigation.\\n\\nA practical example with an environment variable\\n\\nIn builds with the use of Webpack or Vite the base path is often set through an environment variable or the configuration:\\n\\nConclusion: The determination of the base path is necessary for the correct deployment of an application in subdirectories (not at the root of the domain). Use the settings of your framework's router for centralised management, or compute the path from \`window.location\` if a dynamic determination is required. This guarantees that every relative path and route will work correctly in any environment of deployment.","// Example 1: Obtaining the complete path from the URL\\nconst fullPath = window.location.pathname; // For example, '/my-app/users/123'\\n\\n// If the application is always at a fixed path\\nconst hardcodedBasePath = '/my-app/';\\n\\n// Example 2: The dynamic determination, if the application starts from the root\\nlet basePath = '/';\\nif (fullPath.startsWith('/my-app/')) {\\n    basePath = '/my-app/';\\n}\\nconsole.log('Base Path:', basePath);\\n\\n// The configuration for the production build\\nconst BASE_PATH = process.env.PUBLIC_URL || '';\\n\\n// The use for the prefixing of API requests or links\\nfunction getApiUrl(endpoint) {\\n    return \`\${BASE_PATH}/api/v1\${endpoint}\`;\\n}\\n\\n// The use in the markup for static resources\\nconst logoUrl = \`\${BASE_PATH}/static/logo.png\`;"],[1464,"A private npm repository is a store for a company's internal packages, not available publicly. It is needed for the secure exchange of code between teams, the management of the versions of internal libraries and the protection of intellectual property. It can be organised by means of cloud services (npm Private Packages, GitHub Packages) or you can deploy a server of your own (Verdaccio, Sonatype Nexus). This simplifies the development, ensures the control of the dependencies and speeds the building of projects up.","A private npm repository is a closed store for JavaScript packages, which lets teams safely publish, version and use internal libraries, utilities or configurations. Unlike the public npm registry, the access to it is limited, which protects commercial secrets and code not intended for open distribution.\\n\\nWhat is this for?\\n\\n- Security and control: The internal packages (UI kits, API clients, configs, for example) remain inside the company.\\n\\n- The management of dependencies: It simplifies the updating and the versioning of the shared modules between projects.\\n\\n- The speed of the development: The reuse of code speeds the creation of new applications up.\\n\\n- The CI/CD integration: It lets you automate the publication and the use of the packages in the pipelines.\\n\\nHow do you organise it?\\n\\nThere are two main approaches: use a cloud service or deploy a server of your own.\\n\\nThe cloud solutions\\n\\nFor example, npm Private Packages (paid) or GitHub Packages (integrated with the repositories). The setup is simple: you have to create an account, configure the tokens of access and update the npm configuration.\\n\\nThe self-hosted solutions\\n\\nA popular tool is Verdaccio (a lightweight private npm proxy). It can be run in Docker for a fast deployment.\\n\\nThe practical steps for the introduction\\n\\n- Choose a solution (cloud or local) on the basis of the budget and the requirements of security.\\n\\n- Configure the authentication (tokens, logins).\\n\\n- Update the package.json files in your packages, specifying the correct registry.\\n\\n- Integrate the publication of the packages into the CI/CD process (on the creation of a tag in Git, for example).\\n\\n- Document the process for the developers.\\n\\nConclusion: A private npm repository is worth applying in companies in which several teams develop shared libraries or microservices on Node.js/JavaScript. It raises the security, standardises the code and speeds the development up through the reuse of proven modules.","# The installation of a package from a private GitHub repository\\nnpm install @myorg/my-private-package --registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com/\\n\\n# The publication of a package\\nnpm publish --registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com/\\n\\n# The running of Verdaccio in Docker\\ndocker run -it --rm --name verdaccio -p 4873:4873 verdaccio/verdaccio\\n\\n# The configuration of the npm client for the use of the local repository\\nnpm set registry http://localhost:4873/\\n\\n# After that the publication and the installation of packages will go through Verdaccio"],[1465,"When the user clicks on a link with the \`download\` attribute, the browser initiates a GET request to the URL specified. If the server answers with the correct headers (\`Content-Disposition: attachment\`, for example), the browser interprets the response as a file to be saved rather than to be displayed. It then shows the standard dialogue window for the saving of the file into the user's local file system. This process bypasses the CORS security policy for links of the same origin.","The downloading of files through links is a fundamental capability of the web, letting users save content onto their devices. The mechanism is started when the user interacts with an HTML \`<a>\` element (an anchor) that has the \`download\` attribute set.\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\nThe process can be divided into several stages:\\n\\n- The initiation of the request: A click on a link with \`download\` makes the browser carry out an HTTP GET request to the URL specified in the \`href\` attribute.\\n\\n- The handling of the server's response: The key role is played by the \`Content-Disposition\` HTTP header. If the server sends \`Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\\"example.pdf\\"\`, the browser understands that the body of the response is a file that has to be saved rather than displayed in the current tab.\\n\\n- The saving of the file: The browser receives the stream of data (binary or textual) and, following the policies of security, offers the user the choice of a place for the saving through the system's dialogue window.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at simple HTML code for the creation of a download link:\\n\\nIn the second example the server-side handler (on Node.js with Express, for example) has to set the correct headers:\\n\\nThe particulars and the limitations\\n\\nThe \`download\` attribute has limitations for reasons of security. It will not work if the file is on another domain (cross-origin) and the server of that domain does not send the corresponding CORS headers. In such cases the browser will simply navigate to the link rather than offering to download the file. To download data generated on the client (the contents of a text editor, for example) you can use the \`URL.createObjectURL()\` JavaScript API together with \`Blob\` objects.\\n\\nConclusion: The mechanism of the download through links is simple and effective for the distribution of static files or files generated on the server. It is worth applying when you have to provide the user with a simple way of saving a document, an image or an archive, without requiring complex client-side logic.","<!-- A link to a static file on the same server -->\\n<a href=\\"/files/report.pdf\\" download=\\"my_report.pdf\\">\\n  Download the report (PDF)\\n</a>\\n\\n<!-- A link that initiates the download through a server-side script -->\\n<a href=\\"/api/download?id=123\\" download>Download the document</a>\\n\\n// An example in Node.js/Express\\napp.get('/api/download', (req, res) => {\\n  const filePath = path.resolve(__dirname, 'private', 'file123.zip');\\n  // We set the header indicating a download\\n  res.setHeader('Content-Disposition', 'attachment; filename=\\"archive.zip\\"');\\n  res.sendFile(filePath); // We send the file\\n});"],[1466,"The main approaches are the reading of files in parts (chunking) through the File API, the use of streams for gradual handling, the moving of heavy operations out into Web Workers so as not to block the interface, and the application of a Blob for work with binary data without loading the whole file into memory. It also matters to use virtualisation for the display of large lists of data from a file. These methods let you handle gigabytes of data without falls in performance.","In work with large files (CSV, JSON or logs, for example) in a browser the direct loading of the whole file into memory can lead to a freeze of the interface or to an \\"out of memory\\" error. Special techniques are therefore used, which let the data be handled gradually without loading the main memory and the UI thread.\\n\\n1. The reading of a file in parts (chunking)\\n\\nBy means of the \`File\` and \`Blob\` APIs a file can be split into chunks and they can be read in sequence. The \`slice()\` method (or \`blob.slice()\`) creates a new Blob containing part of the original file. This lets you, for example, upload a large file to a server in chunks or handle it line by line without loading it in its entirety.\\n\\n2. The streaming handling (the Streams API)\\n\\nThe Streams API provides a more elegant way of handling data as it arrives. You can create a readable stream from a file and transform the data through a chain of transformations. This is especially useful for the parsing of large CSV or JSON files.\\n\\n3. The moving of the computations out into Web Workers\\n\\nIf the handling of a file requires intensive computations (complex parsing or encryption, for example), it is worth moving it out into a Web Worker. This will prevent the blocking of the main thread and of the user's interface.\\n\\n4. Virtualisation for the display\\n\\nIf thousands of lines of data have been extracted from the file, their display in the DOM may be slow. Libraries of virtualisation (react-window, for example) render only the visible elements, which greatly speeds the working up.\\n\\nConclusion: The approaches described are worth applying when you work with files of tens of megabytes and larger. The reading in parts and streams are suitable for sequential handling, Web Workers for heavy computations, and virtualisation for the display of large volumes of data. This lets you create responsive web applications even in work with gigabytes of information.","const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput');\\nfileInput.addEventListener('change', async (e) => {\\n  const file = e.target.files[0];\\n  const chunkSize = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB\\n  let offset = 0;\\n\\n  while (offset < file.size) {\\n    const chunk = file.slice(offset, offset + chunkSize);\\n    // Handle the chunk (send it to the server or read the text, for example)\\n    const text = await chunk.text();\\n    console.log(\`\${chunk.size} bytes have been read\`);\\n    offset += chunkSize;\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nasync function processLargeFile(file) {\\n  const stream = file.stream();\\n  const reader = stream.getReader();\\n\\n  while (true) {\\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\\n    if (done) break;\\n    // value is a Uint8Array with part of the data\\n    // The handling of the portion of the data\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker('file-worker.js');\\nworker.postMessage({ file: largeFile });\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {\\n  console.log('The result of the handling:', e.data);\\n};\\n\\n// file-worker.js\\nself.onmessage = async (e) => {\\n  const file = e.data.file;\\n  // The heavy handling of the file\\n  const result = processFile(file);\\n  self.postMessage(result);\\n};"],[1467,"The deployment of an application not at the root (in \`/app/\`, for example) causes several problems. The relative paths to the resources (CSS, JS, images) may break if they are not configured correctly. The routing in single-page applications (SPAs) may conflict with the web server's settings, requiring the rewriting of the URLs. Problems with CORS and cookies may arise if the subpaths are handled as different origins. The SEO also suffers, since search engines may index pages with incorrect absolute paths incorrectly.","When a web application is deployed not at the root of the domain but in a subdirectory (\`https://example.com/myapp/\`, for example), that affects a multitude of aspects of its working. The main difficulty consists in the fact that the application expects every resource and route to begin from the server's root (\`/\`), but in fact they have to be tied to the base path (\`/myapp/\`).\\n\\nThe problems with the paths to the resources\\n\\nThe relative paths in the HTML, the CSS and the JavaScript begin to be resolved relative to the browser's current URL. If the file \`style.css\` is specified as \`./styles.css\` on the page \`/myapp/dashboard\`, the browser will try to load it at the address \`/myapp/dashboard/styles.css\`, which is most likely wrong. The solution is the use of absolute paths beginning with the application's base path, or the configuration of a \`<base href>\` tag in the HTML.\\n\\nThe problems with the routing in an SPA (React, Vue, Angular)\\n\\nLibraries of routing, such as React Router, by default work with the root. If the application lives at \`/myapp/\`, every route has to be configured with that prefix. Without that the navigation inside the application will work, but on a reload of the page or a direct navigation by a URL the web server will return a 404 error, since it will not find the corresponding file on the disk. A configuration of the server is required for the rewriting of every request inside \`/myapp/\` to the index HTML file.\\n\\nThe problems with API requests and CORS\\n\\nIf the back-end API is deployed separately or also under a subpath, the front end has to form the URLs for the requests correctly. The use of relative paths for the API (\`/api/users\`) will lead to a request to \`https://example.com/myapp/api/users\`, which may be the wrong address. This may cause CORS errors if the API server is not configured to permit requests from such a path.\\n\\nThe SEO and the social metatags\\n\\nSearch engines and social networks (Open Graph, Twitter Cards) use absolute URLs. If the application generates metatags with relative paths for images or canonical URLs, they will be incomplete, which will worsen the indexing and the display in the social networks.\\n\\nConclusion: A deployment not at the root requires the thorough configuration of the base path in the front-end application, the configuration of the web server (Nginx, Apache) for the correct handling of the routes and the use of absolute paths for the resources and the API. This is especially critical for SPAs and for applications in which deep links and SEO matter.","// An example of the configuration of the base path in React Router v6\\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    // It matters to specify the basename\\n    <BrowserRouter basename=\\"/myapp\\">\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}"],[1468,"The interaction is organised through the sending of an access token (usually a JWT) in the header of the HTTP request. The user first logs in, receiving the token from the server. That token is then saved on the client (in localStorage or an HttpOnly cookie, for example) and automatically added to every subsequent request to the protected endpoints of the API. The server checks the validity of the token before carrying the request out, which ensures secure access to the data.","The organisation of the interaction of a front end with a back-end API using tokens is the standard approach to the management of the user's sessions in modern stateless applications. Instead of holding the state of the session on the server, the client receives a signed token after a successful authentication (through a login and a password, for example) and uses it for access to the protected resources.\\nThe main flow of the working\\nThe typical process includes several steps:\\n\\n- The authentication: The client sends the credentials (a login and a password, for example) to a special endpoint of the API (\`/api/auth/login\`, for example).\\n- The receiving of the token: The server checks the data, and if it is correct generates a token (most often a JWT — a JSON Web Token) and sends it in the response.\\n- The holding of the token on the client: The front end saves the token received. The widespread variants: \`localStorage\`, \`sessionStorage\` or an \`HttpOnly\` cookie (the last is more secure against XSS attacks).\\n- The use of the token: On every subsequent request to the protected API the front end adds the token to the header of the HTTP request, usually in the format \`Authorization: Bearer <your_token>\`.\\n- The verification on the server: The back end checks the token's signature, its term of validity and the other claims before carrying the request out and returning the data.\\nA practical example of code\\nBelow a simplified example in JavaScript with the use of the Fetch API is given.\\n\\nThe security and the best practices\\nIn the use of tokens it matters to take the risks to security into account. The holding in \`localStorage\` is vulnerable to XSS attacks. A more secure approach is the use of \`HttpOnly\` cookies (the token is automatically sent with every request) or a combination of an Access Token (short-lived) and a Refresh Token (long-lived, held in a secure cookie). It is also necessary to use HTTPS for the encryption of the traffic.\\nConclusion: The use of tokens is an effective and scalable way of organising stateless authentication between a front end and an API. This approach is worth applying in SPAs (Single Page Applications), mobile applications and microservice architectures, in which it matters to avoid holding the state of the session on the server.","// 1. The function for the carrying out of the log-in and the receiving of the token\\nasync function login(username, password) {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/auth/login', {\\n        method: 'POST',\\n        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n        body: JSON.stringify({ username, password })\\n    });\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    if (response.ok) {\\n        // We save the token in localStorage\\n        localStorage.setItem('accessToken', data.accessToken);\\n        console.log('The log-in has been carried out');\\n    } else {\\n        throw new Error(data.message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// 2. The function for the carrying out of an authorised request to the API\\nasync function fetchProtectedData() {\\n    const token = localStorage.getItem('accessToken');\\n    if (!token) {\\n        throw new Error('The token was not found');\\n    }\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/protected/data', {\\n        headers: {\\n            'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`\\n        }\\n    });\\n    if (response.status === 401) {\\n        // The token has expired or is invalid\\n        // You can try to refresh the token by means of a refresh token\\n        console.log('A repeated authentication is required');\\n        localStorage.removeItem('accessToken');\\n        // Redirect to the log-in page\\n    }\\n    return await response.json();\\n}\\n\\n// An example of the use\\n// login('user', 'pass').then(() => fetchProtectedData());"],[1469,"The resumption of a file transfer is implemented by splitting the file into parts and tracking the fragments already transferred. On the client side, before the sending, the server is asked how many bytes it has already received, using the HTTP Range header. The server has to support the handling of the Range and Accept-Ranges headers in order to return the part of the file needed. On the client, after a break in the connection the sending can be resumed from the last byte successfully transferred, avoiding the repeated sending of the whole file.","The implementation of a resumable upload or download is important functionality for raising the reliability of the transfer of files, especially in conditions of an unstable internet connection or in work with large volumes of data. The main idea consists in splitting the file into manageable parts (chunks) and having the ability to continue the transfer from the place of the break rather than beginning anew.\\nThe key mechanisms\\nAt the foundation often lies the HTTP protocol and its specification of headers:\\n\\n- The Range header: The client can request a particular range of the file's bytes (\`Range: bytes=500-999\`, for example).\\n- The Accept-Ranges header: The server reports that it supports resumption by sending \`Accept-Ranges: bytes\` in the response.\\n- The Content-Range header: The server indicates which range of bytes exactly is being returned in response to a request with Range (\`Content-Range: bytes 500-999/1500\`, for example).\\nThe typical algorithm for an upload\\nFor the uploading of a file to a server the approach may be like this:\\n\\n- Before the beginning of the upload the client asks the server for information about the part of the file already uploaded (by sending the file's hash, for example).\\n- The server returns the size of the data already received (in bytes, for example).\\n- The client splits the file into chunks and begins the sending from the position following the part already uploaded.\\n- After the successful sending of every chunk the client updates the local progress. On a break in the connection the process repeats from step 1.\\nAn example of code (the client-side logic in JavaScript)\\nBelow a simplified example is given, demonstrating the logic of a resumable upload with the use of the Fetch API and the control of the progress.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nResumption is widely used in cloud storage (Google Drive, Dropbox), in torrent clients, in systems of backup, in streaming services for video and in any corporate applications in which large files are transferred (logs, archives, media files).\\nConclusion: The implementation of resumption is necessary for the creation of fault-tolerant and user-friendly applications that work with files. It is worth applying whenever there is a risk of a break in the connection or when the size of the data transferred is significant, in order to save the user's traffic and time.","async function resumeUpload(file, uploadUrl) {\\n  // 1. We obtain the size already uploaded from the server\\n  const statusResponse = await fetch(\`\${uploadUrl}/status?fileId=\${file.name}\`);\\n  const { uploadedSize } = await statusResponse.json();\\n  \\n  // 2. We open the file for reading from the position needed\\n  const chunkSize = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB\\n  let startByte = uploadedSize;\\n  \\n  while (startByte < file.size) {\\n    const chunk = file.slice(startByte, startByte + chunkSize);\\n    const formData = new FormData();\\n    formData.append('chunk', chunk);\\n    formData.append('fileId', file.name);\\n    formData.append('startByte', startByte.toString());\\n    \\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch(uploadUrl, {\\n        method: 'POST',\\n        body: formData,\\n      });\\n      if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Upload failed');\\n      // 3. We update the progress after the successful sending of the chunk\\n      startByte += chunk.size;\\n      console.log(\`Uploaded \${startByte} of \${file.size} bytes\`);\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      console.error('Chunk upload failed, will retry:', error);\\n      // Here the logic of repeated attempts can be added\\n      break;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (startByte >= file.size) {\\n    console.log('File upload completed!');\\n  }\\n}"],[1470,"The main approaches are the passing of the token in the Authorization header, in a parameter of the URL (the query string) or through a cookie. The header is the safest and most standard way for an API. A parameter of the URL is simpler, but the token may land in the server's logs. Cookies are convenient for browser scenarios, but they require the configuration of the flags of security. The choice depends on the context: an API, a direct link in a browser or the integration with a front end.","In the downloading of files protected by authorisation, the access token has to be passed to the server for the checking of the rights. There are several widespread approaches, each with its own pluses, minuses and scenarios of application.\\n1. The Authorization header (the most recommended)\\nThe token is passed in an HTTP header, usually in the format \`Bearer <token>\`. This is the standard way for REST APIs and for many client libraries (fetch, axios, for example).\\n\\n- The pluses: It is secure (the token is not visible in the URL, which may be preserved in the browser's history or in the server's logs). It cleanly separates the metadata of the authorisation from the request's data.\\n- The minuses: It is not always applicable for simple downloads by a direct link in a browser (through an \`<a>\` tag, for example), since the browser does not add that header automatically.\\n\\n2. A parameter of the URL (a query parameter)\\nThe token is added to the file's URL in the form of a query parameter — for example: \`/download/file.pdf?token=abc123\`.\\n\\n- The pluses: It is very simple to implement. It works for any direct links that can be inserted into a browser, sent by post or used in an \`<a href>\` tag.\\n- The minuses: It is less secure. The token may be preserved in the browser's history, in the web server's logs, in referrers. It is not recommended for long-lived or high-level tokens.\\n\\n3. A cookie\\nThe token is set in an HTTP cookie on the authentication, and the browser automatically sends it with every request to the domain, requests for the downloading of files included.\\n\\n- The pluses: It is convenient for browser applications (SPAs), and it requires no manual passing of the token in every request. It works automatically with the \`<a>\` tag and with \`window.open\`.\\n- The minuses: It requires careful configuration (the \`HttpOnly\`, \`Secure\` and \`SameSite\` flags) for protection against XSS and CSRF attacks. It is less suitable for APIs used by things other than browsers (mobile applications, server-side integrations).\\n\\n4. The combined and special approaches\\nSometimes hybrid methods are used. For one-time downloads, for example, a temporary signed URL is generated with the token in a parameter, which self-destructs after the use. Also, in some APIs (AWS S3, for example) pre-signed URLs are used, which are a particular case of the passing of credentials through query parameters.\\nConclusion: For REST APIs and programmable clients it is preferable to use the \`Authorization\` header, for reasons of security and of the cleanness of the API. For scenarios in which the user has to download a file by a direct link in a browser (by email, for example), you can use temporary tokens in the parameters of the URL or organise the download through an intermediate endpoint, which, having checked the header, will redirect to the actual file. Cookies are a good choice for traditional web applications in which the front end and the back end are closely connected.","// An example of a request with the use of the fetch API\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/files/123', {\\n  headers: {\\n    'Authorization': 'Bearer eyJhbGciOiJ...'\\n  }\\n})\\n.then(response => response.blob())\\n.then(blob => {\\n  // The handling of the file downloaded\\n});\\n\\n// The generation of a link for the download on the server side (a Node.js example)\\nconst downloadUrl = \`https://api.example.com/download/\${fileId}?access_token=\${userToken}\`;\\n// This link can be sent to the user or used in the front end.\\n\\n// The server (Express.js) sets a cookie on the log-in\\nres.cookie('access_token', token, {\\n  httpOnly: true, // Protection against XSS\\n  secure: true,   // Only over HTTPS\\n  sameSite: 'strict'\\n});\\n// On a subsequent request to /download/file.pdf the browser will send the cookie automatically."],[1471,"Yes, a cookie can be used for authorisation in the downloading of files. When the user authenticates on the server, the server sets a cookie in the browser. On subsequent requests, the request for the downloading of a file included, the browser automatically sends that cookie. The server checks the cookie in order to make sure that the user has the right to access the file. This is the standard approach for web applications that manage sessions.","Cookies are small fragments of data that the server sends to the user's browser, and the browser automatically returns them with every subsequent request to the same domain. This makes them a convenient mechanism for the maintenance of state (the user's session, for example) in the stateless HTTP protocol.\\n\\nHow this works in a download\\n\\nThe process usually looks like this:\\n\\n- The user logs in to the system, sending the credentials (a login and a password, for example) through the log-in form.\\n\\n- The server checks the credentials and, if they are correct, creates a session. In the response it sends a \`Set-Cookie\` HTTP header with a unique identifier of the session (\`sessionId=abc123\`, for example).\\n\\n- The browser saves that cookie. For every subsequent request to the same domain the browser will automatically add the header \`Cookie: sessionId=abc123\`.\\n\\n- When the user clicks on a link for the downloading of a protected file, the browser makes a GET request to the corresponding URL. That request includes the cookie with the identifier of the session.\\n\\n- The server, on receiving the request, extracts the identifier of the session from the cookie, checks it in its own store of sessions (in a database or in Redis, for example) and determines whether the user is authorised and has the right to access the file requested.\\n\\n- If the check has passed successfully, the server begins the handing over of the file (usually with the \`Content-Disposition: attachment\` headers). If the cookie is invalid or absent, the server returns a 401 Unauthorized or a 403 Forbidden error.\\n\\nAn example of code (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nBelow a simplified example of a server is given, which checks the cookie for the authorisation of the download.\\n\\nThe important aspects and the limitations\\n\\n- Security: For protection against interception (XSS) use the \`HttpOnly\` and \`Secure\` (for HTTPS) flags. For protection against CSRF attacks additional measures may be required.\\n\\n- The client environment: Cookies work automatically in browsers but not in simple HTTP clients of the \`curl\` kind, unless the \`Cookie\` header is added explicitly. For APIs or mobile applications tokens are often preferred (a JWT in the Authorization header, for example).\\n\\n- The performance: On every request the cookies are sent to the server, which can increase the volume of the data transferred, especially if the cookies are large.\\n\\nConclusion: The use of cookies for authorisation in a download is a standard and effective approach for web applications in which the user interacts through a browser. It is simple to implement and integrates well with the mechanisms of sessions. For APIs or non-browser clients, however, tokens passed in headers are often better suited.","const express = require('express');\\nconst cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\\nconst app = express();\\napp.use(cookieParser());\\n\\n// The store of the sessions in memory (in a real application use Redis or a database)\\nconst sessions = {};\\n\\n// The endpoint for the log-in\\napp.post('/login', (req, res) => {\\n    const { username, password } = req.body;\\n    // The checking of the credentials (simplified)\\n    if (username === 'user' && password === 'pass') {\\n        const sessionId = 'sess_' + Math.random().toString(36).substr(2);\\n        sessions[sessionId] = { username };\\n        // We set a cookie with the identifier of the session\\n        res.cookie('sessionId', sessionId, { httpOnly: true });\\n        res.send('Login successful');\\n    } else {\\n        res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');\\n    }\\n});\\n\\n// The protected endpoint for the download\\napp.get('/download/:file', (req, res) => {\\n    const sessionId = req.cookies.sessionId;\\n    if (!sessionId || !sessions[sessionId]) {\\n        return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');\\n    }\\n    // The checking of the rights of access to the file (the logic may be more complex)\\n    const user = sessions[sessionId];\\n    const fileName = req.params.file;\\n    // ... the logic of the check of whether the user can download fileName ...\\n    // If everything is fine, we hand the file over\\n    res.download(\`/secure-files/\${fileName}\`);\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[1472,"Authorisation in an SPA through tokens such as a JWT works on the principle of \\"statelessness\\". After the successful entering of the login and the password the server checks the credentials and generates a signed token (a JWT), which contains information about the user (the payload). That token is sent to the client (usually saved in localStorage or in an HttpOnly cookie). On every subsequent request to the protected API the client attaches that token in the Authorization header. The server checks the token's signature and, if it is correct, extracts the user's data from the payload without turning to the database for the session. For the refreshing of the tokens a separate refresh token is used.","Authorisation in single-page applications (SPAs) through tokens such as the JSON Web Token (JWT) represents a modern, stateless approach, which eliminates the need to hold sessions on the server. This makes an application more scalable and simplifies the integration with a microservice architecture.\\n\\nHow the process works\\n\\nThe process begins when the user enters their credentials (a login and a password, for example) into the log-in form. The SPA sends that data to the authentication server (usually through an HTTPS POST request).\\n\\n- The server checks the credentials (by comparing the hash of the password with the one saved in the database, for example).\\n\\n- If the check is successful, the server creates a JWT. A JWT consists of three parts encoded in Base64: the header, the payload and the signature.\\n\\n- The payload contains claims about the user, such as their identifier (sub), the token's term of validity (exp) and other data (the roles, for example).\\n\\n- The server signs the token by means of a secret key or a pair of keys (RSA), creating a digital signature that guarantees the token's integrity.\\n\\n- The JWT generated is sent back to the client, usually in the body of the response.\\n\\nThe holding and the use of the token on the client\\n\\nThe token received (often called the access token) has to be saved on the client side for subsequent use. There are two main approaches:\\n\\n- localStorage or sessionStorage: Simple, but vulnerable to XSS attacks if an attacker manages to inject and carry out malicious JavaScript on the page.\\n\\n- An HttpOnly cookie: More secure, since the cookie is not available to JavaScript, which protects against XSS, but it requires the configuration of CORS and protection against CSRF attacks.\\n\\nAfter the saving, the SPA has to attach that token to every request to the protected API. This is usually done through the \`Authorization\` HTTP header with the \`Bearer\` prefix.\\n\\nThe verification of the token on the server\\n\\nWhen a protected endpoint of the server receives a request with a token, it has to:\\n\\n- Extract the token from the Authorization header.\\n\\n- Check its structure and its signature, using the same secret key or the public key (in the case of asymmetric encryption).\\n\\n- Make sure that the token has not expired (by checking the \`exp\` claim).\\n\\n- Extract the user's data from the token's payload. Since the token is signed, the server can trust that information without the need to turn to the database for the checking of the session, which is precisely the key advantage.\\n\\nThe refreshing of the tokens and the Refresh Token\\n\\nAccess tokens usually have a short lifetime (minutes or hours) in order to minimise the risks in the event of a leak. To obtain a new access token without the repeated entering of the password, a refresh token is used. This is a separate, long-lived token, which is held more securely (in an HttpOnly cookie, for example) and is sent to a special endpoint of the server for the obtaining of a new pair of tokens.\\n\\nSummary: Authorisation through a JWT in an SPA is an effective stateless mechanism, ideally suited for scalable RESTful APIs and microservices. It is worth applying when you have to avoid holding the state of the session on the server and ensure a simple integration between various services. It matters, however, to implement the secure holding of the tokens on the client correctly, to use HTTPS and to use short lifetimes for the access tokens in combination with a refresh token for a balance between the user's convenience and the security.","// An example of the sending of a request with the token in JavaScript (the fetch API)\\nconst token = localStorage.getItem('accessToken');\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/protected-data', {\\n  method: 'GET',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Authorization': \`Bearer \${token}\`,\\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json'\\n  }\\n})\\n.then(response => response.json())\\n.then(data => console.log(data));\\n\\n// An example of the checking of a JWT on the server (Node.js with the jsonwebtoken library)\\nconst jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');\\nconst secretKey = process.env.JWT_SECRET;\\n\\nfunction verifyToken(req, res, next) {\\n  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;\\n  if (!authHeader) return res.sendStatus(401);\\n\\n  const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1]; // We extract the part after 'Bearer'\\n  jwt.verify(token, secretKey, (err, decoded) => {\\n    if (err) return res.sendStatus(403); // An incorrect or expired token\\n    req.user = decoded; // We add the user's data to the request object\\n    next(); // We pass control to the next middleware/handler\\n  });\\n}"],[1473,"The main problems include the explosive growth of the size of the bundle, which slows the loading down. The management of state and the communication between a multitude of components become more complex. The difficulties with the organisation of the code base, the dependencies and the consistency of the UI/UX grow. It becomes hard for teams to coordinate the work without clear conventions and tools.","The scaling of a front end is not only about performance but also about the maintenance of the code base, the management of the team and the ensuring of the product's consistency. As the application grows, systemic difficulties arise.\\nThe architectural problems\\n\\n- The bundle size: A single build becomes enormous, increasing the time of the initial loading. The solution is code splitting by routes (route-based) or by components (component-based), and lazy loading.\\n- The management of state: The flow of the data becomes tangled. The global state (through Redux, for example) can become overloaded and hard to debug. It matters to divide the state by domains and to use normalised structures.\\n- The performance of the rendering: Large lists or complex components can block the main thread. Virtualisation, memoization (React.memo, useMemo) and the optimisation of the re-renders are required.\\nThe organisational and process problems\\n\\n- A monolithic code base: A single repository becomes a bottleneck. The solutions: the move to a monorepo with tools of the Lerna or Nx kind, or the division into micro front ends.\\n- The consistency of the UI and the logic: Without strict conventions and design systems the components begin to be duplicated and the behaviour diverges. A library of UI components and shared hooks are necessary.\\n- The management of dependencies: The updating of libraries (React, for example) in a large project becomes a risky operation. Automatic testing and tools for gradual updating help.\\nAn example: the optimisation of the bundle with a dynamic import\\n\\nConclusion: The problems of the scaling of a front end require a well-thought-out architecture from the first stages: the splitting of the code, the choice of a suitable strategy for the management of state, the introduction of a design system and of tools for work with a monorepo. This is critically important for the maintenance of the speed of the development and of the application's performance in the long term.","// Instead of the static import of the whole module\\nimport { HeavyChartLibrary } from './chartLibrary';\\n\\n// We use a dynamic import for the lazy loading\\nconst renderChart = async () => {\\n  // This code will be loaded only on the call of the function\\n  const { HeavyChartLibrary } = await import('./chartLibrary');\\n  HeavyChartLibrary.draw();\\n};\\n\\n// In React Router v6\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./pages/Dashboard'));\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/dashboard\\" element={<Dashboard />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}"],[1474,"The key is in a well-thought-out information architecture and in the principle of progressive disclosure. First show the user only the main, frequently used functions. The additional, specialised actions can be hidden in context menus, behind \\"More\\" buttons or in advanced modes. A responsive toolbar matters, which changes depending on the context of the task. Do not forget about personalisation, letting experienced users configure the interface to suit themselves, and about a good search over the functions.","In the design of an interface for a functionally rich application the main challenge is the balance between power and simplicity. The direct showing of every capability at once leads to a cognitive overload and frightens newcomers. The solution lies in the area of information architecture and of UX patterns oriented towards the management of complexity.\\nThe key strategies\\n\\n- Progressive Disclosure: Show the functions as they are needed. The main working flow should use a minimal set of elements. The additional options open on a click, on a hover or in a separate dialogue.\\n- Contextual interfaces: The toolbars and the menus should change dynamically depending on the object chosen or on the mode of the working. This cuts the visual noise.\\n- Modularity and grouping: Logically combine related functions into groups with clear headings. Use separators, tabs or accordions for the organisation.\\n- Personalisation: Provide experienced users with the ability to pin frequently used tools to the main panel, hide the unnecessary ones and create keyboard shortcuts of their own.\\n- An effective search and a Command Palette: Introduce a global search over the functions and actions. This lets you quickly obtain access to any command, bypassing the navigation through the menus.\\nAn example of the implementation of a search over the commands\\nHere is a simplified example of a component for the search of commands in React, which filters the actions available:\\n\\nSuch an approach, inspired by code editors, lets you obtain instantaneous access to hundreds of functions.\\nWhere it is applied\\nThese principles are critically important in professional software: graphics editors (Photoshop, Figma), IDEs (VS Code, IntelliJ), complex CRM and ERP systems, and also in the administration panels of web applications.\\nConclusion: To ensure convenience in a multifunctional application, use progressive disclosure, contextual interfaces and a powerful search, in order to lower the barrier to entry for newcomers without limiting the capabilities for experts. Personalisation and a responsive interface are the key to long-term UX.","function CommandPalette({ commands }) {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const filteredCommands = commands.filter(cmd =>\\n    cmd.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()) ||\\n    cmd.keywords.some(kw => kw.includes(query))\\n  );\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input\\n        value={query}\\n        onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)}\\n      />\\n      <ul>\\n        {filteredCommands.map(cmd => (\\n          <li key={cmd.id} onClick={cmd.action}>\\n            {cmd.name}: {cmd.description}\\n          </li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// The use: the commands are an array of objects {id, name, action, keywords}"],[1475,"The base URL for the HTTP client (Axios or Fetch, for example) should be set at the stage of the application's initialisation, before the creation of the main services or modules that will carry the requests out. This is usually done at the point of entry (index.js or main.js, for example) or in a special configuration file. Such an approach lets you manage the API's address centrally and switch easily between different environments (development, testing, production). This improves the maintainability and prevents the duplication of code.","The concept of a base URL relates to the configuration of the HTTP client that is used for the interaction with the back-end API. The setting of the base URL at the right stage of the application's initialisation is critically important for the application's architecture, since it affects the management of the configuration, the switching between environments and the general organisation of the code.\\nWhy does this matter?\\nIn modern web applications different API servers are often used for development, testing and production. The hard-coding of the URL in every request leads to duplication, to difficulties on a change of the environment and to potential errors. The use of a centralised base URL solves those problems.\\nWhen and where should the base URL be set?\\nThe ideal place for the setting of the base URL is the earliest stage of the application's initialisation, right after the loading of the environment's configuration. This usually happens:\\n\\n- At the application's point of entry (\`index.js\` or \`main.ts\`, for example).\\n- Inside the module that creates and exports the configured instance of the HTTP client (\`apiClient.js\`, for example).\\n- On the initialisation of the global state or context (in a React application it can be wrapped in a provider, for example).\\nThe configuration (the base URL itself, for example) is often taken from environment variables (\`process.env.API_URL\`) or from a configuration file specific to the environment.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at an example of the configuration of Axios in a JavaScript/TypeScript application:\\n\\nThen that configured client is imported and used in every service or component of the application:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe base URL should be set at the earliest stage of the application's initialisation, centrally, through the creation of a pre-configured instance of the HTTP client. This approach is worth applying always in work with external APIs, since it ensures the cleanness of the code, easy management of the environments and simplifies future changes.","// The file: apiClient.js\\nimport axios from 'axios';\\n\\n// We determine the base URL on the basis of an environment variable.\\n// The default value for the local development.\\nconst BASE_URL = process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL || 'http://localhost:3000/api';\\n\\n// We create and configure the Axios instance.\\nconst apiClient = axios.create({\\n  baseURL: BASE_URL,\\n  timeout: 10000,\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }\\n});\\n\\n// Interceptors can be added for the handling of errors or for the authorisation.\\napiClient.interceptors.response.use(\\n  (response) => response,\\n  (error) => {\\n    console.error('API Request Failed:', error);\\n    return Promise.reject(error);\\n  }\\n);\\n\\nexport default apiClient;\\n\\n// The file: userService.js\\nimport apiClient from './apiClient';\\n\\nexport const fetchUser = (id) => {\\n  return apiClient.get(\`/users/\${id}\`); // The relative path is automatically added to the baseURL\\n};"],[1476,"For React there are a multitude of UI libraries providing ready-made components. PrimeReact, Material-UI, Ant Design and Chakra UI are among the most popular. PrimeReact offers an extensive set of components with various themes, Material and Bootstrap included. Material-UI strictly follows the principles of Google's Material Design. Ant Design is popular for corporate applications with clear, functional components. The choice depends on the requirements of the design, the accessibility and the speed of the development.","UI libraries for React provide a set of ready-made, styled and interactive components (buttons, tables, modal windows), which substantially speeds the development of interfaces up and ensures the uniformity of the design. They spare the developer the need to write CSS and logic for the widespread elements from scratch.\\nThe popular libraries and their particulars\\n\\n- Material-UI (MUI): The most popular library, implementing Google's Material Design design system. It stands out for its high customisability, its excellent documentation and its large community. It is ideal for projects that want to follow the modern Material guidelines.\\n- Ant Design: A powerful library for corporate (enterprise) applications. It offers an extremely rich set of professional-looking components, especially for data (complex tables, forms, calendars). It is popular in the React ecosystem alongside Next.js.\\n- PrimeReact: It stands out for the enormous quantity of its components (more than 80), complex ones of the diagram or tree kind included. It supports several built-in themes (Material, Bootstrap, its own) and has excellent accessibility (a11y). A good choice when the maximum functionality \\"out of the box\\" is needed.\\n- Chakra UI: A library gaining popularity, focused on simplicity, accessibility and composability. The styling is founded on props, which makes the creation of a UI very declarative and fast. It is excellently suited for start-ups and for projects in which speed and a modern look and feel matter.\\nAn example of the use of PrimeReact\\nHaving installed the library, you can use its components at once. Here is an example of a simple button and a dialogue window:\\n\\nThis code demonstrates how quickly you can add a styled button with an icon and a controlled modal window without writing a single line of CSS.\\nConclusion: The choice of a UI library depends on the project's requirements: for the strict following of Material Design choose MUI, for corporate dashboards Ant Design, for the maximum functionality and themes PrimeReact, and for fast prototyping and a modern approach Chakra UI. The use of any of them cuts the time of the development and improves the consistency of the interface.","import { Button } from 'primereact/button';\\nimport { Dialog } from 'primereact/dialog';\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [visible, setVisible] = useState(false);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Button\\n        label=\\"Show\\"\\n        icon=\\"pi pi-external-link\\"\\n        onClick={() => setVisible(true)}\\n      />\\n      <Dialog\\n        header=\\"Header\\"\\n        visible={visible}\\n        onHide={() => setVisible(false)}\\n      >\\n        <p>This is PrimeReact Dialog content.</p>\\n      </Dialog>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\nexport default App;"],[1477,"The deployment of an SPA in an on-premise environment means the deployment on the company's own servers, which gives complete control over the infrastructure, the security and the configuration, but requires more resources for the support. In the cloud (AWS S3 or Vercel, for example) the hosting of static files is simpler, scales automatically and is often integrated with CI/CD. The key difference is the management of the infrastructure: on-premise requires the manual configuration of the web servers (Nginx, Apache) and of SSL, while the cloud provides managed services.","The deployment of a Single Page Application (SPA) is the process of placing the static files built (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) on a server so that users can obtain access to them. The main task is to ensure the correct handing over of index.html for every route (client-side routing) and the fast loading of the resources. The approach to the deployment depends greatly on the environment chosen: local (on-premise) or cloud.\\nThe particulars of the deployment in an on-premise environment\\nOn-premise (or \\"on your own servers\\") presupposes that the infrastructure is physically located in the company's data centre. This gives complete control, but it also imposes the obligations of support.\\n\\n- Complete control and customisation: You configure the web server (Nginx, Apache), the policies of security, the network rules and the backup yourself.\\n- The responsibility for the infrastructure: You have to service the hardware, watch the uptime, update the software and ensure the fault tolerance.\\n- The configuration for the SPA: The web server has to be configured so that every request (except the API) returns index.html. An example of an Nginx configuration:\\n\\n- Security and SSL: You generate and renew the SSL certificates yourself (through Let's Encrypt, for example).\\n- The scaling: As the load grows you have to add servers by hand and configure a balancer.\\nThe particulars of the deployment in a cloud environment\\nA cloud environment (AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, Vercel, Netlify) provides managed services, which abstract the infrastructure away.\\n\\n- Simplified hosting: Services of the AWS S3 + CloudFront or Vercel kind are intended for static sites. It is enough to upload the files, and the routing and the SSL are configured automatically.\\n- Automatic scaling: The cloud automatically copes with peak loads without your intervention.\\n- The integration with CI/CD: The platforms often have built-in pipelines. For example, on a push to a Git branch the build and the deployment can be started automatically.\\n- A global CDN: The files are cached on edge servers all over the world, which speeds the loading up for users from different regions.\\n- The management through a console or IaC: The infrastructure can be described in code (Terraform, CloudFormation), which makes it reproducible.\\nThe practical differences and an example\\nLet us look at the typical scenario of the deployment of a React application. In the cloud (Vercel) this may be a single command:\\n\\nFor on-premise the process is more complex: you have to configure the server, copy the files (through rsync, for example), check the configuration and restart the service.\\nConclusion: The choice between on-premise and the cloud depends on the requirements of control, security and budget and on the team's expertise. On-premise is suitable for strict corporate environments in which the data has to remain inside the perimeter. The cloud is optimal for a fast start, for scaling and for the lowering of the operational costs of the support of the infrastructure.","server {\\n    listen 80;\\n    server_name myapp.local;\\n    root /var/www/myapp;\\n    index index.html;\\n    location / {\\n        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# The installation of the CLI and the deployment\\nnpm i -g vercel\\nvercel --prod"],[1478,"A dynamic configuration lets you change an application's settings (API addresses, keys) without a rebuild. The main ways: the use of environment variables, the loading of a configuration JSON file from the server, and the passing of parameters through global variables in window. This makes an application portable between different environments (development, testing, production).","A dynamic configuration at the start of an SPA is a technique that lets you change an application's parameters (the URLs of the back-end services, the API keys, the settings of the functions, for example) after the process of the build, directly at the moment of the start in the target environment. This is critically important for the practices of CI/CD and of containerisation, since one and the same artefact built (a Docker image, for example) can be deployed in different environments.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Environment variables: The most widespread way. The values are substituted at the stage of the build (through Webpack's DefinePlugin, for example) or are read from the global \`process.env\` object in the Node.js environment during the build. For purely client-side code in the browser they are not available directly after the build, so they are often used at the stage of the build for the creation of a static config.\\n- The loading of an external configuration file: The application at its start (usually in \`public/index.html\` or at the point of entry) carries out an AJAX request (\`fetch\`, for example) to a static JSON file placed on the same domain (\`/config.json\`, for example). The contents of that file can be replaced on the deployment without a rebuild of the application.\\n- Global variables in the window object: The configuration can be injected into the HTML template by the server on the deployment. For example, server-side rendering or a simple script in \`index.html\` can set \`window.APP_CONFIG = {...}\`. The application then reads that variable after the loading.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at an example of the loading of the configuration from an external JSON file on the initialisation of an application in React:\\n\\nThe contents of the file \`public/config.json\` may look like this and be replaced on the deployment:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis approach is widely used in microservice architectures and in containerisation (Docker/Kubernetes). In Docker images of an SPA, for example, the statically built files are served by nginx. The configuration JSON file can be mounted into the container as a volume or generated by an entrypoint script on the basis of the container's environment variables, which ensures the complete separation of the code and the configuration.\\nConclusion: A dynamic configuration at the start is necessary for the building of genuinely portable applications corresponding to the principles of the 12-Factor App. Use the loading of an external config or the injection through \`window\` when settings have to be changed between environments without a rebuild, especially in CI/CD pipelines and in orchestrators of the Kubernetes kind.","// The application's point of entry (index.js or App.js)\\nasync function bootstrapApp() {\\n  try {\\n    // We load the config before the rendering of the root component\\n    const response = await fetch('/config.json');\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(\`Failed to load config: \${response.status}\`);\\n    }\\n    const config = await response.json();\\n\\n    // We save the config in a context, a store or a global variable\\n    window.appConfig = config;\\n\\n    // Now the application can be initialised with those settings\\n    const { API_BASE_URL, FEATURE_FLAGS } = config;\\n    console.log('API Base URL:', API_BASE_URL);\\n\\n    // We render the application, passing the config through a context\\n    ReactDOM.render(\\n      <ConfigProvider value={config}>\\n        <App />\\n      </ConfigProvider>,\\n      document.getElementById('root')\\n    );\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Could not bootstrap application:', error);\\n    // A fallback UI can be displayed\\n    document.getElementById('root').innerHTML = \\n      'Application configuration failed to load.';\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nbootstrapApp();\\n\\n{\\n  \\"API_BASE_URL\\": \\"https://api.production.example.com\\",\\n  \\"ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_FEATURES\\": false,\\n  \\"SENTRY_DSN\\": \\"https://key@o450000.ingest.sentry.io/0\\",\\n  \\"LOG_LEVEL\\": \\"warn\\"\\n}"],[1479,"Constants should be moved out into separate modules or files, grouping them by their logical purpose. Use a named export for convenience of importing. Avoid \\"magic numbers\\" and strings scattered through the code. For the application's configuration create a separate file (config.js, for example). This simplifies the changing of the values and the search for errors.","The organisation of constants is the practice of moving immutable values out of the body of the code into places specially set aside for them. This is critically important for any project that is planned to be maintained and scaled.\\nThe main principles of the organisation\\n\\n- Logical grouping: Constants relating to one subject area (API endpoints, localStorage keys, error messages, for example) should be held together.\\n- The naming: The names of constants should be descriptive and, as a rule, in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (\`MAX_RETRY_COUNT\`, for example), so as to be visually distinct from variables.\\n- A single source of truth: One and the same value should not be duplicated in different parts of the code.\\nPractical examples\\nInstead of writing \\"magic\\" strings or numbers straight in the functions, create a module of constants.\\n\\nThen import them where they are necessary:\\n\\nWhere and how to apply it\\nSuch an approach is applied everywhere: for the holding of the strings of internationalisation (i18n), the keys of actions in Redux, the colours of a design system, the sizes of the breakpoints for a responsive design and any other values that should not change during the program's execution or may change depending on the environment (development/production).\\nConclusion: The centralised holding of constants makes code cleaner, eases refactoring and global changes, and also lowers the probability of typos and logical errors. This is an obligatory practice for projects of any size.","// constants/api.js\\nexport const API_BASE_URL = 'https://api.example.com/v1';\\nexport const API_ENDPOINTS = {\\n  USERS: '/users',\\n  PRODUCTS: '/products',\\n};\\nexport const HTTP_STATUS_CODES = {\\n  OK: 200,\\n  NOT_FOUND: 404,\\n  SERVER_ERROR: 500,\\n};\\n\\n// constants/validation.js\\nexport const PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH = 8;\\nexport const EMAIL_REGEX = /^[^\\\\s@]+@[^\\\\s@]+\\\\.[^\\\\s@]+$/;\\n\\n// config.js (for the configuration of the environment)\\nexport const APP_CONFIG = {\\n  IS_PRODUCTION: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',\\n  SENTRY_DSN: process.env.REACT_APP_SENTRY_DSN,\\n  MAX_ITEMS_PER_PAGE: 20,\\n};\\n\\n// services/userService.js\\nimport { API_BASE_URL, API_ENDPOINTS } from '../constants/api';\\n\\nasync function fetchUsers() {\\n  const response = await fetch(\`\${API_BASE_URL}\${API_ENDPOINTS.USERS}\`);\\n  return response.json();\\n}"],[1480,"Besides the passing through DI (Dependency Injection) or Depends, the default values can be set directly in the function's signature, using default parameters. You can also use configuration objects or dictionaries, which are merged with the default values. Another approach is factory functions or builders, which create objects with pre-set parameters. In some frameworks the Service Locator pattern is applied for the resolution of the dependencies.","The passing of default values for arguments is a key aspect of the design of flexible interfaces of functions and classes. The direct setting of the values in the function's signature is the simplest way, but it is not always suitable for complex dependencies or configurations.\\nThe main alternatives\\n\\n- Default parameters in the function's signature: The direct specification of the value on the declaration of the parameter.\\n- Configuration objects or dictionaries: The passing of a single object containing every setting, with a subsequent merge with the default values.\\n- Factory functions or builders: Separate functions that create instances with pre-set parameters.\\n- The Service Locator pattern: A global or contextual registry from which the dependencies are extracted by name or by type.\\n- The use of currying or of the partial application of a function: The creation of a new function with part of the arguments \\"fixed\\".\\nExamples of code\\nLet us look at several approaches with Python as the example.\\n\\nThese approaches are applied in different contexts. Default parameters are good for simple cases. Configuration objects are for complex settings with a multitude of options. Factories and partial application help you create specialised versions of functions. The Service Locator is often met with in large frameworks for the management of dependencies.\\nConclusion: The choice of the alternative depends on the context: for simple values use default parameters, for the management of dependencies in large applications DI or a Service Locator, and for configurations objects or factories.","# 1. Default parameters\\ndef connect_to_db(host='localhost', port=5432):\\n    print(f'Connecting to {host}:{port}')\\n\\n# 2. A configuration dictionary with defaults\\ndef process_data(config):\\n    defaults = {'timeout': 30, 'retries': 3}\\n    final_config = {**defaults, **config}  # The merge\\n    print(final_config)\\n\\n# 3. A factory function\\ndef create_logger(level='INFO'):\\n    # ... the configuration of the logger\\n    def logger(message):\\n        print(f'[{level}] {message}')\\n    return logger\\n\\ninfo_log = create_logger()  # It uses the level 'INFO' by default\\n\\n# 4. The partial application (functools.partial)\\nfrom functools import partial\\n\\ndef send_email(to, subject, body):\\n    print(f'Sending to {to}: {subject}')\\n\\nsend_to_admin = partial(send_email, to='admin@site.com')\\nsend_to_admin(subject='Alert', body='Something happened.')"],[1481,"It is worth returning an empty result (null, an empty array, Optional.empty(), for example) when the absence of the data is an expected and normal state in the context of the business logic. An exception should be thrown when an unforeseen error, a violation of the contract or a state that the program cannot handle correctly has taken place. For example, the search for a user by ID: if the user is not found, that may be normal (we return null), but if the database is unavailable, that is an exceptional situation (we throw an exception).","The choice between the return of an empty result and the generation of an exception is a key decision in the design of reliable and comprehensible interfaces. The main goal is to report clearly to the calling code the result of the operation: success, the expected absence of data, or an error.\\n\\nWhen to return an empty result\\n\\nAn empty result (null, an empty collection, a special object of the Optional.empty kind) signals that the operation finished normally but the data sought is absent. This is part of the normal flow of the program's execution.\\n\\n- The examples: A search by an optional criterion, the obtaining of a list of elements that may be empty, the checking of the existence of an entity.\\n\\n- The advantage: It avoids the overheads of the creation and the handling of exceptions for frequent cases.\\n\\nWhen to throw an exception\\n\\nAn exception should be used for the signalling of exceptional (erroneous) situations that violate the usual flow of the execution or the method's contract.\\n\\n- The examples: A failure of the connection to the database, the passing of invalid arguments (null, for example, when an object is expected), the violation of business rules that have to be guaranteed.\\n\\n- The advantage: It lets you separate the handling of the errors from the main logic, and it often leads to cleaner code.\\n\\nA practical example in Java\\n\\nConclusion: Use the return of an empty result for the expected \\"absent\\" states within the bounds of the business logic. Apply exceptions for the handling of real errors, failures and violations of the preconditions, which require separate attention or the emergency termination of the operation.","public class UserRepository {\\n    // The return of an Optional - clear semantics: the user may be absent.\\n    public Optional<User> findUserById(Long id) {\\n        // ... the carrying out of the query to the database\\n        if (userFound) {\\n            return Optional.of(user);\\n        } else {\\n            return Optional.empty(); // The expected case: there is no user.\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // The throwing of an exception on a violation of the contract or a failure of the system.\\n    public User getUserById(Long id) throws DatabaseException {\\n        if (id == null) {\\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\\"ID cannot be null\\");\\n        }\\n        try {\\n            // ... the carrying out of the query to the database\\n            if (userFound) {\\n                return user;\\n            } else {\\n                throw new EntityNotFoundException(\\"User with id \\" + id + \\" not found\\");\\n            }\\n        } catch (SQLException e) {\\n            // An unforeseen error of the infrastructure.\\n            throw new DatabaseException(\\"Failed to fetch user\\", e);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[1482,"Specialised classes of exception let you identify the type of the error precisely. This helps you separate the handling of critical failures from expected problems in the business logic. Different handlers can react to different exceptions differently. Such an approach makes code cleaner and eases its debugging.","Exceptions are a mechanism for the handling of errors and non-standard situations in a program. The use of the built-in general exceptions, such as \`Exception\` or \`RuntimeException\`, is often insufficient, because they do not convey the sense of the specific error.\\nThe advantages of specialised exceptions\\n\\n- Precise identification: From the type of the exception you can understand at once what exactly went wrong (\`UserNotFoundException\` vs \`InsufficientFundsException\`, for example).\\n- Selective handling: You can intercept and handle only particular types of error without touching the others.\\n- Improved readability of the code: The name of the exception's class itself documents the possible problem.\\n- Safety: The temptation to intercept the overly broad \`Exception\` class, which can hide unforeseen errors, is avoided.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a simple banking service. Instead of throwing a \`RuntimeException\` with a textual message everywhere, we create specific classes.\\n\\nThis approach is applied in practically every modern language (Java, C#, Python, TypeScript) for the structuring of the errors in applications of any scale, especially in the business logic of the server-side part.\\nConclusion: Specialised classes of exception are worth applying for the clear separation of the types of error in your domain, which substantially simplifies the handling, the logging and the maintenance of the code.","// The specialised classes of exception\\nclass InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException {\\n    public InsufficientFundsException(String message) {\\n        super(message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass AccountNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {\\n    public AccountNotFoundException(String accountId) {\\n        super(\\"Account not found: \\" + accountId);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The use in the service\\npublic void transferMoney(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {\\n    Account from = accountRepository.findById(fromId);\\n    if (from == null) {\\n        throw new AccountNotFoundException(fromId); // A clear type of error\\n    }\\n    if (from.getBalance().compareTo(amount) < 0) {\\n        throw new InsufficientFundsException(\\"Insufficient funds in account: \\" + fromId);\\n    }\\n    // ... the logic of the transfer\\n}\\n\\n// The handling at the top level\\npublic void processTransfer() {\\n    try {\\n        transferMoney(\\"acc123\\", \\"acc456\\", new BigDecimal(\\"1000\\"));\\n    } catch (AccountNotFoundException e) {\\n        // Show the user the message \\"The account was not found\\"\\n        logger.warn(\\"User error: \\" + e.getMessage());\\n    } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {\\n        // Offer to top the account up\\n        logger.warn(\\"Business rule violation: \\" + e.getMessage());\\n    } catch (Exception e) {\\n        // An unforeseen error (a problem with the database, for example)\\n        logger.error(\\"Unexpected error\\", e);\\n        throw e;\\n    }\\n}"],[1483,"Constants are worth moving out into a separate module when they are used in several places in the application and their values are logically connected. This improves the readability of the code, since every key value is gathered in one place. A change of a value requires an edit in only one module, which lowers the risk of errors. Such an approach also eases the testing and the refactoring.","The moving of constants out into a separate module is a practice of the organisation of code aimed at the centralisation of the immutable values that are used in different parts of an application. This is not simply the mechanical moving of numbers and strings but a way of making code more comprehensible, more manageable and less prone to errors.\\n\\nWhen does this make sense?\\n\\n- Repeated use: If one and the same string, number or object is used in several modules, files or components.\\n\\n- Logical grouping: When the constants relate to one subject area — the settings of an API (the URL, the keys, the timeouts), the codes of statuses, the error messages or the configuration of the UI (the colours, the sizes), for example.\\n\\n- The simplification of changes: If a value may change in the future (the base URL of the API on a change of the environment, for example), it is simpler to update it in one place.\\n\\n- The raising of the readability: Named constants (\`MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS\`, for example) are more comprehensible than \\"magic numbers\\" (3, for example) scattered through the code.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at an example in JavaScript/TypeScript. Instead of hard-coding the values in different files, we create a \`constants.js\` or \`config.ts\` module.\\n\\nNow in any component or module of the application we import those constants:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nThis approach is universal and is applied in practically every paradigm of programming and in every technology stack: the front end (React, Vue, Angular), the back end (Node.js, Python, Java), and also in the configuration of the build (Webpack) and in the settings of the environment.\\n\\nConclusion: Move constants out into a separate module for values that are used in several places, are logically grouped or may change. This is a key practice for the creation of clean, maintainable and scalable code, especially in medium and large projects.","// constants/api.js\\nexport const API_BASE_URL = 'https://api.example.com/v1';\\nexport const API_TIMEOUT = 30000; // 30 seconds\\nexport const DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE = 20;\\n\\n// constants/errors.js\\nexport const ERROR_CODES = {\\n  NOT_FOUND: 404,\\n  UNAUTHORIZED: 401,\\n  SERVER_ERROR: 500\\n};\\n\\n// constants/ui.js\\nexport const COLORS = {\\n  PRIMARY: '#007bff',\\n  SUCCESS: '#28a745',\\n  DANGER: '#dc3545'\\n};\\n\\n// userService.js\\nimport { API_BASE_URL, DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE } from '../constants/api';\\n\\nasync function fetchUsers(page = 1) {\\n  const response = await fetch(\\n    \`\${API_BASE_URL}/users?page=\${page}&limit=\${DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE}\`\\n  );\\n  return response.json();\\n}"],[1484,"In JavaScript, to limit concurrency semaphores, queues or ready-made libraries are used. A semaphore is a primitive that tracks the number of tasks being carried out simultaneously. You can create a simple semaphore by means of a counter and a Promise. You can also use the pattern of a 'queue with limited parallelism', in which the tasks are placed in a queue and are carried out as the slots are freed. This is useful for work with APIs that have limits on the number of requests.","The limitation of concurrency is an important practice in work with asynchronous operations, especially when you have to carry out a multitude of tasks (network requests, for example) without overloading the target server or exhausting the system's limits (the number of open files, for example). In JavaScript, which is single-threaded, concurrency relates to the simultaneous starting of asynchronous operations that are carried out in the background, and their number has to be controlled.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- A semaphore: the classic primitive of synchronisation, which governs the access to a shared resource through a counter. In JS it can be implemented by means of a Promise and a counter of the active tasks.\\n\\n- A queue with limited parallelism: the tasks are placed in a queue, and no more than N tasks are extracted from it and started simultaneously. As soon as one task finishes, the next is started.\\n\\n- Ready-made libraries: \`p-limit\`, \`async\` (with the \`parallelLimit\` function) or \`bottleneck\` for more complex rate limiting, for example.\\n\\nAn example of the implementation of a simple semaphore\\n\\nLet us create a Semaphore class, which will track the number of permits. The \`acquire\` method returns a Promise, which will be resolved when a permit is available.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\nThe limitation of concurrency is used in:\\n\\n- The calling of external APIs with strict limits on the number of requests per second (rate limiting).\\n\\n- The parallel handling of files or data, so as not to overload the memory or the CPU.\\n\\n- Work with databases, in which too many simultaneous connections can lead to errors.\\n\\n- The scraping of web pages, so as not to be blocked because of overly frequent requests.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the limitation of concurrency when you have to carry out many asynchronous tasks but you have to control the load on the system or observe external limitations. A simple implementation through a semaphore or a queue is suitable for most cases, and for complex scenarios (distributed systems, for example) it is worth considering specialised libraries.","class Semaphore {\\n  constructor(maxConcurrency) {\\n    this.max = maxConcurrency;\\n    this.current = 0;\\n    this.queue = [];\\n  }\\n\\n  async acquire() {\\n    if (this.current < this.max) {\\n      this.current++;\\n      return Promise.resolve();\\n    }\\n    return new Promise(resolve => {\\n      this.queue.push(resolve);\\n    });\\n  }\\n\\n  release() {\\n    this.current--;\\n    if (this.queue.length > 0) {\\n      this.queue.shift()(); // We permit the next task in the queue\\n      this.current++;\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The use\\nconst sem = new Semaphore(3);\\nasync function limitedTask(id) {\\n  await sem.acquire();\\n  try {\\n    console.log(\`Task \${id} has begun\`);\\n    // The imitation of asynchronous work\\n    await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, 1000));\\n    console.log(\`Task \${id} has finished\`);\\n  } finally {\\n    sem.release();\\n  }\\n}\\n// The start of 10 tasks, but no more than 3 simultaneously\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) limitedTask(i);"],[1485,"The reading of files from a disk is not considered a CPU-bound task because the main time of the execution goes on the waiting for the input-output (I/O) rather than on the handling of the data by the processor. In CPU-bound tasks the performance is limited by the speed of the processor, whereas in the reading of files the limitation is the speed of the disk, the controller and the data bus. The processor for most of the time simply waits while the data is delivered from the store, which makes that operation I/O-bound. An understanding of this difference helps you design systems correctly, choosing asynchronous approaches for I/O operations.","The reading of files from a disk is classified as an I/O-bound operation rather than a CPU-bound one. The key difference consists in the fact that the limiting factor of the performance is not the processor's computational power but the speed of the subsystem of the storage of the data and of the mechanisms of the transfer of the data.\\n\\nThe main causes\\n\\n- The waiting for the hardware: After the sending of the request to read, the processor has to wait while the hard disk (HDD) or the solid-state drive (SSD) finds the data, reads it and transfers it through the controller and the data bus. That time is measured in milliseconds (for an HDD) or microseconds (for an SSD), which is a very long time by the processor's standards, as in that time it could have carried out millions of instructions.\\n\\n- The low load on the CPU: During the reading of a file the processor carries out no intensive computations — it either waits for an interrupt from the disk controller or switches to the execution of other tasks, if asynchronous input-output is used.\\n\\n- The separation of responsibility: Modern operating systems and drivers use DMA (Direct Memory Access), letting the data be copied straight into memory without the participation of the processor, which lowers its involvement even further.\\n\\nPractical examples and the application\\n\\nIn web development an understanding of this difference is critical for the choice of the architecture. For example, Node.js uses non-blocking I/O for the handling of a multitude of simultaneous requests to the file system or to a database, without creating separate threads for every request.\\n\\nIn Python an analogous approach is implemented through asyncio and aiofiles for asynchronous work with files, which lets a multitude of I/O operations be handled efficiently in one thread.\\n\\nA comparison with CPU-bound tasks\\n\\n- A CPU-bound example: Complex mathematical computations, the encryption of data, the rendering of graphics, the compilation of code. Here the performance depends directly on the processor's clock speed and on the number of cores.\\n\\n- An I/O-bound example: The reading and writing of files, network requests, queries to databases, the waiting for the user's input. Here the processor idles while waiting for the external devices.\\n\\nConclusion: The reading of files is an I/O-bound operation, in which the main time goes on the waiting for the data from the drive rather than on its handling by the processor. This understanding helps you choose the right approaches: asynchronous programming, caching, the use of an SSD and the optimisation of the access to the data for such operations, whereas for CPU-bound tasks the parallelisation of the computations or the optimisation of the algorithms will be more effective.","// The synchronous reading (blocking) - bad for I/O-bound operations\\nconst fs = require('fs');\\nconst data = fs.readFileSync('/path/to/large/file.txt'); // The process is blocked here\\nconsole.log(data.length);\\n\\n// The asynchronous reading (non-blocking) - correct for I/O-bound operations\\nfs.readFile('/path/to/large/file.txt', (err, data) => {\\n  if (err) throw err;\\n  console.log(data.length);\\n});\\n// The code carries on being carried out here while the file is being read\\nconsole.log('The reading of the file has begun...');"],[1486,"The change of a function's input arguments inside its body creates side effects, which makes the function's behaviour unpredictable and complicates the debugging. This violates the principle of pure functions, since the function begins to change the state of the outside world. For example, if an array passed into a function is changed, that will affect every other part of the program that uses that array. Such code is hard to test and to reason about.","The change of a function's input arguments directly is the classic example of the creation of a side effect. A function that modifies its parameters ceases to be pure, because its execution affects the program's state beyond its own bounds. This leads to several specific problems.\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Unpredictability: The code that calls the function may not expect the data passed to it to change. This leads to subtle bugs that are hard to catch, especially in large code bases or in asynchronous operations.\\n- The violation of the encapsulation: The function begins to manipulate data that does not belong to it, which contradicts the principles of modular design.\\n- The difficulty of debugging and testing: Since the system's state is changed in a hidden way, it becomes hard to track down where and why the data was modified. Pure functions, by contrast, always return the same result for the same input data, which makes them ideal for unit testing.\\n- Problems with parallel execution: In multithreaded or asynchronous environments the simultaneous modification of the same data from different functions can lead to a race condition and to the corruption of the data.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at the bad and the good practice with the example of a function that has to add tax to the prices in an array.\\n\\nWhere this is applied and how to avoid the problems\\nThe principle of immutability is a cornerstone in functional programming and is widely used in modern frameworks such as React (for state) and Redux. To avoid accidental mutations:\\n\\n- Always regard the input arguments as read-only.\\n- If the data has to be changed, create and return a new copy (use methods of the \`.map()\` or \`.filter()\` kind, the spread operator \`[...arr]\`, \`Object.assign({}, obj)\` or libraries of the Immer kind).\\n- Explicitly document the functions that by design have to change their arguments (the methods of classes that work with internal state, for example).\\nConclusion: Avoid changing a function's input arguments. This makes code more predictable, simplifies the testing and the debugging, and also prevents complex errors connected with shared state. Apply this approach wherever the reliability and the maintainability of the code matter, especially in work with collections of data and with an application's state.","// BAD: The function mutates the input argument\\nfunction addTaxBad(prices, taxRate) {\\n    for (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\\n        prices[i] = prices[i] * (1 + taxRate); // The direct change of the array!\\n    }\\n    // The function returns nothing, but the \`prices\` array in the calling code has already changed.\\n}\\n\\nconst cart = [100, 200, 300];\\naddTaxBad(cart, 0.2);\\nconsole.log(cart); // [120, 240, 360] - the original array has been irretrievably lost.\\n\\n// GOOD: The function returns a new array without touching the original\\nfunction addTaxGood(prices, taxRate) {\\n    return prices.map(price => price * (1 + taxRate)); // We use .map for the creation of a new array\\n}\\n\\nconst originalCart = [100, 200, 300];\\nconst cartWithTax = addTaxGood(originalCart, 0.2);\\nconsole.log(originalCart); // [100, 200, 300] - the original data is intact\\nconsole.log(cartWithTax);   // [120, 240, 360] - a new array with the result"],[1487,"One error message is not enough because it contains none of the context necessary for an understanding of the cause of the failure. For example, the error \\"File not found\\" will not say which file exactly, at what path and at what moment in time the program tried to open it. For effective debugging additional data is needed: a timestamp, the identifier of the user or of the session, the call stack, the values of the key variables and the state of the system. Without that context the search for and the fixing of the error turn into a long and complex task.","Logging is not simply the recording of errors but the creation of a detailed journal of the events in an application, which lets you reproduce the sequence of actions that led to the failure. One error message, \\"Division by zero\\" for example, is merely a symptom but does not explain the cause.\\n\\nWhy the context is critically important\\n\\nWithout context an error message loses its practical value. Let us look at an example: in a web application an error of validation takes place. The message \\"Invalid input\\" tells the developer nothing. A log containing the context, however, points to the problem at once:\\n\\n- The timestamp: When did the error take place?\\n\\n- The level of the logging: Is this an error, a warning or an informational message?\\n\\n- The identifiers: The ID of the user, the ID of the session, the ID of the request (the correlation ID). This lets you track every action of a specific user.\\n\\n- The stack trace: It shows the precise place in the code at which the error arose and the chain of the calls of the functions.\\n\\n- The state of the system: The values of the variables, the parameters of the request, the data of the configuration.\\n\\n- The user's actions: What steps preceded the error.\\n\\nAn example of bad and of good logging\\n\\nLet us compare two approaches with the simple example of a function for the handling of a request:\\n\\nIn the second case, on receiving an alert about the error, the developer can find every record with the given \`requestId\` in the logs at once and see the complete picture: when the handling began, what data was in the order and at what moment everything went wrong.\\n\\nWhere structured logging is applied\\n\\nSuch an approach is necessary in distributed systems (microservices), in which one request passes through a multitude of components. Without an end-to-end identifier (a correlation ID) it is impossible to track its path. Contextual logging is also used for:\\n\\n- The analysis of incidents and post-mortems.\\n\\n- The monitoring of the application's health (observability).\\n\\n- The gathering of business metrics (the number of successful transactions, for example).\\n\\n- The audit of users' actions (especially important in finance and healthcare).\\n\\nConclusion: One error message is not enough because it gives no context for the analysis. Effective logging should always include identifiers, timestamps, the call stack and the relevant state of the system. This is especially critical in a production environment for the fast diagnosis and solution of problems.","// BAD: Only the error message\\nfunction processOrder(orderId) {\\n  try {\\n    // ... the logic\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\\"Failed to process order\\");\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: Logging with context\\nfunction processOrder(orderId, userId) {\\n  const requestId = generateRequestId(); // A unique ID for this request\\n  logger.info({ requestId, orderId, userId }, \\"Starting order processing\\");\\n  \\n  try {\\n    // ... the logic\\n    logger.debug({ requestId, itemCount: order.items.length }, \\"Order details\\");\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    // We log the error with all of the context\\n    logger.error({\\n      requestId,\\n      orderId,\\n      userId,\\n      error: error.message,\\n      stack: error.stack,\\n      additionalInfo: { paymentMethod: order.paymentMethod }\\n    }, \\"Failed to process order\\");\\n    throw error;\\n  }\\n}"],[1488,"Base filters are added to the user's ones through composition or the 'Decorator' pattern. First the base logic of the filtering is defined — by a range of dates, for example. Then the user's filters are applied on top, combining the conditions through 'AND' or 'OR'. This lets you preserve the standard limitations and add flexible settings. It is implemented through a chain of functions or a wrapper object, which applies every filter in sequence.","The addition of base (system) filters to the user's ones is a task of the extension of the functionality of filtering, often met with in the interfaces of admin panels, reports or APIs. The base filters ensure the obligatory limitations — the filtering by the user's organisation or by a period of time, for example — while the user's filters provide the flexibility for the configuration of the queries.\\nThe main approaches\\nThere are two main architectural approaches:\\n\\n- The composition of functions (or predicates): Every filter is a function taking the data (or the criteria of the query) and returning the filtered result. The base and the user's filters are combined into a chain, in which the result of one is passed to the next.\\n- The \\"Decorator\\" pattern: A base class or object is created, implementing the interface of a filter. Then it is \\"wrapped\\" in decorators, each of which adds its own logic of filtering. This is especially useful when the filters have complex state or have to be applied in a particular order.\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\nLet us look at a simple example of the composition of functions for the filtering of an array of user objects.\\n\\nIn this example the base filters (\`filterByActive\`, \`filterByOrg\`) set the obligatory conditions, while the user's one (\`filterByName\`) adds an additional refinement. The order of the application may matter: usually the base filters are applied first in order to cut the volume of the data.\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis approach is widely used in:\\n\\n- Back-end development: The building of queries to a database (in an ORM of the Sequelize or TypeORM kind, for example), in which conditions from the API's parameters are added to the base query (WHERE org_id = X).\\n- Front-end development: The filtering of data in tables (in React components, for example), in which there are fixed filters (the default sorting) and dynamic ones chosen by the user.\\n- Systems of reporting and BI tools: The global filters (the period, for example) are combined with the user's detailed settings.\\nConclusion: Base filters should be added to the user's ones through composition or a decorator, which ensures the observance of the system's limitations and provides flexibility. This approach is worth applying wherever the obligatory business logic and the conditions of the selection of data configurable by the user have to be separated.","// The base filters (the system ones)\\nconst filterByActive = (users) => users.filter(u => u.isActive);\\nconst filterByOrg = (orgId) => (users) => users.filter(u => u.orgId === orgId);\\n\\n// The user's filter (dynamic)\\nconst filterByName = (name) => (users) => users.filter(u => u.name.includes(name));\\n\\n// The function for the composition of the filters\\nconst applyFilters = (data, filters) => {\\n    return filters.reduce((result, filterFn) => filterFn(result), data);\\n};\\n\\n// An example of the use\\nconst allUsers = [\\n    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', isActive: true, orgId: 101 },\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', isActive: false, orgId: 101 },\\n    { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', isActive: true, orgId: 102 }\\n];\\n\\n// We create the chain: first the base ones, then the user's\\nconst filters = [\\n    filterByActive,               // Base: only the active ones\\n    filterByOrg(101),             // Base: only organisation 101\\n    filterByName('A')             // The user's: the name contains 'A'\\n];\\n\\nconst filteredUsers = applyFilters(allUsers, filters);\\nconsole.log(filteredUsers); // [{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', ... }]"],[1489,"In work with a network delays (latency) arise because of the distance, the routing and the load on the servers. The instability shows itself as the loss of packets (packet loss) or breaks in the connections, which leads to timeouts of the requests. This affects the application's responsiveness and requires the handling of errors — repeated attempts (retries) or the display of the state of the loading, for example. Developers have to design systems with those problems taken into account in order to ensure the reliability.","Network interaction is the foundation of modern distributed applications, but it is subject to a number of problems that directly affect the user experience and the reliability of the system. The main ones are delays (latency) and the instability of the connection.\\nDelays (Latency)\\nA delay is the time within which data passes from the sender to the recipient. It arises because of:\\n\\n- The physical distance: the signal needs time to overcome the kilometres, even at the speed of light.\\n- The routing: the data passes through a multitude of routers, each of which adds a small delay.\\n- The congestion of the channels: under a high load the packets may stand in queues.\\n- The handling on the server: the time of the generation of the response by the server-side application.\\nA high delay makes an application \\"sluggish\\". In a web application, for example, the user will wait a long time for the loading of the data.\\nThe instability of the connection\\nA network is unreliable by its nature. This shows itself as:\\n\\n- The loss of packets (Packet Loss): packets may be discarded because of congestion or of errors in the channel of communication.\\n- Breaks in the connections: a mobile user may enter a tunnel, or the server may restart.\\n- The change of the order of the packets (out-of-order delivery): the packets may arrive not in the order in which they were sent.\\nThese problems lead to timeouts of the requests, to errors and to the partial loss of data.\\nPractical examples and approaches to the solution\\nIn code this means that any network call has to handle the possible failures. Let us look at an example in JavaScript with the use of \`fetch\`:\\n\\nIn this example we implement:\\n1. A timeout: we abort the request if it lasts longer than 5 seconds.\\n2. Repeated attempts (a retry): on a failure we make up to 3 attempts with an exponential delay.\\n3. The handling of HTTP errors: we check the response's status.\\n4. The management of the UI's state: we show a spinner and error messages for the user.\\nAt the architectural level, to combat the delays people use:\\n- The caching of the data on the client (localStorage, a Service Worker) or on edge servers (a CDN).\\n- Optimistic updates (an Optimistic UI): the interface is updated at once and the request is sent in the background.\\n- Pagination and lazy loading, so as not to load everything at once.\\nConclusion: Taking the problems of the network into account is an obligatory part of the development of client-server applications. Apply timeouts, mechanisms of repeated attempts, caching and comprehensible handling of errors in the UI, so that the application remains responsive and robust in conditions of a less than ideal internet connection.","async function fetchDataWithRetry(url, maxRetries = 3) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++) {\\n    try {\\n      // We set a sensible timeout for the request\\n      const controller = new AbortController();\\n      const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);\\n\\n      const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });\\n      clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n\\n      if (!response.ok) {\\n        throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${response.status}\`);\\n      }\\n      return await response.json();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      // We log the error if this is not the last attempt\\n      console.warn(\`Attempt \${i + 1} failed:\`, error.message);\\n      if (i === maxRetries - 1) {\\n        throw new Error(\`Failed after \${maxRetries} retries: \${error.message}\`);\\n      }\\n      // An exponential delay before the repeated attempt\\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000 * Math.pow(2, i)));\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The use with the handling of the UI's state\\nasync function loadUserData() {\\n  showLoadingSpinner();\\n  try {\\n    const data = await fetchDataWithRetry('/api/user');\\n    displayData(data);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    showErrorMessage('The data could not be loaded. Check the connection.');\\n  } finally {\\n    hideLoadingSpinner();\\n  }\\n}"],[1490,"To iterate over a Map in JavaScript you can use several methods. The most widespread is the for...of loop, which lets you obtain the [key, value] pairs directly. You can also use the map.forEach() method, passing a callback function into it. If you need only the keys or only the values, you can first obtain the iterators through map.keys() or map.values() and then pass over them.","A Map in JavaScript is a collection of key-value pairs in which the keys can be of any type (unlike in ordinary objects). For the iteration over its elements there are several convenient ways.\\n1. The for...of loop with destructuring\\nThis method lets you obtain access to both the key and the value in every iteration. A Map is an iterable object, and with the use of for...of it returns [key, value] pairs.\\n\\n2. The forEach() method\\nA Map also provides the familiar forEach method, analogous to arrays'. It takes a callback function, into which the value, the key and the Map collection itself are passed.\\n\\n3. The keys(), values() and entries() iterators\\nYou can obtain iterators for the individual parts of the collection:\\n\\n- \`map.keys()\` — it returns an iterator over the keys.\\n- \`map.values()\` — it returns an iterator over the values.\\n- \`map.entries()\` — it returns an iterator over the [key, value] pairs (it is used by default in for...of).\\nThese iterators can be used with for...of or converted into an array by means of Array.from().\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\nThe iteration over a Map is often needed in the handling of data in which a guaranteed sequential order of the elements matters (the order in a Map corresponds to the order of the addition). This is useful in caching, in configurations and in the holding of related data with non-string keys.\\nConclusion: Use for...of for the simple iteration over every pair, forEach for the functional style, and the keys()/values() methods when you need only the keys or the values. The choice of the method depends on the specific task and on the style of the code.","const myMap = new Map([\\n  ['name', 'Alice'],\\n  [42, 'The Answer'],\\n  [{ id: 1 }, 'Object Key']\\n]);\\n\\nfor (const [key, value] of myMap) {\\n  console.log(key, '->', value);\\n}\\n// The output:\\n// 'name' -> 'Alice'\\n// 42 -> 'The Answer'\\n// { id: 1 } -> 'Object Key'\\n\\nmyMap.forEach((value, key) => {\\n  console.log(\`The key \${key} corresponds to the value \${value}\`);\\n});\\n\\n// Obtain every key in the form of an array\\nconst allKeys = Array.from(myMap.keys());\\nconsole.log(allKeys); // ['name', 42, { id: 1 }]\\n\\n// The iteration over the values only\\nfor (const val of myMap.values()) {\\n  console.log(val);\\n}"],[1491,"HTTP statuses are three-digit codes that the server sends to the client in response to its request. They are grouped by the first digit: 1xx are informational, 2xx are success, 3xx are redirection, 4xx are the client's error, 5xx are the server's error. For example, 200 OK means the successful carrying out of the request, 404 Not Found means the resource was not found, and 500 Internal Server Error means an error on the server's side. An understanding of these codes helps you quickly diagnose problems in web applications and APIs.","HTTP statuses (or codes of state) are an inalienable part of the HTTP protocol. They report to the client (the browser or another application, for example) the result of the server's handling of its request. Every code consists of three digits, of which the first determines the class of the response.\\nThe classes of HTTP statuses\\n\\n- 1xx (Informational): They indicate that the request has been received and the handling continues. An example: 100 Continue.\\n- 2xx (Successful): The request has been handled successfully. The best known is 200 OK.\\n- 3xx (Redirections): They require the client to carry out additional actions to finish the request — to navigate to another address, for example (301 Moved Permanently).\\n- 4xx (The client's errors): The request contains an error or cannot be carried out. For example, 400 Bad Request (an incorrect syntax) or 404 Not Found (the resource was not found).\\n- 5xx (The server's errors): The server could not handle a correct request. The classic example is 500 Internal Server Error.\\nThe practical application and examples\\nIn the development of a REST API the correct use of the statuses is critically important. It makes an API predictable and convenient to use. For example, on the creation of a resource you should return 201 Created, and on the unsuccessful validation of the data from the client 422 Unprocessable Entity or 400 Bad Request.\\nHere is an example of the handling on the server (Node.js with Express):\\n\\nConclusion: A knowledge and the correct application of HTTP statuses is necessary for the building of reliable, fault-tolerant web services and APIs comprehensible to their consumers. They are a key tool for the standardised communication between the components of distributed systems.","app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {\\n  const user = findUserById(req.params.id);\\n  if (!user) {\\n    // The client requested a non-existent resource\\n    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });\\n  }\\n  // The successful response with the data\\n  res.status(200).json(user);\\n});\\n\\napp.post('/api/users', (req, res) => {\\n  const newUser = createUser(req.body);\\n  if (newUser) {\\n    // The resource has been created successfully\\n    res.status(201).json(newUser);\\n  } else {\\n    // An error on the server's side in the creation\\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to create user' });\\n  }\\n});"],[1492,"An immutable class is a class whose state cannot be changed after the creation of the object. For that every field has to be declared as final and private, and the class as final, so that it cannot be inherited from. The constructor has to initialise every field, and there should be no setters. If the class contains mutable objects (collections, for example), you have to return their defensive copies in the getters and not preserve references to the external mutable objects passed into the constructor.","An immutable class is a class whose instances cannot change their internal state after the creation. This is a key concept for the writing of thread-safe, predictable code that is simple to debug, since the object is guaranteed to remain in one and the same state throughout its whole lifecycle.\\nThe main rules of the creation of an immutable class\\n\\n- Declare the class as \`final\`, in order to prevent inheritance and the overriding of the methods.\\n- Declare every field as \`private\` and \`final\`, so that they can be initialised only in the constructor.\\n- Provide no setter methods for the changing of the fields.\\n- If the class contains references to mutable objects (collections, arrays, other non-final classes, for example), you have to ensure a deep protection:\\n- In the constructor create copies of the mutable objects passed in from outside rather than preserving references to them.\\n- In the getters that return such objects, return their copies (a defensive copy) rather than the original references.\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nIn this example, even if the external code changes the original \`hobbies\` list passed into the constructor, or obtains the list through the getter, the internal state of the \`ImmutablePerson\` object will remain unchanged.\\nWhere immutable classes are applied\\nImmutable objects are widely used in multithreaded programming, since they are thread-safe by their nature (they require no synchronisation). They are ideal candidates for keys in a \`HashMap\` or elements in a \`Set\`, since their hash code does not change. They are also often applied in functional programming and as DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) for the passing of data between the layers of an application.\\nConclusion: Create immutable classes when you need objects with a constant, reliable state, especially in a multithreaded environment, for the keys of collections or for the simplification of a program's logic through the absence of side effects.","import java.util.*;\\n\\npublic final class ImmutablePerson {\\n    private final String name;\\n    private final int age;\\n    private final List<String> hobbies;\\n\\n    // The constructor with defensive copying\\n    public ImmutablePerson(String name, int age, List<String> hobbies) {\\n        this.name = name;\\n        this.age = age;\\n        // We create a copy of the list passed in\\n        this.hobbies = new ArrayList<>(hobbies);\\n    }\\n\\n    // The getters\\n    public String getName() { return name; }\\n    public int getAge() { return age; }\\n    // We return a copy of the list rather than the original\\n    public List<String> getHobbies() {\\n        return new ArrayList<>(hobbies);\\n    }\\n}"],[1493,"HTTP methods (or verbs) define the action that the client wants to carry out on a resource on the server. The main methods: GET for the obtaining of data, POST for the creation of a new resource, PUT for the complete updating of an existing resource, DELETE for its deletion. Additional methods, such as PATCH (a partial update), HEAD (the obtaining of the headers) and OPTIONS (the querying of the methods supported), extend the protocol's capabilities. An understanding of these methods is the foundation for work with web APIs.","HTTP methods, also called verbs, are a key part of the HTTP protocol and define the type of the operation that the client requests of the server for a specific resource (a web page or a user's data, for example). Their correct use lies at the foundation of the REST architectural style, which is widely applied in the building of web services and APIs.\\nThe main methods\\nThe following methods are used most often:\\n\\n- GET: It requests a representation of the resource. It should be safe (it should not change the server's state) and idempotent (repeated requests give the same result). It is used for the reading of data.\\n- POST: It is used for the sending of data to the server, often for the creation of a new resource. It is neither safe nor idempotent. The repeated sending of one and the same POST request may create several resources.\\n- PUT: It is used for the complete updating of the resource at the URI specified. If the resource does not exist, the server may create it. The PUT method is idempotent: the repeated carrying out of one and the same request leaves the resource in the same state.\\n- DELETE: It deletes the resource specified. It is also idempotent.\\nThe other important methods\\n\\n- PATCH: It is applied for the partial updating of a resource. Unlike PUT, only the fields being changed are sent.\\n- HEAD: It is analogous to GET, but the server returns only the response's headers without the body. It is useful for the checking of the presence of a resource or of its metadata.\\n- OPTIONS: It queries the server about the methods supported for the resource specified.\\n- CONNECT, TRACE: They are used more rarely, for the establishment of a tunnel or for diagnosis.\\nAn example of the use in code\\nHere is an example in JavaScript with the use of the Fetch API, demonstrating the different methods:\\n\\nIn the context of a RESTful API the resources (\`/users\`, for example) are identified through a URI, while the HTTP methods define the actions on them. This ensures a uniform and predictable interface.\\nConclusion: An understanding and the correct application of the HTTP methods is critically important for the development of clean, comprehensible and efficient web APIs, especially in the following of the principles of REST. GET and POST are used most often, but for a full CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) PUT/PATCH and DELETE are also necessary.","// A GET request for the obtaining of the list of users\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));\\n\\n// A POST request for the creation of a new user\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 })\\n});\\n\\n// A PUT request for the complete updating of the user with id=1\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users/1', {\\n  method: 'PUT',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Bob', age: 25 })\\n});\\n\\n// A DELETE request for the deletion of the user with id=1\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users/1', { method: 'DELETE' });"],[1494,"PUT is used for the complete updating of a resource, replacing all of its fields with new ones. PATCH is applied for a partial update, changing only the fields specified. PUT is idempotent: a repeated identical request gives one result. PATCH is not always idempotent, since the result depends on the resource's current state. The choice of the method depends on the task: a complete replacement or a pinpoint change.","The HTTP methods PUT and PATCH are used for the updating of data on a server, but they do it differently. An understanding of their differences is critical for the creation of predictable and correct RESTful APIs.\\n\\nThe complete update by means of PUT\\n\\nThe PUT method is intended for the complete updating of a resource. The client has to send the complete representation of the resource even if only one field is changing. The server replaces the whole resource with the data provided. If the resource does not exist, PUT often creates it (although that depends on the API's implementation).\\n\\nThe key property of PUT is idempotence. This means that the carrying out of one and the same PUT request several times in a row will lead to the same result as carrying it out once. This makes PUT reliable for operations that may be repeated because of network failures.\\n\\nThe partial update by means of PATCH\\n\\nThe PATCH method is intended for the partial updating of a resource. The client sends only the fields that have to be changed and the instructions for their change. This is more efficient, especially for large resources.\\n\\nPATCH does not guarantee idempotence by default. The result depends on the resource's current state and on the content of the request. For example, a PATCH request that increments a counter by 1 will give a different result on a repeat. If, however, formats that describe specific changes are used (JSON Patch, for example), idempotence can be achieved.\\n\\nWhere and what to apply?\\n\\n- Use PUT when you have to guarantee that after the update the resource will look exactly as specified in the request. In the saving of a user's settings from a form in which every field is displayed, for example.\\n\\n- Use PATCH when you have to update one or two fields of a large object (the status of an order or a user's avatar, for example). This reduces the traffic and prevents the accidental overwriting of data that was not sent.\\n\\nConclusion: PUT is a tool for the complete and predictable replacement of a resource, ideally suited for idempotent operations. PATCH is a tool for pinpoint, efficient changes, but it requires the careful design of the format of the request in order to ensure the reliability. The choice depends on the specific scenario of the updating of the data in your API.","// An example of a PUT request for the updating of the user with id=1\\nPUT /api/users/1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\n  \\"id\\": 1,\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Ivan Ivanov\\", // A completely new name\\n  \\"email\\": \\"ivan@example.com\\", // A completely new email\\n  \\"age\\": 30 // A completely new age\\n}\\n// Every previous field of the user will be replaced with this object.\\n\\n// An example of a PATCH request for the updating of only the user's email\\nPATCH /api/users/1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\n  \\"email\\": \\"new.ivan@example.com\\"\\n}\\n// Only the email field will change; the name and the age will remain as they were.\\n\\n// An example with the JSON Patch format (RFC 6902) for an idempotent change\\nPATCH /api/users/1\\nContent-Type: application/json-patch+json\\n\\n[\\n  { \\"op\\": \\"replace\\", \\"path\\": \\"/email\\", \\"value\\": \\"new.ivan@example.com\\" }\\n]"],[1495,"The main ways of authentication through HTTP include Basic Auth, a Bearer Token (often a JWT), OAuth/OAuth2 and authentication through sessions with cookies. Basic Auth passes the login and the password in a header in Base64 encoding, which is insecure without HTTPS. A Bearer Token uses a token, a JWT for example, which the client sends in the Authorization header. OAuth lets you delegate the access through third-party providers. Sessions hold the state on the server, while the identifier of the session is passed in the browser's cookies.","Authentication in HTTP is the process of the checking of the authenticity of a user or a client before the granting of access to protected resources. Since HTTP is a stateless protocol, various mechanisms are used for the maintenance of the user's \\"session\\" between the requests, which can be divided into several main types.\\n1. Basic Authentication\\nThis is the simplest method, defined in the HTTP standard. The client sends the login and the password, joined by a colon and encoded in Base64, in the \`Authorization\` header. The server decodes the string and checks the credentials.\\n\\nThe disadvantages: The credentials are passed in the open (Base64 is not encryption), so the method is absolutely insecure without HTTPS. It is suitable for simple internal systems or APIs in which additional encryption at the transport level is used.\\n2. Authentication through a Bearer Token (token-based)\\nIn this approach, after the successful checking of the credentials (through a log-in form, for example), the server issues the client a token — usually a JSON Web Token (JWT). The client then sends that token in every subsequent request in the \`Authorization\` header with the \`Bearer\` prefix.\\n\\nThe server checks the token's signature and extracts the data about the user from its payload. The main advantage is the absence of state on the server (stateless), which scales well. The tokens have a lifetime and can carry additional claims within them.\\n3. OAuth / OAuth 2.0\\nOAuth 2.0 is a protocol of authorisation, which is often used for authentication through third-party providers (\\"Log in through Google\\", for example). It lets an application obtain limited access to a user's data on another service without disclosing the password. The process includes the obtaining of an access token, which is then used analogously to a Bearer Token.\\n\\n- The user is redirected to the provider's page (Google, GitHub).\\n- After the consent the provider redirects back with a code.\\n- The application exchanges the code for an access token.\\nThis is the standard for integrations between services and for single sign-on (SSO) solutions.\\n4. Session-based authentication\\nThe traditional approach for web applications. The user enters their login and password, the server creates a unique identifier of the session and saves it in a store (a database, Redis). That identifier (the session ID) is sent to the client in a cookie (usually with the HttpOnly flag, for security). The browser automatically attaches that cookie to every request to the same domain.\\n\\nThe server, having received the sessionId from the cookie, finds the user's related data in its store. This approach is stateful, since the server has to hold the state of the session.\\nA practical example of the choice of a method\\nFor a mobile API or a microservice architecture a JWT (Bearer Token) is often chosen, because of its stateless nature. For a traditional website with server-side rendering sessions and cookies are convenient. For the integration with social networks or with a corporate SSO, OAuth 2.0. Basic Auth is sometimes used for simple scripts or for internal APIs in protected networks.\\nConclusion: The choice of the way of authentication depends on the type of the application (web, mobile, API), on the requirements of security and scalability and on the need for integration with external systems. For modern SPAs and APIs the combination of a JWT + HTTPS is popular, and for classic server-side applications sessions with protected cookies.","// An example of the header in a request\\nAuthorization: Basic dXNlcjpwYXNzd29yZA==\\n// where dXNlcjpwYXNzd29yZA== is the Base64 of \\"user:password\\"\\n\\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\\n\\n// The HTTP response from the server sets a cookie\\nSet-Cookie: sessionId=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict"],[1496,"The elements of a WeakMap are deleted by the garbage collector when there are no longer any strong references to their keys from other parts of the program. Unlike a Map, a WeakMap holds the keys as weak references, which does not prevent the collection of garbage. This is useful for the holding of metadata or caches connected with objects whose lifetime you do not control. When a key object becomes unreachable, the corresponding key-value pair is automatically deleted from the WeakMap.","A WeakMap is a collection in JavaScript that lets you hold key-value pairs in which the keys can only be objects. Its main particular feature is the holding of weak references to the keys. This means that the presence of a key in a WeakMap does not retain that object in memory.\\n\\nHow the collection of garbage works with a WeakMap\\n\\nThe garbage collector in JavaScript periodically frees memory, deleting the objects that are no longer reachable from the program's root (the global object, for example). If an object is a key in a WeakMap but there are no longer any other (strong) references to it, the garbage collector can delete that object. After the deletion of the key object the corresponding key-value pair automatically disappears from the WeakMap.\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- The holding of private data: The binding of hidden metadata to DOM objects or to third-party libraries without an effect on their lifetime.\\n\\n- Caching: The creation of a cache in which the entries are automatically cleared on the deletion of the main object.\\n\\n- The association of temporary data: The holding of state for objects that may be deleted at any moment, for example.\\n\\nAn example of code\\n\\nConclusion: Use a WeakMap when you have to associate additional data with objects whose lifetime is managed from outside (DOM nodes or objects from third-party libraries, for example) and you want to avoid memory leaks. This is the ideal tool for scenarios in which the data has to be cleared automatically together with the key object.","// We create an object and a WeakMap\\nlet obj = { id: 1 };\\nconst weakMap = new WeakMap();\\n\\n// We save the object as the key\\nweakMap.set(obj, 'secret data');\\nconsole.log(weakMap.has(obj)); // true\\n\\n// We delete the only strong reference to the object\\nobj = null;\\n\\n// Now the garbage collector can delete the object.\\n// The key-value pair will be deleted from the WeakMap.\\n// This cannot be checked directly, since a WeakMap\\n// has no methods for iteration or for the obtaining of the size."],[1497,"On an attempt to assign a new value directly to a variable declared through const, a TypeError: Assignment to constant variable will arise. This happens because const creates an immutable binding to the value. It matters to remember, however, that if the value of the const is an object or an array, then its internal properties or elements can be changed (mutated), since it is the reference itself that is immutable rather than the contents.","The \`const\` keyword in JavaScript is used for the declaration of variables that cannot be reassigned. This means that after the initialisation you cannot assign a new value to the variable. An attempt to do so will lead to an error at run time.\\n\\nThe direct assignment of a new value\\n\\nThe simplest case is an attempt to change a primitive value or to assign a completely new object to the variable.\\n\\nThe interpreter will stop the execution at that line and throw an error.\\n\\nThe mutation of objects and arrays\\n\\nThe concept of \`const\` protects against a change of the reference but not against a change of the contents of the object that that reference points to. This often causes confusion.\\n\\nThe same thing works for arrays:\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- Protection against accidental reassignment: Use \`const\` by default for every variable whose value should not change within the bounds of the current scope. This makes code more predictable.\\n\\n- Work with objects: For the complete immutability of an object (the prohibition of a change of its properties) you have to use additional methods, such as \`Object.freeze()\`, or work through libraries (Immutable.js).\\n\\nConclusion: Use \`const\` for the declaration of variables whose reference to a value remains constant. This is good practice, which prevents a multitude of errors. If you have to change a variable's value, use \`let\`. Remember that \`const\` does not make objects immutable but merely protects the variable itself from reassignment.","const myConst = 10;\\nmyConst = 20; // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.\\n\\nconst myObject = { name: 'Alice' };\\nmyObject.name = 'Bob'; // This is permitted! The object has been mutated.\\nconsole.log(myObject.name); // 'Bob'\\n\\nmyObject = { name: 'Charlie' }; // TypeError! An attempt to change the reference itself.\\n\\nconst myArray = [1, 2, 3];\\nmyArray.push(4); // Permitted. The array is now [1, 2, 3, 4]\\nmyArray[0] = 99; // Permitted. The array is now [99, 2, 3, 4]\\nmyArray = []; // TypeError! An attempt to assign a new reference."],[1498,"On an attempt to obtain access to an object's property, JavaScript first searches for it in the object itself. If the property is not found, the search continues in its prototype (the __proto__ property), then in the prototype of the prototype and so on, until it reaches the end of the chain (null). This mechanism ensures the inheritance of properties and methods. If the property is not found in the whole chain, undefined is returned.","In JavaScript objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects through the mechanism of prototypes. Every object has an internal reference to another object, called its prototype. On a reference to a property or a method the engine carries out a search along the chain of prototypes.\\nHow the search works\\nThe process takes place in the following way:\\n\\n- The engine checks whether the property exists in the object itself (its own properties).\\n- If not, it moves on to the object's prototype (through the \`__proto__\` property or \`Object.getPrototypeOf()\`).\\n- The search continues recursively along the chain of prototypes until the property is found.\\n- If the end of the chain is reached (the prototype is equal to \`null\`), \`undefined\` is returned.\\nAn example of code\\nLet us look at a simple hierarchy:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe chain of prototypes lies at the foundation of inheritance in JavaScript. It is used:\\n\\n- In the classic prototypal inheritance before the appearance of the ES6 classes.\\n- The built-in objects (arrays and strings, for example) inherit methods from their prototypes (Array.prototype, String.prototype).\\n- In the creation of user hierarchies of objects for the reuse of code.\\nIt matters to understand that a change of a prototype dynamically affects every object that inherits from it, which can be both a powerful tool and a source of errors.\\nConclusion: An understanding of the chain of prototypes is necessary for effective work with object-oriented programming in JavaScript, for debugging and for the creation of scalable structures of code. This mechanism is especially useful in the building of hierarchies of objects without the use of classes.","// The base prototype object\\nconst animal = {\\n  eats: true,\\n  walk() {\\n    console.log('Animal walks');\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// We create an object inheriting from animal\\nconst rabbit = {\\n  jumps: true,\\n  __proto__: animal // We establish the prototype\\n};\\n\\n// rabbit inherits the properties of animal\\nconsole.log(rabbit.eats); // true, the property was found in the prototype\\nconsole.log(rabbit.jumps); // true, its own property\\nrabbit.walk(); // 'Animal walks', the method was found in the prototype\\n\\n// The search along the chain: if the property is absent\\nconsole.log(rabbit.nonExistent); // undefined, the chain: rabbit -> animal -> Object.prototype -> null"],[1499,"The catch method in a Promise is carried out when the promise passes into the rejected state. This happens if reject() is called inside the promise or an unhandled error (an exception) arises. catch also intercepts the errors thrown in the chain of thens before it. If the promise finishes successfully (resolve), the catch block is skipped and the execution continues in the next then.","Promises in JavaScript are a mechanism for work with asynchronous operations. They can be in one of three states: pending, fulfilled (carried out successfully) and rejected (carried out with an error). The \`.catch()\` method is a special handler, which is called precisely on the passing of the promise into the rejected state.\\n\\nThe main conditions of the firing of catch\\n\\n- The explicit call of reject(): When the \`reject\` function is called inside the promise's executor function.\\n\\n- An unhandled exception (a throw): If in the body of the promise or in a synchronous \`then\` function an error arises that is not intercepted by a try...catch.\\n\\n- The failure of a previous promise in the chain: \`catch\` intercepts the errors from any promise situated higher up in the chain of the calls.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nLet us look at code demonstrating the different scenarios:\\n\\nIt matters to remember that \`catch\` returns a new promise. This lets you build long chains of handling, in which after the correction of an error the execution can be continued.\\n\\nConclusion: Use \`.catch()\` for the centralised handling of every possible error in asynchronous operations. This makes code cleaner and prevents the \\"silent\\" falls of an application. Always add a \`catch\` at the end of a chain of promises, so that no error remains unhandled.","// 1. An explicit reject\\nconst promise1 = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    reject(new Error('Something went wrong'));\\n});\\npromise1.catch(err => console.log('Scenario 1:', err.message));\\n\\n// 2. An unhandled error\\nconst promise2 = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    throw new Error('A synchronous error');\\n});\\npromise2.catch(err => console.log('Scenario 2:', err.message));\\n\\n// 3. An error in a chain of thens\\nPromise.resolve()\\n    .then(() => {\\n        throw new Error('An error in the then');\\n    })\\n    .catch(err => console.log('Scenario 3:', err.message)); // It will fire\\n\\n// 4. A successful execution - the catch is ignored\\nPromise.resolve('Success')\\n    .then(msg => console.log(msg))\\n    .catch(err => console.log('This will not be carried out'))\\n    .then(() => console.log('The chain continues'));"],[1500,"Yes, a cookie can be obtained through JavaScript, but with limitations. The main way is the use of the \`document.cookie\` property. It returns a string with every cookie available to the current origin (the domain and the path) that is not marked as HttpOnly. Cookies with the HttpOnly flag are protected from access through JavaScript and can be passed only over HTTP(S). This is an important measure of security for the protection of sensitive data such as session tokens.","Web applications often use cookies for the holding of small fragments of data on the client side, such as the identifiers of a session or the user's settings. JavaScript provides an interface for the interaction with that data through the \`document.cookie\` property.\\n\\nHow document.cookie works\\n\\nOn the reading of \`document.cookie\` a string is returned containing every cookie available to the current page, in the format \`name=value\`, separated by semicolons. It matters to understand that the access is limited by the Same-Origin Policy. JavaScript can read and write only those cookies that belong to the current domain and path and that do not have the \`HttpOnly\` flag set.\\n\\nAn example of the reading and the writing of a cookie\\n\\nThe security and the HttpOnly flag\\n\\nThe \`HttpOnly\` flag is a critically important attribute of security. When a cookie is marked as HttpOnly (this is usually done by the server on the setting of the \`Set-Cookie\` header), the browser forbids any access to it through JavaScript. This protects the cookie from theft through cross-site scripting (XSS). Therefore, although JavaScript can read many cookies, the most important ones (the identifiers of a session, for example) often remain unavailable.\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- The holding of the user's preferences: The theme of the interface or the settings of the language, for example, which can safely be held in client-side cookies.\\n\\n- The tracking of state: Simple flags or tokens for analytics (with the regulatory requirements, such as the GDPR, taken into account).\\n\\n- Work with legacy systems: Some old applications may rely on client-side cookies for the passing of data between pages.\\n\\nConclusion: Use \`document.cookie\` for work with non-critical data on the client, but never hold confidential information in cookies available to JavaScript — for that apply the HttpOnly flag and server-side sessions.","// The reading of every cookie available\\nconst allCookies = document.cookie;\\nconsole.log(allCookies); // The output: \\"sessionId=abc123; theme=dark\\"\\n\\n// The setting of a new cookie\\n// We specify the name, the value and additional attributes, such as the term of validity (expires) or the path.\\ndocument.cookie = \\"username=JohnDoe; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 2024 23:59:59 GMT; path=/\\";\\n\\n// After the setting, document.cookie will contain the new cookie too.\\nconsole.log(document.cookie);"]]`);export{e as default};